P.transport["npc:agathelos-the-raging"] = {
  hash = "45101d",
  fullHash = "6be05c",
  text = "Agathelos the Raging. Deep within Razorfen Kraul, Agathelos the Raging is penned among the thorned tunnels. His great size matters to the quilboar, who revere Agamaggan as their god and progenitor, and rage makes the armored boar more fearsome still. His strength and mass make him one of the most dangerous beasts beneath the southern Barrens. Agamaggan, a titanic boar, is the god and progenitor of the quilboar. Among the Razorfen tribes, a beast as large as Agathelos carries deep spiritual meaning. He stands roughly twice as tall as a Barrens thunder lizard. The quilboar keep Agathelos from roaming freely through the Kraul. Several guards maintain a field in front of the short, dead-end tunnel where he is penned. Intruders who break through the barrier face a raging beast, made even bigger and more terrifying by his fury.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_agathelos-the-raging-a21cc2701fe529677593/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.6661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:aggem-thorncurse"] = {
  hash = "4f4b52",
  fullHash = "4f4b52",
  text = "Aggem Thorncurse. In Razorfen Kraul, Aggem Thorncurse bears the title Death's Head Prophet. He calls a boar spirit to his aid when threatened. Aggem guards part of Razorfen Kraul, where the Death's Head tribe dwells. His shamanic power lets him summon a boar spirit, reflecting the quilboar's reverence for Agamaggan.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_aggem-thorncurse-bbb9194590d4ae3a68ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 17.4759184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:aku-mai"] = {
  hash = "67d697",
  fullHash = "67d697",
  text = "Aku'mai. At Blackfathom Deeps, Aku'mai haunts the sunken ruins of Elune's temple along the Zoram Strand. An Old God's corruption tainted the temple's sacred Moon Well, and Aku'mai rose from the waters as its servant. The Twilight's Hammer now sacrifices innocents to Aku'mai for power. Blackfathom Deeps was once a great temple of Elune, until an Old God's corruption tainted its sacred Moon Well. Aku'mai, a gargantuan hydra and servant of the Old God, rose from the waters. Now Aku'mai lairs in the submerged ruins of the night elves' fallen temple, a blight upon the Zoram Strand whose malice poisons the waters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_aku-mai-f81d715a4a76d392ba26/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.7281633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:arcanist-doan"] = {
  hash = "1f7e63",
  fullHash = "1f7e63",
  text = "Arcanist Doan. Arcanist Doan is a master human mage residing within the Athenaeum of the Scarlet Monastery's Library wing. He serves the Scarlet Crusade as one of its most lethal arcane specialists and guardian of key crusade archives. While the fanatical ranks of the Scarlet Crusade lean heavily on martial fury and holy fervor, Doan remained convinced that raw arcane power was essential to purging the Scourge from Lordaeron. Stationed deep inside the Athenaeum of the Monastery's library, he was entrusted as both a lorekeeper and an operational gatekeeper, safeguarding the Scarlet Key that secures crusade sanctuaries across the northern kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_arcanist-doan-f5b59c26b4c2084c5893/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.8661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:baron-aquanis"] = {
  hash = "0b0e8c",
  fullHash = "0b0e8c",
  text = "Baron Aquanis. Blackfathom Deeps is where the Twilight's Hammer has made its lair, and Baron Aquanis rises from its flooded Moonshrine Ruins. He appears when someone disturbs the Fathom Stone, while the Earthen Ring suspects the cult has corrupted him. The Fathom Stone lies beneath the flooded halls of the Moonshrine Ruins, right under the noses of the Twilight's Hammer cultists who occupy them. Within the stone rests a fathom core, a device that reveals the history of nearby elemental activity when properly read. Je'neu Sancrea of the Earthen Ring sends Horde adventurers to recover it. Disturbing the stone draws Baron Aquanis up from the depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_baron-aquanis-85467b601b41cf1852df/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.884898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:baron-silverlaine"] = {
  hash = "8c96b4",
  fullHash = "ed1009",
  text = "Baron Silverlaine. Baron Silverlaine's spirit lingers in the Great Hall above Pyrewood Village. He once ruled Silverlaine Keep, until Archmage Arugal unleashed worgen upon the region and they slaughtered him. Baron Silverlaine ruled a noble estate in the southern hills of Silverpine. His fortress guarded the surrounding lands and kept them safe. That peace ended when Archmage Arugal summoned worgen to fight the rampaging Scourge and unleashed them upon the region. The feral beasts could not be controlled. They stormed the stronghold, overwhelmed its guards, and butchered the baron. Baron Silverlaine remains bound to the keep's ancient Great Hall. His restless ghost wanders among the wrecked dining tables where he once feasted. The cursed lord attacks intruders who enter his former home.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_baron-silverlaine-1e5b0870178d93beb339/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.5461224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bazil-thredd"] = {
  hash = "2861ed",
  fullHash = "047ac4",
  text = "Bazil Thredd. In the Stormwind Stockade, Bazil Thredd leads the Defias inmates in revolt from the eastern cell block. Edwin VanCleef's lieutenant has been imprisoned there since the Stonemasons' riots. Bazil Thredd stood beside Edwin VanCleef when the Stonemasons Guild rose against Stormwind's nobility. The guild had been refused payment for rebuilding the city, and VanCleef rejected the government's appeasements and sparked open revolt. Thredd served as his chief lieutenant and most trusted assistant. The city guard captured Thredd during the violent clashes that followed. VanCleef and the surviving masons fled into Westfall and formed the Defias Brotherhood, while Thredd was sent into the Stormwind Stockade. A prison riot has overrun the guards, opened the cells, and turned the Stockade into an armed fortress beneath the Mage Quarter. Thredd commands the imprisoned Defias bandits, insurgents, and allied cutthroats from a post in the eastern wing, guarded by an ogre named Hamhock. For years, he had stayed silent behind bars, seemingly forsaken. Now Warden Thelwater wants Thredd's head, hoping to break the rebellion's leadership before the chaos reaches the city streets.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bazil-thredd-d27bf5f5b217f3888eed/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.6383673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bazzalan"] = {
  hash = "4ba4c4",
  fullHash = "b34067",
  text = "Bazzalan. Bazzalan commands the Searing Blade cult from the lava caverns beneath Orgrimmar. Alongside Jergosh the Invoker, he directs its work in Ragefire Chasm. Beneath Orgrimmar, Bazzalan has joined the Searing Blade cult in Ragefire Chasm. While Thrall works to establish the Orcs' new home across Durotar, the satyr rogue commands the cult's field forces from winding lava caverns. He is swift and lethal with twin blades. From a volcanic ledge above the central chamber, Bazzalan oversees the cultist guards. Jergosh the Invoker conducts demonic rituals below. Like all satyrs, Bazzalan's ultimate loyalty belongs to demonic masters. He uses poison, quick strikes, and stealth against Horde scouts who venture near the cult's staging ground.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bazzalan-a5ba91bcb7cd0b30b665/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.6432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:blind-hunter"] = {
  hash = "c80801",
  fullHash = "11affb",
  text = "Blind Hunter. Deep within Razorfen Kraul, the Blind Hunter roosts in the sunless depths. This elusive albino bat prowls the quilboar redoubt, far from common bats near the cavern entrances. Razorfen Kraul winds through briars and dark caverns below ground. Common bats roost nearer the surface, where faint light reaches in from outside. The Blind Hunter keeps to the furthest, darkest alcoves of the tangle. Its pale hide and milky gaze may come from generations spent apart from natural sunlight. The Blind Hunter is thought to have lost most or all of its ordinary sight. Scholars and scouts believe it tracks prey through the gloom with keen hearing and echolocation. It rarely appears, hunting only now and then among the dense thickets. For cavern explorers, seeing this phantom beast is a rare encounter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_blind-hunter-a82836ab650bf70338ba/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.2032653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bloodmage-thalnos"] = {
  hash = "ac4634",
  fullHash = "10d1aa",
  text = "Bloodmage Thalnos. Bloodmage Thalnos is a skeletal spellcaster who haunts the Graveyard wing of the Scarlet Monastery. Who he was in life is not recorded, but the plague of undeath claimed him, and he now lingers deep within the Crusade's holy grounds, hungering for vengeance. No record tells who Thalnos was in life. Whatever his past, the plague of undeath claimed him. Rather than being cleansed or permanently destroyed, he rose as a withered skeleton, still clinging to his fiery magic and arcane mastery, and crying out for vengeance and for more souls. Confined to the deepest corner of the monastery's courtyard tombs, Thalnos commands apparitions and shambling undead minions against anyone who disturbs his domain. His continued presence poses a bitter contradiction for the Crusade: an undead monstrosity lingering right beneath their sacred halls, embodying the very horror they swore an eternity to eradicate.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bloodmage-thalnos-7d4d983af4060f6a240f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.1706122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bruegal-ironknuckle"] = {
  hash = "300765",
  fullHash = "a50049",
  text = "Bruegal Ironknuckle. Bruegal Ironknuckle prowls the riot-torn halls of the Stormwind Stockade. The dangerous dwarf is a powerful brawler, and anyone trying to restore order may meet his fists. Bruegal Ironknuckle roams the Stormwind Stockade during its open revolt. The dangerous dwarf is known for his brutal brawling strength. Many of the city's most volatile cutthroats have seized the underground cellblocks, and Bruegal is among them. He does not stay in one cell, but prowls the riot-torn halls, ready to bash anyone trying to quell the uprising. Bruegal Ironknuckle is one of many rogues and outlaws held deep beneath Stormwind. The Defias Brotherhood and its ringleaders direct much of the revolt, while inmates like Bruegal show that the prison holds criminals from across the eastern realms. Whatever crimes brought him there, his fierce resilience makes him elusive and one of the deadliest lone inmates in the corridors.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bruegal-ironknuckle-ee49f043116972754451/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.1216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:captain-greenskin"] = {
  hash = "2cef2b",
  fullHash = "45a8e3",
  text = "Captain Greenskin. In the Deadmines cove, Captain Greenskin readies a warship for Edwin VanCleef. The goblin pirate oversees its crew and construction for the Defias Brotherhood, preparing the ironclad juggernaut to sail against Stormwind. Captain Greenskin paces the main deck of the juggernaut under construction in the Deadmines cove. A seasoned goblin mariner, he was brought into Edwin VanCleef's inner circle to turn an underground cavern into a drydock for a warship. Captain Greenskin commands cutthroats, not stonemasons or bandits. His crew includes Mr. Smite, his fearsome tauren first mate, and Cookie, the murloc ship's cook. Together, they hold the gangplanks and upper deck, guarding the way to VanCleef's private cabin atop the bridge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_captain-greenskin-ed8857470960c3e0b7bc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.1616327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:charlga-razorflank"] = {
  hash = "985bdf",
  fullHash = "985bdf",
  text = "Charlga Razorflank. In Razorfen Kraul, Charlga Razorflank leads the Razorfen quilboar. They drove the tauren from the southern Barrens, claiming to fight in the name of their fallen god, Agamaggan. Charlga Razorflank rules from the tangled brambles of Razorfen Kraul. Her people revere her as a master geomancer, and no quilboar can match her experimental magic. She united her fractious kin into a lethal force. With primal earth and giant thorns growing from the remains of the demigod Agamaggan, Charlga reshaped the Kraul into a fortress that is hard to enter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_charlga-razorflank-b38f3227f3a258cb5dc4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 32.4179592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:commander-springvale"] = {
  hash = "e44ff4",
  fullHash = "e5f5f3",
  text = "Commander Springvale. At Shadowfang Keep, Commander Springvale's spirit guards the halls where he fell. Once a devoted paladin and commander, he was slain with his men by Arugal's worgen and remains to haunt the castle. Commander Springvale led the garrison at Shadowfang Keep in Silverpine Forest. Before the keep's ruin, he was an honorable paladin devoted to the Holy Light. After Lordaeron fell to the Scourge, he oversaw the fortress's security through those troubled days. Archmage Arugal summoned extraplanar worgen to strengthen Dalaran's failing ranks against the undead Scourge. The wild beasts soon escaped his control and descended on Shadowfang Keep. They slaughtered Baron Silverlaine and his entire guard. Springvale died in the halls he had sworn to protect, his service cut short. Death did not release Springvale. His ghost haunts the keep's council room with its spectral soldiers. He still wields paladin powers, including Holy Light and Hammer of Justice, against intruders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_commander-springvale-aa1e1572f28db36030ad/1-0000.mp3", duration = 56.7118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:cookie"] = {
  hash = "59d851",
  fullHash = "050087",
  text = "Cookie. Cookie tends the crew's cauldrons aboard the juggernaut deep in the Deadmines. A murloc among the Defias Brotherhood, he cooks beneath the watch of Captain Greenskin and Edwin VanCleef. In the underground dry dock, he is the only entirely optional boss. Cookie cooks beneath the decks of the towering juggernaut in Ironclad Cove. No human outrider or goblin engineer takes the duty; the solitary murloc does. How Cookie came to hold the position is unknown. The rest of the crew may doubt a murloc's cooking, but pirates rarely choose their meals with care. In Moonbrook's underworld, a meal is a meal. Cookie patrols the waterways and shores around the pirate flagship. He wields cooking utensils and throws acidic brews. Though apart from typical murloc tribes, he defends his cauldron with savage force, flinging caustic mixtures at intruders who enter the docks. His station lies away from the main gangplanks, so many who storm the cove pass his kitchen by. Cookie is the only overseer aboard the Defias juggernaut who can be bypassed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_cookie-e5c2e77e531619146ebe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.0669388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:dark-iron-ambassador"] = {
  hash = "b3e761",
  fullHash = "b3e761",
  text = "Dark Iron Ambassador. Deep within Gnomeregan, the Dark Iron Ambassador draws notice as an envoy of his clan. His presence leaves the clan’s interest in the ruined gnome capital unexplained. Among Gnomeregan’s irradiated halls, the Dark Iron Ambassador represents his clan. No one explains the purpose of his mission.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_dark-iron-ambassador-edffb692dc25e496c962/1-0000.mp3", duration = 17.8677551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:dextren-ward"] = {
  hash = "7bb011",
  fullHash = "f3525b",
  text = "Dextren Ward. In the Stormwind Stockade, Dextren Ward remains a prisoner amid a bloody uprising. Before his capture, he dug up bodies at Raven Hill Cemetery in Duskwood and sold them to the necromancer Morbent Fel. Darkshire's town council has marked him for death. Before chains bound him beneath Stormwind, Dextren Ward robbed graves in Duskwood for profit. At Raven Hill Cemetery, where sorrow and horror had grown, he dug up the newly buried dead. He sold the bodies to local practitioners of dark arts. Morbent Fel, a necromancer whose rituals plagued Duskwood and swelled the undead ranks, was Ward's main customer. By supplying him with bodies, Ward helped spread terror through Darkshire. Stormwind authorities eventually captured Ward and brought him to the Stockade. His crimes left the people of Duskwood demanding justice, and Councilman Millstipe called for Ward's hand as proof. A rebellion swept through the Stockade, and Ward was freed from his cell amid the chaos. Armed and vicious, he seized control of his corner of the prison and uses fear to keep intruders away. Alliance forces seeking to restore order count him among their priority targets.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_dextren-ward-449e97d18afbf2d7a3ac/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.8253061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:edwin-vancleef"] = {
  hash = "480462",
  fullHash = "25c9ca",
  text = "Edwin VanCleef. In the Deadmines, brilliant Edwin VanCleef commands the Defias Brotherhood. He once led the Stonemasons who rebuilt Stormwind City after the Second War. The House of Nobles denied the workers their promised pay, and VanCleef now seeks vengeance against the kingdom he helped restore. Edwin VanCleef led the Stonemasons Guild as it rebuilt Stormwind City after the First War. A peerless craftsman and idealist, he mastered architecture and directed the building of the capital's grand ramparts and towering cathedrals. His childhood friend Mathias Shaw trained him in stealth, subterfuge, and bladecraft, making VanCleef a deadly operative as well as a master architect. After years of labor, the Stonemasons finished rebuilding Stormwind. The House of Nobles had run up colossal debts through military campaigns and refused the guild its promised compensation. VanCleef demanded restitution, but the nobles dissolved the guild. He led the workers in a riot and out of the city. In the chaos, his lieutenant and most trusted assistant, Bazil Thredd, was captured and left in the Stockade. In Westfall, VanCleef gathered the exiled workers and allied outcasts under the Defias Brotherhood's banner.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_edwin-vancleef-ff8da600ae5d36c5cad2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.6685714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:electrocutioner-6000"] = {
  hash = "c648ee",
  fullHash = "75e1b0",
  text = "Electrocutioner 6000. In Gnomeregan's Launch Bay, Electrocutioner 6000 guards the upper platform. The spider tank fights for Mekgineer Thermaplugg and the irradiated leper gnomes who follow him. Mekgineer Thermaplugg and the irradiated leper gnomes who follow him took control of Gnomeregan's war machines after the city fell. Electrocutioner 6000 guards the upper platform of the Launch Bay, striking intruders with high-voltage current. Electrocutioner 6000 guards the Workshop Key, which opens Gnomeregan's back door into its workshops. Bring down the machine, and the key offers a quicker route into the city's depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_electrocutioner-6000-5b86577512fcd698c0ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.604898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:fenrus-the-devourer"] = {
  hash = "20a690",
  fullHash = "815931",
  text = "Fenrus the Devourer. In Shadowfang Keep, Fenrus the Devourer prowls the upper sanctum. Archmage Arugal keeps the enormous worg as a pet and guardian, its body swollen by unnatural magic. Intruders seeking the archmage's study must face the beast and its master. Fenrus the Devourer lurks outside Archmage Arugal's personal chambers deep within Shadowfang Keep. Unlike the worgen on the ramparts, Fenrus is a worg, swollen to enormous size. The beast prowls among the shelves, scrolls, and arcane apparatus in Arugal's private study. The archmage keeps Fenrus as both pet and guard against anyone who comes seeking his head. Fenrus's unnatural bulk points to dark magic saturating the keep since Baron Silverlaine's downfall. Archmage Arugal or his unleashed worgen warped the creature through arcane or shadowy experiments, turning a native predator into a ravenous juggernaut. Fenrus is so feral and bound by magic that no hunter can charm or tame him.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_fenrus-the-devourer-0ece46afebfdf26f723c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.9918367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gelihast"] = {
  hash = "b12b85",
  fullHash = "3c040f",
  text = "Gelihast. In Blackfathom Deeps, Gelihast guards a strange altar in the flooded Pool of Ask'ar. His Blindlight murlocs gather around him in the sunken temple. Deep beneath the Zoram Strand, Gelihast has claimed a lair in Blackfathom Deeps. Most murlocs keep to coastal shallows, but he strikes at intruders with twin swords, moving too quickly for many to react. Casters who come within his reach rarely survive. Gelihast rules the Pool of Ask'ar with his Blindlight brood, who swarm to his side at the first sign of trouble. An altar stands beyond his lair. Those who defeat him and touch it receive the Blessing of Blackfathom.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gelihast-ac2e20323b76a0c3bbd2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.9746939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:ghamoo-ra-classic"] = {
  hash = "502e9a",
  fullHash = "567dd6",
  text = "Ghamoo-ra. In Blackfathom Deeps, Ghamoo-ra guards a small island in the Pool of Ask'ar. The ancient giant turtle tramples anyone who comes near. Blackfathom Deeps was once a night elven temple to Elune. Flooded now, the ruins are claimed by naga, satyr, and the nihilistic Twilight's Hammer cult. Ghamoo-ra dwells among them, his enormous, thick shell turning aside all but the most determined blows. Lesser turtles surround Ghamoo-ra on a small island in the Pool of Ask'ar, and they leave travelers be. He alone attacks, crushing anyone who comes near beneath his trampling bulk.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_ghamoo-ra-classic-c3c4a30dd20b718d7f09/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.4930612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gilnid"] = {
  hash = "067e84",
  fullHash = "067e84",
  text = "Gilnid. Deep in the Deadmines, Gilnid runs the goblin forges for the Defias Brotherhood. Known as the Smelter, he oversees the work that turns raw ore into refined metal and munitions for Edwin VanCleef's hidden ironclad juggernaut. His foundry makes the heavy cannons the Brotherhood needs to strike Stormwind. Deep in the Deadmines, the Defias Brotherhood depends on skilled hands to forge its weapons of war. Gilnid, VanCleef's head smelter, commands goblin laborers, tinkers, and smiths in the central foundry. Taller than most of his kin, he drives them through smoke and slag, barking orders and allowing no breaks. Their work turns raw iron ore into heavy plating and deadly ordnance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gilnid-165e035a4275576caa70/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.1314286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:hamhock"] = {
  hash = "3cd297",
  fullHash = "2bb83a",
  text = "Hamhock. In the Stormwind Stockade, Hamhock remains locked among the rioting prisoners. The massive two-headed ogre is one of the prison’s most brutal inmates. Hamhock is a towering two-headed ogre in the Stormwind Stockade. The prison was built for petty thieves, murderers, and political dissidents, so his size makes him an imposing presence among the cells. When the prisoners rose up and took control, Hamhock stayed amid the disorder, crushing anyone who entered his cellblock. No record tells how Hamhock was captured or why he was locked among Stormwind’s thieves and dissidents. Whatever brought him to the Stockade, he is now one of its most dangerous inmates.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_hamhock-d7f4c63ff5d841e02801/1-0000.mp3", duration = 37.3812245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:herod"] = {
  hash = "645865",
  fullHash = "61aa1b",
  text = "Herod. Herod is a renowned champion and berserker of the Scarlet Crusade, commanding the Armory within the Scarlet Monastery. Known for his ferocious martial prowess and unyielding zeal, he trains recruits in the brutal melee combat needed to wage holy war. His death is sought by both Alliance dissidents and the Horde's rulers in Undercity to weaken the Crusade's military core. Within the fortified halls of the Scarlet Monastery's Armory, Herod stands as the embodiment of the Crusade's martial aggression. Unlike the spellcasters and paladins who channel holy magic in the Cathedral, Herod relies on raw, bloodthirsty physical power. Clad in mail and wielding a massive two-handed axe, he personally trains cohorts of Scarlet Trainees, instilling a furious devotion to exterminating anyone not allied with their fanatical cause. Because Herod oversees the armaments and martial training of the Crusade's front-line warriors, taking him down is essential for anyone seeking to dismantle the order. Raleigh the Devout, an ex-Crusader disillusioned with the order's madness residing in Southshore, calls for Herod's demise to shatter their military leadership. Across Lordaeron, the dreadlord Varimathras in the Undercity similarly seeks his execution to clear out a dangerous human bastion bordering Forsaken territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_herod-977a72192f7a7531a6ac/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.6432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:high-inquisitor-fairbanks"] = {
  hash = "e3dde8",
  fullHash = "e3dde8",
  text = "High Inquisitor Fairbanks. High Inquisitor Fairbanks was once a revered priest of the Knights of the Silver Hand and the most trusted advisor to Highlord Alexandros Mograine. Betrayed, executed, and hidden away behind a secret door in the Scarlet Monastery Cathedral, his rotting, undead form stands as a grim monument to the Scarlet Crusade's dark descent. Before the fall of Lordaeron, Fairbanks was a respected holy man among the Knights of the Silver Hand and the most trusted advisor of Highlord Alexandros Mograine, the bearer of the legendary Ashbringer. He stood beside the Highlord when Mograine, High General Abbendis and Grand Inquisitor Isillien founded the Scarlet Crusade, at first a noble order sworn to destroy the Scourge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_high-inquisitor-fairbanks-86dc17bcae6af6b258e9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.7510204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:houndmaster-loksey"] = {
  hash = "6ab590",
  fullHash = "ebe181",
  text = "Houndmaster Loksey. Houndmaster Loksey is the officer charged with breeding and training the fearsome war dogs of the Scarlet Crusade. Stationed in the Scarlet Monastery's Library wing, he directs packs of hounds to hunt down undead, intruders, and perceived heretics. His merciless discipline has made the Monastery's beasts infamous throughout Tirisfal Glades. Within the heavily fortified walls of the Scarlet Monastery, Houndmaster Loksey commands the training of vicious tracking and attack dogs. The Scarlet Crusade relies on these trained hounds to sniff out plague-tainted corpses, uncover hidden infiltrators, and run down any survivors fleeing the zealots' wrath. Loksey holds absolute authority over the hounds guarding the Library wing, ensuring his charges are primed to tear intruders apart limb by limb. Loksey is not the only beast handler in his family serving the cause. His brother, Huntsman Leopold, serves as the Scarlet Crusade's envoy to the Argent Dawn at Light's Hope Chapel. Leopold holds a low opinion of his brother, privately asserting that Loksey can barely manage his own hounds, let alone represent the order in high-stakes diplomacy. Despite Leopold's scorn, Loksey remains a key officer within the Monastery's inner hierarchy.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_houndmaster-loksey-adba189a4d2c6cc50e20/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.9485714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:interrogator-vishas"] = {
  hash = "32eb7e",
  fullHash = "32eb7e",
  text = "Interrogator Vishas. Interrogator James Vishas is the Scarlet Crusade's master of torment, stationed deep within the Scarlet Monastery's Graveyard wing. Infamous for his shrill voice and sheer cruelty, he takes twisted pleasure in breaking captives and testing the limits of mortal flesh. Few who enter his torture chambers ever leave with their minds or bodies intact. Within the dark crypts and burial grounds of the Scarlet Monastery, Interrogator Vishas tends to the Crusade's captive enemies. While the zealots outside march in gleaming armor under banners of purity, Vishas represents the grim reality behind their crusade: unbridled sadism cloaked in religious righteous fury. He approaches interrogation not merely as an intelligence duty, but as a prized craft, reveling in the agony of Forsaken captives, captured Scourge, and any living travelers unlucky enough to be branded heretics.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_interrogator-vishas-72f821e00941bb186657/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.8530612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:jergosh-the-invoker"] = {
  hash = "698714",
  fullHash = "698714",
  text = "Jergosh the Invoker. Deep beneath Orgrimmar, Jergosh the Invoker plots against the Horde. He leads Searing Blade cultists in Ragefire Chasm and practices demonic arts while Thrall guides the orcs toward honor and shamanism. Jergosh gathers the Searing Blade beneath Orgrimmar. While Thrall leads the orcs toward honor and shamanism, Jergosh turns to demonic power. He hides in Ragefire Chasm, where he draws followers into a sect aligned with the remnants of the Shadow Council. From below the Horde capital, the cult works against the path the new Horde has chosen.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_jergosh-the-invoker-b263d190ea58f54c90df/1-0000.mp3", duration = 32.9665306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:kam-deepfury"] = {
  hash = "c934aa",
  fullHash = "e2161a",
  text = "Kam Deepfury. In Stormwind's Stockade, Kam Deepfury waits apart from the Defias rebels. The Dark Iron dwarf was captured after arranging the blast that shattered the Thandol Span, far to the north. Kam Deepfury is a Dark Iron dwarf held deep in the Stormwind Stockade. Most prisoners there are disgruntled Stonemasons or Defias rebels, but he belongs to neither. Captured and brought to Stormwind after an operation crippled vital northern infrastructure, he remains cut off from his kin beneath Blackrock Mountain. Like the rebels who share the corridors, he is hostile to the Alliance. Alliance intelligence at Dun Modr, near the Wetlands' northern border, named Kam Deepfury as the chief architect of the blast that shattered the Thandol Span. The explosion severed one of the massive stone bridges linking the Wetlands and the Arathi Highlands. It crippled a vital trade and military route for Ironforge and the Alliance, and killed a kin of the dwarf commander Longbraid the Grim.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_kam-deepfury-836441851ddd26ccf570/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.5738776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lady-anacondra"] = {
  hash = "bce7b5",
  fullHash = "e5b9ce",
  text = "Lady Anacondra. Lady Anacondra is one of the Fanglords leading the Druids of the Fang within the Wailing Caverns. Once one of the night elf druids who followed Naralex in his ambitious effort to heal the Barrens, she was lost when her master's Emerald Dream communion turned into a nightmare. Her mind broke, twisting her devotion into a drive to remake the land to match that twisted dream. Before adopting her serpentine title, Lady Anacondra was one of Naralex's druids. When the elder druid Naralex conceived of tapping into the subterranean springs of the Barrens through the Emerald Dream, she followed him into the caverns. Her loyalty, however, proved her undoing. As Naralex's mind was seized by dark visions, the nightmare seeped out from the dreaming realm and contaminated his disciples, shattering her sanity and reshaping her reverie into a cold, reptilian obsession. Now styling herself Lady Anacondra, she stands guard among the first subterranean chambers of the Wailing Caverns. Like her fellow Fanglords, she abandoned the traditional tenets of the Cenarion Circle in favor of the vicious aspects of the viper. Channeling lightning, nature magic, and slumbering trances, she enforces Naralex's twisted slumber and works to spread the nightmare's corruption into the arid land above.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lady-anacondra-0b675cd08c2ec8aac3af/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.8930612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lady-sarevess"] = {
  hash = "44831b",
  fullHash = "24b812",
  text = "Lady Sarevess. Within Blackfathom Deeps, Lady Sarevess works dark sorceries in the flooded ruins. Lady Sarevess performs rituals in a flooded grotto within the Pool of Ask'ar. The Twilight's Hammer cultists haunt the sunken temple too, pursuing their own worship deeper in the cavern. Lady Sarevess commands elemental currents and crackling lightning. Loyal naga sentinels and summoned water elementals guard her sanctuary as she fights intruders who threaten her dark work.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lady-sarevess-766a281e0f317d30d392/1-0000.mp3", duration = 26.5142857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lord-cobrahn"] = {
  hash = "b6cfd1",
  fullHash = "0d0f70",
  text = "Lord Cobrahn. Lord Cobrahn is one of the four leaders of the corrupted Druids of the Fang lurking deep within the Wailing Caverns. Once a night elf druid of Naralex's noble order, which hoped to heal the Barrens, he succumbed to the nightmare infecting the cavern springs. Taking a lethal serpent guise, Cobrahn now commands broods of deviate serpents and defends the twisted labyrinth. Before his fall, Cobrahn was one of the noble druids who followed Naralex. When Naralex ventured into the Wailing Caverns beneath the Barrens hoping to tap their underground springs and restore lush life to the arid wastes, Cobrahn accompanied him. However, when Naralex's Emerald Dream communion decayed into a twisting nightmare, Cobrahn's mind shattered alongside his brothers. Rather than channeling the traditional totemic beasts of Cenarion tradition, the nightmare warped Cobrahn's shapeshifting into something predatory and reptilian. He now commands a deadly serpentine form that he unleashes in battle. Flanked by venomous deviate pythons and bearing a vicious clawed right hand, he oversees his section of the grottos, violently purging anyone seeking to break the nightmare's hold on the caverns.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lord-cobrahn-c2dd600be9fd66fe539d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.4334694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lord-pythas"] = {
  hash = "274a76",
  fullHash = "d632a4",
  text = "Lord Pythas. Lord Pythas, once a night elf warrior, is one of the four corrupted leaders of the Druids of the Fang in the Wailing Caverns. Originally one of the personal guards of the druid Naralex, he fell victim to the nightmare tainting the oasis underground. Now he wields both martial might and twisted serpent magic against any who try to reach his slumbering master. Before his corruption, Pythas was a seasoned night elf warrior who served among the druid Naralex's personal guard when the expedition ventured into the dry wastes of the Barrens. Alongside his martial training, he took up the druidic arts in service of Naralex's dream of restoring lush life to the arid realm. However, physical discipline and martial valor offered no shelter against the insidious corruption waiting deep within the subterranean fissures. When Naralex's connection to the Emerald Dream was hijacked by dark nightmares, Pythas's mind succumbed alongside those of his fellow acolytes. Abandoning his former loyalty, he became one of the leaders of the Druids of the Fang. Where his fellow serpent lords favor cunning spellcasting or beast forms, Pythas seamlessly blends lethal combat prowess with nature magic. He now prowls the winding tunnels of the caverns, determined to defend the nightmare's twisted grip on the cavern spring.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lord-pythas-7220a2889a487f14f208/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.9787755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lord-serpentis"] = {
  hash = "36c6db",
  fullHash = "d1d42a",
  text = "Lord Serpentis. Lord Serpentis is a corrupted night elf druid who stands as one of the four leaders of the rogue Druids of the Fang within the Wailing Caverns. Once a druid of great power and prestige in the order of Naralex, he was twisted into madness when Naralex's dream became a nightmare within the subterranean springs. Now, he helps command the serpent-worshipping sect deep in the caverns where the corrupted Naralex lies slumbering. Before darkness overtook the cavern springs, Serpentis was a druid of great power and prestige, one of the noble order led by Naralex that hoped to use the caverns' springs and the Emerald Dream to heal the Barrens. However, when Naralex's vision became a corrupting nightmare, Serpentis was among the druids whose minds fell to the emerging madness. Succumbing completely to the corruption radiating from Naralex's twisted dreams, Serpentis and his fellow druids became the Druids of the Fang. Forsaking traditional Cenarion balance and the totem animals of old, they now seek to remake the Barrens to match their own twisted dreams rather than heal it. Serpentis rules the sect as one of its four leaders, alongside Lady Anacondra, Lord Cobrahn and Lord Pythas.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lord-serpentis-7a72ea8a99b463c01b89/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.6832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lorgus-jett"] = {
  hash = "45efc0",
  fullHash = "45efc0",
  text = "Lorgus Jett. In Blackfathom Deeps, Lorgus Jett commands the Twilight's Hammer among flooded ruins. The Horde has marked him as a target. Lorgus Jett is a human among the Twilight's Hammer in Blackfathom Deeps. While many cultists there are orcs or tauren, Jett ranges from the upper Moonshrine Ruins to the tunnels beyond Gelihast, the murloc defiler. He coordinates the cult's rituals and violent ambitions in the ancient night elf ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lorgus-jett-5ff87ca2127bfd4eee25/1-0000.mp3", duration = 25.2342857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:miner-johnson"] = {
  hash = "809436",
  fullHash = "28fef4",
  text = "Miner Johnson. Deep in the Deadmines, Miner Johnson wanders among the Defias laborers. He is a seasoned miner who oversees excavation ahead of the heavy machinery, though he appears only now and then. Miner Johnson works among the laborers digging beneath the Deadmines for Edwin VanCleef's hidden fortress. He waits in an alcove to the left, before the shredders in the Mast Room. Stronger and more aggressive than the other miners, he can be hard to find as he wanders the tunnels. Beneath Moonbrook, the Defias Brotherhood needs ore for weapons, reinforced shafts, and mechanical contraptions. Johnson and the other miners dig the mineral seams beneath Westfall. Anyone who enters his work area will find that he guards it fiercely.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_miner-johnson-59f583307df3c6a0963d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.3779592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:mr-smite"] = {
  hash = "7b17d5",
  fullHash = "aeff90",
  text = "Mr. Smite. At Ironclad Cove, Mr. Smite keeps watch over the Defias ship and its crew. The tauren serves Captain Greenskin as first mate, guarding the docks where Edwin VanCleef's dreadnaught is being fitted out. Any intruder who meets him faces a changing array of weapons and formidable strength. Mr. Smite keeps order on the docks of Ironclad Cove as first mate to the goblin Captain Greenskin. The Defias Brotherhood is made up mostly of disaffected humans and goblin tinkerers, yet Smite is a battle-hardened tauren far from Kalimdor's plains. He has charge of a rowdy pirate crew while they fit out Edwin VanCleef's secret dreadnaught, and he allows no disruption to the work. Mr. Smite fights with more than brute force; he is a showman and a versatile weaponmaster. He toys with challengers, drawing out the fight and offering them a false hope of victory. When pressed, he stamps the ground with such force that foes are stunned, then searches a nearby chest for another weapon. He begins with a quick dueling blade, moves to twin cleaving reavers, and brings out a huge two-handed warhammer when pushed far enough.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_mr-smite-72bfe01536761a92b9ec/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.0636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:mutanus-the-devourer"] = {
  hash = "3d0edc",
  fullHash = "3d0edc",
  text = "Mutanus the Devourer. Mutanus the Devourer is a monstrous, pale murloc manifested directly from the Emerald Nightmare. Lurking in the darkest recesses of the sleeping druid Naralex's mind, it emerges to devour anyone attempting to break the Nightmare's hold over the Wailing Caverns. Slaying this twisted aberration is the final step in freeing Naralex from his tormented slumber. When the night elf druid Naralex tapped into the underground springs beneath the Barrens, he aimed to restore life to the arid wastes by connecting them to the Emerald Dream. Instead, an ancient corruption known as the Emerald Nightmare invaded his thoughts. Trapped in an agonizing vision, Naralex's subconscious fears took physical form. The corruption gave that nightmare the shape of an immense, albino murloc.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_mutanus-the-devourer-3bee7d646a4b85f2227c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.6767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:naralex"] = {
  hash = "526094",
  fullHash = "6dcdbf",
  text = "Naralex. Naralex is a night elf druid who sought to restore the parched Barrens to a lush forest using the powers of the Emerald Dream. Instead, his slumber was overtaken by a dark nightmare, twisting the Wailing Caverns beneath the earth and turning his followers into venomous fiends. Long before the Great Sundering, the sun-scorched plains of the Barrens were once a vibrant forest. Viewing this loss as a tragedy, Naralex journeyed to the Barrens with a cohort of devoted disciples to reverse the drought. Deep beneath the savannah, he discovered an intricate subterranean network of underground springs and rushing geysers, naming them the Wailing Caverns after the howling wind and steam that moaned through the rock. He believed that by entering a deep trance and channeling the pristine energies of the Emerald Dream into these subterranean waters, the life-giving flow would reach the surface and cause the entire wasteland to bloom. Naralex seated himself in deep meditation deep within the subterranean caverns, but his noble design collapsed the moment his consciousness touched the realm of the Dream. Rather than finding peaceful fonts of wild nature, his mind was ensnared by a malignant, encroaching corruption—an Emerald Nightmare. The festering corruption seized the sleeping druid's body, transforming him into an open conduit through which dark energies bled into the living world. The pristine springs turned foul, sprouting mutated fungal blooms and warping the native beasts of the caverns into ravenous deviate horrors.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_naralex-99ecb352d6c3730966ad/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.5583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:oggleflint"] = {
  hash = "6e56f3",
  fullHash = "bd424f",
  text = "Oggleflint. Beneath Orgrimmar, Oggleflint leads the trogg tribe in Ragefire Chasm. His pack climbs from lava-filled tunnels toward the surface, where he commands them near the cavern entrance and threatens the Horde capital above. Deep beneath Orgrimmar, Oggleflint and his troggs climbed from fiery tunnels into Ragefire Chasm. They carved a primitive domain among the molten rock and defend it with brutal force. Troggs have little advanced civilization, and Oggleflint rules his pack through sheer strength. Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem sent diplomats into the chasm to make peace with the troggs. The troggs turned on them and killed them. Maur Grimtotem, one of the envoys, lies dead in the caverns under Oggleflint’s watch. Near the cavern entrance, the chieftain waits and strikes at outsiders who venture into the dark. Oggleflint’s tribe lives directly beneath Thrall’s capital, a hazard the Horde cannot tolerate. The troggs creep closer to the surface, and their volatile nature threatens to carry them into the Drag and Cleft of Shadow if left undisturbed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_oggleflint-bbc2bc44f7bc0c73fc73/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.0220408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:old-serra-kis"] = {
  hash = "4722af",
  fullHash = "59fb05",
  text = "Old Serra'kis. Beneath the Moonshrine Sanctum, Old Serra'kis lurks in the flooded depths of Blackfathom Deeps. The enormous threshadon is hidden in a submerged cave, and deadly to anyone who seeks him there. The Twilight's Hammer has made its home in the sunken Moonshrine Ruins. Beneath the Moonshrine Sanctum, dark waters conceal older, wilder things. Old Serra'kis, the largest threshadon in the sunken temple, lurks in the Forgotten Pool, a flooded cave beneath the ruins where few would think to look. To reach Old Serra'kis, you must dive deep beneath the temple floor into a flooded cavern. No air pockets break the water, making the cave as deadly as the beast within. Cornered in the murk, Serra'kis shows an unnatural resilience. He drains life from his attackers with shadow energy, sustaining his own vitality in the darkness.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_old-serra-kis-cc2b087e899d085dbedf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.0244898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:overlord-ramtusk"] = {
  hash = "a42068",
  fullHash = "f0b157",
  text = "Overlord Ramtusk. Deep in Razorfen Kraul, Overlord Ramtusk leads the quilboar warriors into battle. He cries out for victory and for Agamaggan, and his fall breaks the Kraul defenders' martial strength. Overlord Ramtusk commands the quilboar stronghold deep in Razorfen Kraul, in the southern Barrens. He enters battle with a cry to Agamaggan, the boar demigod, and rules the Kraul's fighters with unyielding authority. Ramtusk directs the quilboar warriors through the Kraul's winding brambles. He keeps tight discipline among the bristled fighters who defend their tribe's sanctum. Elite marksmen flank him as he fights on the front line, bellowing rallying shouts and unleashing slams that can break opposing battle lines in seconds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_overlord-ramtusk-25b642ab5b5723456f91/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.4563265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:rhahk-zor"] = {
  hash = "93df5a",
  fullHash = "88cada",
  text = "Rhahk'Zor. Deep in the Deadmines, Rhahk'Zor drives the work forward by force. As head foreman, he keeps Defias laborers and miners in line while they build Edwin VanCleef's underground fortress. The Defias Brotherhood has turned Moonbrook's abandoned gold mines into an underground fortress. Rhahk'Zor was appointed head foreman to coordinate the work. He oversees the excavation crews and laborers as they carve tunnels and build VanCleef's secret projects. Rhahk'Zor keeps order with muscle and an immense stone-headed hammer. Defias diggers and goblin engineers know that defiance or falling behind schedule can bring a crushing blow from the towering foreman.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_rhahk-zor-c0884126df4d21a6fd2b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 37.3812245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:scarlet-commander-mograine"] = {
  hash = "53fc83",
  fullHash = "53fc83",
  text = "Scarlet Commander Mograine. Scarlet Commander Mograine serves as the military leader garrisoned inside the Cathedral of the Scarlet Monastery. A zealous paladin of the Scarlet Crusade, he defends the chapel alongside High Inquisitor Whitemane. Both the Horde and Alliance target him to disrupt the zealots threatening Tirisfal Glades and the surrounding lands. Stationed within the grand Crusader's Chapel at the heart of the Scarlet Monastery, Scarlet Commander Mograine commands the standing garrison of the Scarlet Crusade. He wields the holy power of a paladin with ferocious dedication, relying on retribution, divine shields, and stunning blows to shatter any whom the Crusade deems corrupted or unclean.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_scarlet-commander-mograine-2fbc62d60504c0376faf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.7844898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:sneed"] = {
  hash = "c4901b",
  fullHash = "2e5347",
  text = "Sneed. Deep in the Deadmines, Sneed directs the Defias Brotherhood’s timber work. From the Mast Room, he pilots a heavy shredder of his own design to cut lumber for Edwin VanCleef’s secret juggernaut. The Defias Brotherhood draws most of its members from human stonemasons and laborers, but an ironclad warship needs skilled trades. Edwin VanCleef brought Sneed in for his engineering knowledge and ruthless efficiency. Sneed works in the Mast Room, beside the underground rail network. There he directs the felling, cutting, and shaping of timber for VanCleef’s colossal dreadnaught. Sneed cares about cutting costs and meeting quotas, whatever the consequences. He directs the work from a custom shredder fitted with oversized buzz saws. Its blades turn raw trunks into shipbuilding timber in seconds. When intruders corner him, Sneed uses the machine’s armor to grind them down. If it fails, he scrambles from the wreckage and fights barehanded.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_sneed-eac81b33a91fde9e37b2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.7265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:sneed-s-shredder"] = {
  hash = "a850c3",
  fullHash = "3feb04",
  text = "Sneed's Shredder. Deep in the Deadmines, Sneed's Shredder cuts timber and guards the works. Goblin taskmaster Sneed pilots the machine for Edwin VanCleef's shipwrights, turning its blades against intruders. In the Deadmines foundry, goblin shredders cut timber for Edwin VanCleef's hidden juggernaut. They also meet intruders with armored force. Sneed oversees the lumber works and pilots this modified machine himself, wielding its oversized buzzsaw as a weapon of defense. Sneed rides above the spinning blades in an open cockpit, protected by heavy iron plating. The machine's clatter can scatter his foes. When the shredder breaks down and collapses in sparks, Sneed leaps from the wreckage and fights on.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_sneed-s-shredder-d9cc9bddf8d628e9b014/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.1763265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:taragaman-the-hungerer"] = {
  hash = "b8e8fe",
  fullHash = "b8e8fe",
  text = "Taragaman the Hungerer. Beneath Orgrimmar, Taragaman the Hungerer prowls the molten caverns of Ragefire Chasm. The Horde seeks to destroy the hulking felguard, feared as the demonic master behind the Searing Blade cult, and end the demonic taint beneath the city. Taragaman the Hungerer prowls among Ragefire Chasm's magma pools, threatening Orgrimmar below. Scouts and young warriors sent into the dark caverns see the demonic felguard as the Searing Blade cult's leader. With infernal strength, he bends the weak-willed and corrupts the passages beneath Thrall's fortress.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_taragaman-the-hungerer-45c300ffd185bdf3c130/1-0000.mp3", duration = 32.8881633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:targorr-the-dread"] = {
  hash = "042214",
  fullHash = "d197a5",
  text = "Targorr the Dread. Deep in the Stormwind Stockade, Targorr the Dread waits among the rioting convicts. His execution was delayed by corruption and neglect, and the Defias uprising gave him a chance to escape. Before his capture, Targorr the Dread served Warlord Gath'Ilzogg as the Blackrock clan's supreme executioner. He worked from the occupied ruins in Redridge, where his cruelty surpassed even the Dark Horde's violent ways. Stormwind guards and militiamen who defended the borderlands suffered torment and slow deaths at his hands. Across the kingdom, few names were more reviled. After Alliance forces captured Targorr alive, they brought him in chains to the capital and locked him in the high-security Stockade. His execution stalled in Stormwind's judicial and political bureaucracy. Graft, endless paperwork, and neglect kept the courts from delivering the death sentence he had earned. A bloodthirsty war criminal remained in a cell beneath the heart of the city. When the Defias Brotherhood broke prisoners out by force, Targorr seized his chance. He escaped his cell and joined the Defias rioters, turning his cellblock into a slaughterhouse. Warden Thelwater has lost control of the prison. Lakeshire still bears the wounds Targorr dealt its defenders, and seeks adventurers willing to enter the riots and bring back the butcher's head.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_targorr-the-dread-f19009a22362f0216a6d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.9118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:twilight-lord-kelris"] = {
  hash = "5235c2",
  fullHash = "05adfc",
  text = "Twilight Lord Kelris. Twilight Lord Kelris is an orc warlock and high-ranking leader within the Twilight's Hammer cult operating deep inside Blackfathom Deeps. From his perch in the Moonshrine Sanctum, he leads dark rituals in worship of the primordial hydra Aku'mai, aiming to usher in a reign of cosmic chaos. Twilight Lord Kelris directs the cultists of the Twilight's Hammer who seized the sunken ruins of Blackfathom Deeps. For years, Kelris slipped through the grasp of watchful eyes in Darnassus, evading the Argent Dawn and earning a grim reputation: whenever ancient malice stirred across Kalimdor, Kelris was suspected of orchestrating it. Rather than serving warlords or clans, this orc has pledged himself entirely to the Old Gods, seeking to unmake mortal civilization and return Azeroth to a state of absolute, primordial chaos. Within the Moonshrine Sanctum, Kelris oversees the cult's worship of Aku'mai, a vicious three-headed hydra and servant of an Old God. Allied with the naga, the Twilight's Hammer occupies the drowned temple, and Kelris presides over the cult's rites, sacrificing innocents to Aku'mai for power.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_twilight-lord-kelris-35bd9bb441af26481a63/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.3959184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:verdan-the-everliving"] = {
  hash = "d1368c",
  fullHash = "d1368c",
  text = "Verdan the Everliving. Verdan the Everliving is a colossal bog beast lurking deep within the verdant tunnels of the Wailing Caverns. He dwells amid the nightmare spreading from the sleeping druid Naralex, alongside the corrupted Druids of the Fang, and violently attacks any who venture into his damp hollows. Verdan is a hulking bog beast, an elemental of foliage, silt, and mire that lurks in the overgrown depths of the Wailing Caverns. Nothing is known of him from before the caverns fell to corruption; he is simply the largest and most dangerous creature in their lush, flooded hollows.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_verdan-the-everliving-231b242d2e0cbf8aacd6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 31.6604082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:viscous-fallout"] = {
  hash = "8e4d32",
  fullHash = "fd03b1",
  text = "Viscous Fallout. In Gnomeregan's lower passages, Viscous Fallout drifts through the toxic runoff. Radiation flooded Gnomeregan on Mekgineer Thermaplugg's treacherous advice, meant to wipe out the invading troggs. The fallout filled the city's lower levels with toxic slime and contamination. Viscous Fallout is a water elemental of those poisoned depths, its substance thick with the city's toxic waste. Its origins before the fall are unknown. Viscous Fallout drifts through the irradiated lower passages as a living pool of toxic sludge. It spews volleys of toxin and lashes out at anything in its path. Those who enter Gnomeregan to cleanse its tainted depths must destroy the creature.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_viscous-fallout-1da33c810c890e122d51/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.7697959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-narache"] = {
  hash = "24823f",
  fullHash = "9624ee",
  text = "Camp Narache. Young tauren begin their training at Camp Narache, high on Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. The camp is the Hawkwind tribe’s ancestral home, where elders preserve old ways as quilboar press close and centaur scouts linger. Camp Narache sits at the northern edge of Red Cloud Mesa, the main settlement of the Hawkwind tribe. Hide lodges and pavilions house the wounded, store weapons, bake bread, and offer a place for meditation and communion with ancestors. At the camp’s heart, elders and path trainers gather in a circle. Greatmother Hawkwind guides the newly initiated as they take up their roles as defenders of the Earth Mother. Steep cliffs shield Camp Narache, but threats reach the mesa all the same. Bristleback quilboar from Brambleblade Ravine press into Hawkwind lands, while centaur scouts remain in the distance. Young hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors prove their mettle by thinning aggressive wildlife, guarding the village well, and driving back quilboar raiders. Then they descend the grand path into Mulgore’s wider valleys.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-narache-3e42ab6856b96ddd9290/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.2236735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:coldridge-valley"] = {
  hash = "30046b",
  fullHash = "91d6d7",
  text = "Coldridge Valley. In Coldridge Valley, young dwarves and gnome refugees train for service to Ironforge. Snow blankets the sheltered vale in Dun Morogh's southwestern heights, and Coldridge Pass is its lone route to the wider mountain realm. Anvilmar is where new recruits learn to fight, move unseen, and wield sorcery. Veteran dwarven instructors train them alongside displaced gnome scholars. Beyond the hall, snow covers the valley floor, where boars and hungry winter wolves test the novices' skill at surviving the frontier. Sheer cliffs keep the vale apart from much of Dun Morogh's trade. Frostmane trolls camp in the valley, including at Frostmane Hovel, and skirmish with mountaineers over their ancestral claims against dwarven encroachment. Rockjaw troggs have overrun Coldridge Pass, the tunnel linking the valley to Kharanos. With the pass blocked, travelers and supplies cannot move freely between the valley and Ironforge, the mountain realm's high seat.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_coldridge-valley-c58cc8e1a3b321a46e6f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.1036735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:deathknell"] = {
  hash = "c4c1f2",
  fullHash = "dfa181",
  text = "Deathknell. Newly awakened Forsaken gather in Deathknell, a fortified village in a secluded valley of southwestern Tirisfal Glades. Mindless Scourge, Scarlet zealots, and dangerous beasts press the settlement as the undead claim a place in Sylvanas Windrunner’s kingdom. You enter Deathknell through a narrow gorge, sealed by elite Deathguards against intruders. Newly awakened undead first open their eyes here, north of the Silverpine border and behind jagged hills. Most Tirisfal settlements kept the human names they bore under the fallen Kingdom of Lordaeron, but the undead who claimed this town gave it a new name. The old church anchors the southern grounds, where the Forsaken hold on. North of it, the village forge and surrounding streets remain overrun by mindless Scourge zombies and feral ghouls. Local caretakers bring new arrivals into the Banshee Queen’s service. Deathknell faces danger from every side. Night Web’s Hollow, a gold mine northwest of the village, has fallen to monstrous spiders, cutting off a resource vital to the Forsaken’s fledgling economy. Southeast of town, the Scarlet Crusade keeps an encampment in the hills, watches the pass, and trades couriers with strongholds to the east while testing the valley’s defenses. Caretakers fight the mindless undead roaming the northern ruins and gather scrap metal, tools, and supplies for newly arisen souls setting out on the long road ahead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_deathknell-9bfce9870c4c24ab5ac3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.6326531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northshire-abbey"] = {
  hash = "8ed36b",
  fullHash = "ff69cb",
  text = "Northshire Abbey. Northshire Abbey trains new defenders in Northshire Valley, among Elwynn Forest's northern foothills. Rebuilt after the First War, it is the spiritual heart of the Brotherhood of Northshire and a leading school for those answering Stormwind's call. Clerics from Lordaeron came south centuries ago to spread the Holy Light and founded the Holy Order of Northshire Clerics and their abbey. The monastery soon became part of kingdom life, caring for the sick and tending to the young Guardian Medivh during his years in a coma. In the First War, brigands and the Orcish Horde attacked it repeatedly, and the abbey was destroyed. After the Alliance of Lordaeron liberated the region in the Second War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol helped rebuild the sanctuary and refounded its protectors as the Brotherhood of Northshire. Northshire Abbey trains volunteers, militia recruits, and novices who hope to defend Stormwind. Its Main Hall, Hall of Arms, and Library Wing house instructors in martial, arcane, and holy arts, while scouts and rogues train in the outer yards. The Knights of the Silver Hand keep watch, but peace remains fragile. Kobolds overrun nearby mines, aggressive wolves prowl the orchards, and Defias Brotherhood agents stalk the valley roads. They steal ancient scrolls and sacred librams from the abbey's archives.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northshire-abbey-a8cce7104bb0105266a9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.9755102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sen-jin-village"] = {
  hash = "940fe4",
  fullHash = "1b43ab",
  text = "Sen'jin Village. Sen'jin Village shelters the Darkspear on Durotar's southern shore. Vol'jin founded it after Zalazane drove his people from the Echo Isles, naming it for his fallen father. The villagers endure exile and prepare to reclaim their home. Sen'jin Village shelters the Darkspear survivors on Durotar's shore. After the witch doctor Zalazane betrayed the tribe and seized the Echo Isles, Chieftain Vol'jin led them across the strait to the mainland. They raised crude huts beneath coastal cliffs, facing the water and the home they had lost. The village bears the name of Sen'jin, Vol'jin's father, who was captured and sacrificed by murlocs during the Third War. Though the Darkspear are closely allied with Thrall's Horde, exile remains their burden. Each day, the lost isles lie just across the shallows. Life in Sen'jin Village turns on preparation and defense. Quilboar raids, aggressive sea beasts, and Zalazane's hexes from across the sea threaten the villagers. Herbal remedies and potent alchemical spirits help sustain them. Vol'jin coordinates the tribe's wider defense from the Horde capital, while local shamans, hunters, and scouts gather recruits arriving from the Valley of Trials. They seek resources, guard Durotar's southern flank, and plan Zalazane's downfall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sen-jin-village-2c593bec4a6c6282d442/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.4604082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadowglen"] = {
  hash = "3ce49c",
  fullHash = "24d4b9",
  text = "Shadowglen. Young night elves begin their training in Shadowglen, beneath the boughs of Teldrassil. The vale gathers around Aldrassil, where elders, priestesses, and mentors guide them before they travel farther into the world. Shadowglen lies beneath the northeastern boughs of Teldrassil, ringed by high ridges that shelter it from the rest of the world. Eternal dusk falls over the vale, where Aldrassil rises as a massive barrow-tree. Its hollowed trunk houses elders, priestesses, and mentors who guide young night elves beginning their training. A narrow mountain pass leads south toward Starbreeze Village and Dolanaar. This single route out leaves the sanctuary sheltered and peaceful. Danger reaches even this sheltered vale. Shadowthread Cave lies in the extreme northwest, where venomous spiders threaten the glade. Young defenders drive back the encroaching vermin and untamed wildlife before venturing farther among Teldrassil's boughs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadowglen-ce0a9d724af8f97e78d7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.04, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-trials"] = {
  hash = "f9cb37",
  fullHash = "f9cb37",
  text = "Valley of Trials. In the Valley of Trials, young Horde recruits test their strength in southern Durotar. Orcs and Darkspear trolls face the valley’s wildlife and dangers before they leave ready to defend their people. Young fighters face the Valley of Trials before they venture into Durotar. Chieftains and trainers watch over them from The Den, a shelter carved into the cliffs. In the sunlit gulch, recruits hunt scorpids, cull aggressive boars, and keep lazy peons in line as they harvest scarce timber. The trial is hard, and only those who prove their mettle earn the right to leave as soldiers of the Horde. Those who fail are left beneath the desert sun.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-trials-2e1927edb07a53bdd56b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 34.4032653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:alliance-of-lordaeron"] = {
  hash = "be36a6",
  fullHash = "be36a6",
  text = "Alliance of Lordaeron. The Alliance of Lordaeron was the historic grand coalition of the seven human kingdoms, Khaz Modan dwarves, Wildhammer gryphon riders, gnomes, and high elves formed to resist the Old Horde in the Second War. Led militarily by Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar and diplomatically by King Terenas Menethil II, it shattered the invading orcish forces and secured the Eastern Kingdoms. Though political fractures and the devastation of the Scourge tore it apart during the Third War, its legacy laid the foundation for the current Alliance. Following the horrific sack of Stormwind in the First War, Regent Lord Anduin Lothar brought the battered southern refugees across the Great Sea to Capital City. Pleading their cause before King Terenas Menethil II, Lothar convinced him to gather the rulers of the human nations in council. Old rivalries and border tensions ran deep, but the threat of the Horde outweighed them. Choosing Lothar as their Supreme Commander, the seven human nations bound themselves together in a desperate pact. Facing brutal orcish warriors, dark warlocks, and monstrous red dragons, the Alliance forged entirely new doctrines of war. Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Lothar established the Knights of the Silver Hand, elevating holy paladins like Uther the Lightbringer to counter the Horde's dark magic. When the war raged across the Hinterlands and quelled the siege of Aerie Peak, Kurdran Wildhammer pledged his fierce gryphon riders to rule the skies. Shortly thereafter, the Bronzebeard dwarves and Gnomeregan gnomes were liberated, joining high elven rangers under Alleria Windrunner. Despite the treacherous betrayal of King Perenolde of Alterac, the grand coalition drove the Horde back to Blackrock Spire and crushed their dominion. Victory brought bitterness rather than enduring peace. Bitter disputes erupted over the high taxes required to maintain the orc internment camps and rebuild the blasted kingdom of Stormwind. Seeing little personal gain, Gilneas built the Greymane Wall and severed ties, Stromgarde departed over territorial and prisoner quarrels, and Quel'Thalas pulled back behind its enchanted runestones. The coalition that remained under King Terenas attempted to weather the political storm, only to be utterly dismantled during the Third War when the Cult of the Damned and the Plague of Undeath swept across Lordaeron. Arthas Menethil's betrayal tore the heart out of the kingdom, ending the golden age of the northern realm.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_alliance-of-lordaeron-d472178dd0fec24d6351/1-0000.mp3", duration = 141.662041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:alterac-kingdom"] = {
  hash = "5ee004",
  fullHash = "5ee004",
  text = "Alterac (kingdom). Alterac was one of the seven historic human kingdoms, nestled high in the rugged Alterac Mountains. During the Second War, its ruler, King Aiden Perenolde, made a treacherous pact with the Old Horde to spare his realm, leading to the kingdom's military downfall and total collapse. Today, its ruined capital lies abandoned in the snow, while dispossessed nobles and outlaws wage a guerrilla war under the banner of the Syndicate. Alterac arose thousands of years ago as an Arathorian city-state following the Troll Wars. Settlers capitalized on strategic mountain passes and trading outposts, eventually establishing an independent realm when the empire fractured. Though rugged and mountainous, Alterac bordered the prosperous lands of Lordaeron and Stromgarde. Historically, it fielded tough mountain fighters, but enduring border friction with Thoras Trollbane's Stromgarde left the kingdom politically isolated from its neighbors long before the Dark Portal ever opened. When Sir Anduin Lothar forged the Alliance of Lordaeron, King Aiden Perenolde joined only with deep reluctance. As Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde swept north, Perenolde concluded humanity was doomed and secretly struck a deal with the Warchief. In exchange for the safety of Alterac, Perenolde opened fortified mountain passes, conspired against Alliance forces, and even dispatched soldiers and sailors to assist the Horde's siege of Lordaeron. The treachery allowed the orcs to reach Capital City far ahead of schedule, shaking the Alliance to its core when the truth was laid bare. Retribution was swift. Stromgarde's forces marched into the mountains, exposing Perenolde's treason and breaking Alterac's military. King Terenas Menethil II placed the fallen nation under martial law, while arguments over who should inherit the throne paralyzed Alliance diplomats for years. Following the kingdom's collapse, King Perenolde died in disgrace. His son, Lord Aliden Perenolde, rallied the bitter, displaced nobility to form the Syndicate. Wearing the orange cloth of their fallen homeland, these bandits and mercenaries now fight ruthlessly to reclaim their ancestral holdings across Hillsbrad and Alterac.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_alterac-kingdom-73fa7f9d6dabd0191d39/1-0000.mp3", duration = 127.059592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:anduin-lothar"] = {
  hash = "67a3d9",
  fullHash = "67a3d9",
  text = "Anduin Lothar. Sir Anduin Lothar, known as the Lion of Stormwind, was the last descendant of the ancient Arathi bloodline and the Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron. A childhood companion of King Llane Wrynn and the Guardian Medivh, Lothar led humanity through the horrors of the First War and rallied the northern nations during the Second War before falling at Blackrock Mountain. Carrying the ancient bloodline of the Arathi kings, Lothar grew up within Stormwind's royal court alongside Prince Llane Wrynn and Medivh. The trio embarked on reckless excursions into the wilderness, skirmishing with Gurubashi trolls in Stranglethorn Vale. Lothar rose rapidly through Stormwind's martial ranks, joining the King's Honor Guard and earning renown as an Armsman of the Brotherhood of the Horse. Arriving on the shores of Lordaeron, Lothar presented the grim reality of the Horde to King Terenas Menethil II. Invoking his ancestral Arathi lineage and diplomatic gravitas, he unified the human realms, Ironforge dwarves, and high elves into the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron. Named Supreme Commander of the united forces, Lothar spearheaded the campaign that pushed the Horde back south to the foot of Blackrock Mountain. There, in a fateful clash near the end of the Second War, Lothar was slain by Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. His death did not shatter the Alliance; instead, it galvanized his lieutenant, Turalyon, to lead the charge that broke the Horde forever.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_anduin-lothar-b71e69369df5af3b6cf6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.7004082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:argent-dawn"] = {
  hash = "aee6cf",
  fullHash = "aee6cf",
  text = "Argent Dawn. The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to stamping out malevolent threats across Azeroth, most notably the Scourge and the Burning Legion. Formed by survivors of the Knights of the Silver Hand who rejected the fanatical zealotry of the Scarlet Crusade, they welcome champions of any race or faction who honor the cause of the Light. From their fortified sanctuary at Light's Hope Chapel to far-flung outposts across Kalimdor, they stand on the front lines against darkness. When Highlord Alexandros Mograine fell and Lordaeron collapsed into ruin, the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand broke into bitter disagreement. Many survivors surrendered to grief and paranoia, morphing into the xenophobic Scarlet Crusade. Yet veterans like Lord Maxwell Tyrosus recognized that their former comrades had abandoned the true tenets of the Holy Light. Refusing to succumb to madness or abandon the dead kingdom, they split away to form the Argent Dawn—an order committed to defending Azeroth alongside anyone willing to fight, whether Alliance or Horde. The Dawn's primary stronghold is Light's Hope Chapel, situated in the blighted expanse of the Eastern Plaguelands. From this consecrated bastion, the order coordinates an unending war against Kel'Thuzad's necromancers and skeletal legions. Operating forward camps at Chillwind Camp and outside the Bulwark in Tirisfal Glades, Argent officers issue enchanted commissions to adventurers, paying bounties in exchange for Scourgestones wrested from fallen undead. Despite lacking the raw manpower of the Scarlet Crusade, the Dawn holds the line through perseverance, unity, and unshakable discipline. While Lordaeron remains their principal battleground, the Argent Dawn maintains an active watch across Kalimdor and the capitals of the world. They keep diplomatic presences in Stormwind City, Darnassus, and the goblin trade town of Everlook. Farther afield, operatives like Delgren the Purifier contest demonic cults in Darkshore and Ashenvale, while scouts in Desolace and the flooded ruins of Blackfathom Deeps investigate sinister threats involving the Shadow Council and the Twilight's Hammer cult.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_argent-dawn-3ac58db3ddb42d0aaffa/1-0000.mp3", duration = 124.839184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:arthas-menethil"] = {
  hash = "3dd0ea",
  fullHash = "3dd0ea",
  text = "Arthas Menethil. Once the golden prince of Lordaeron and a celebrated paladin of the Silver Hand, Arthas Menethil fell from grace during the Third War while pursuing the demonic orchestrators of the plague. After claiming the cursed runeblade Frostmourne in the frozen north, he surrendered his soul, murdered his father King Terenas, and spearheaded the destruction of his own homeland as the Scourge's dark champion. Born to King Terenas Menethil II, Arthas was raised to embody the ideals of the Alliance of Lordaeron. Trained in combat by Muradin Bronzebeard and inducted into the Order of the Silver Hand under the tutelage of Uther the Lightbringer, he was revered by high and low alike as the future of humanity. Bold, skilled, and fiercely protective of his people, he fought tenaciously against Blackrock clan orc incursions and forest troll warbands. Yet beneath his radiant exterior lay an uncompromising pride and an obsessive dread of failure.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_arthas-menethil-4503dc682b6c70e4130c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.9877551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:black-dragonflight"] = {
  hash = "e4da39",
  fullHash = "e4da39",
  text = "Black dragonflight. Once the stalwart Earth-Warders charged by the titans to safeguard Azeroth's deep subterranean realms, the black dragonflight fell to madness and treachery. Corrupted by the insidious whispers of the imprisoned Old Gods, their leader Neltharion became Deathwing, turning his brood into one of the most destructive and hated forces on the planet. Today, their surviving scions weave plots from the shadows and wage covert wars against mortal kingdoms. Long ago, the Titan Khaz'goroth bestowed his blessing upon Neltharion, creating the black dragonflight to watch over the earth, raise mountains, and guard Azeroth's deep caverns. For ages, they stood alongside the other flights as patient, unyielding guardians. Yet their intimate bond with the world's subterranean foundations proved their undoing. Dwelling within the deepest hollows, they lay dangerously close to the sunken prisons of the Old Gods. Over centuries, dark whispers eroded the noble Earth-Warder's mind, driving him to madness and twisting his entire brood into vicious instruments of ruin. During the ancient War of the Ancients, Neltharion shattered the unity of dragonkind, turning upon his kin as Deathwing the Destroyer. Ever since, the black flight has waged relentless, spiteful war against the red, blue, green, and bronze flights, who view their cruelty as an absolute profanation of life. Rather than tending the soil, black dragons now wield molten magma, black magic, and searing brimstone to scorch the lands they once nurtured, finding delight in sowing discord, misery, and endless strife across mortal realms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_black-dragonflight-d7eb372af9cb142716d0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.764898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:blackrock-clan"] = {
  hash = "24138e",
  fullHash = "24138e",
  text = "Blackrock clan. The Blackrock clan is a martial orcish clan renowned for its strict military discipline, unmatched blacksmithing, and central role in the First and Second Wars. Today, the clan is deeply fractured: while some veterans follow Thrall in the New Horde, a vicious and renegade splinter group known as the Dark Horde occupies the upper peaks of Blackrock Mountain under Rend Blackhand. Hailing from Draenor, the Blackrock clan made its name through rigid hierarchy, heavy armor, and mastery over the forge. Under Chieftain Blackhand, they served as the brutal core of the Old Horde during the invasion of Azeroth, laying siege to Stormwind. When Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand to cleanse the Horde of Gul'dan's direct puppet mastery, leadership of both the clan and the Horde remained firmly in Blackrock hands through the grueling campaigns of the Second War. Following the Horde's catastrophic defeat at Blackrock Mountain, the clan shattered. Many warriors were rounded up and placed in Alliance internment camps, later to be liberated by Thrall and inducted into his reformed New Horde. However, unrepentant elements rallied behind Blackhand's sons, Rend and Maim. This faction retreated into the heights of Blackrock Spire, forming the hostile Dark Horde alongside remnants of the Dragonmaw and other rogue clans. Today, Rend Blackhand claims the mantle of true Warchief from his throne in Blackrock Spire. The Dark Horde maintains an uneasy and treacherous truce with the Dark Iron dwarves who lurk in the molten depths below, all while waging war against both the Alliance and Thrall's Horde. Uncompromising, demon-tainted remnants of the clan have also been spotted lingering in Lordaeron, seeking to revive dark sacrificial rituals.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_blackrock-clan-a4c3358996f4264f987b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 102.29551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:bronzebeard-clan"] = {
  hash = "faf8c1",
  fullHash = "faf8c1",
  text = "Bronzebeard clan. The Bronzebeard clan is the ruling dwarven royal line and dominant population of Ironforge. Following their victory in the ancient War of the Three Hammers, they forged strong ties with humanity and the gnomes, standing today under King Magni Bronzebeard as a cornerstone of the Alliance. Long ago, dwarven society in Ironforge flourished under High King Modimus Anvilmar. When the king died of old age roughly 230 years ago, simmering rivalries erupted into civil war. Thane Madoran Bronzebeard commanded the traditional garrison and largest standing army within Ironforge, successfully driving both the Wildhammer and Dark Iron clans from under the mountain. When Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan mounted an assault to reconquer Khaz Modan, Madoran Bronzebeard led his clan in a decisive campaign that shattered the Dark Iron host, solidifying Bronzebeard supremacy over Ironforge. Human kingdoms first encountered the Bronzebeards nearly two millennia ago, building mutual respect through shared mastery of metalcraft, trade, and a passion for battle. When the Orcish Horde swept through the continent in the Second War, King Magni Bronzebeard brought his realm into the Alliance of Lordaeron. Despite withstanding brutal sieges within their subterranean fortress, Bronzebeard engineers, gunners, and demolitionists provided pivotal firepower and arms that helped turn the tide of the war.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_bronzebeard-clan-bf7f5a9155a3417c26c9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.6253061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:burning-blade-clan"] = {
  hash = "4f0c45",
  fullHash = "4f0c45",
  text = "Burning Blade clan. Once an ancient orcish clan renowned for its deadly blademasters and consumed by chaotic frenzy during the early wars, the Burning Blade now largely persists as a sinister demon-worshiping cult. Its acolytes infiltrate Durotar and the Barrens under the direction of the Shadow Council. Meanwhile, a handful of honorable blademasters have forsaken its demonic corruption to serve Warchief Thrall in the New Horde. Originating in the lands of Draenor alongside clans like the Warsong, the Burning Blade were long respected for the lethal tradition of their blademasters. When they drank the blood of Mannoroth and marched through the Dark Portal into Azeroth during the First War, however, the clan fractured mentally. By the Second War, they were scarcely an organized society, behaving instead like a berserk force of nature. Nomadic and demented, they threw themselves into battle with total disregard for survival, requiring ogres to hold them in check until unleashed. Ultimately, their numbers were battered and routed near the Dark Portal by the Alliance. Following the Second War, the surviving remnants of the Burning Blade split dramatically. A disciplined contingent of blademasters swore solemn oaths to liberate their people from demonic enslavement, pledging their blades directly to Warchief Thrall as his personal honor guard. Yet a far larger portion of the scattered clan fell entirely under the sway of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, morphing into a clandestine cult of warlocks, fel acolytes, and conspirators lurking throughout Kalimdor. The modern cult operates covens across Kalimdor, troubling newly arrived orcs and Darkspear trolls from their first days in the Valley of Trials. Burning Blade warlocks inhabit Thunder Ridge, and garrison a major hideout within Skull Rock near Orgrimmar's eastern gates. Their cultists rally around dark relics like the Demon Seed and harvest souls for demonic rituals, making them a primary internal menace against the fledgling Horde's stability in Durotar and the Barrens.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_burning-blade-clan-6f5b927f5b0d3a2d6550/1-0000.mp3", duration = 117.289796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:burning-legion"] = {
  hash = "a7353f",
  fullHash = "a7353f",
  text = "Burning Legion. The Burning Legion is an infinite, apocalyptic host of demons led by the fallen titan Sargeras, dedicated to scouring all life from creation. Having already shattered ancient Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, the demons remain Azeroth's most relentless nemesis, orchestrating the corruption of the orcs and the horrors of the Third War. Long ago, Sargeras stood as the champion of the titans, tasked with hunting down and imprisoning the demons that plagued the cosmos. Over countless ages of battling their evil, he grew disillusioned and concluded that creation itself was fatally flawed and must be unmade in flame. To enact his crusade, he freed the demons he had imprisoned, bending them to his will through fel magic. He augmented their chaos with the intellect of the corrupted eredar—elevating Archimonde and Kil'jaeden as his chief lieutenants—and forged an unstoppable engine of cosmic annihilation. Ten millennia ago, the reckless sorceries of the Highborne drew the Legion's gaze to Azeroth. Whispering through the Well of Eternity, Sargeras seduced Queen Azshara and her councilor Xavius with promises of reshaping the world into paradise. Instead, the portal unleashed commanders like Mannoroth and Archimonde alongside swarms of doomguard, felhounds, and flaming infernals. The ensuing war reduced Kalimdor's civilization to ruin before an alliance of night elves, dragonflights, and demigods collapsed the gateway. The catastrophic implosion tore the supercontinent apart in the Great Sundering, casting the ruined lands across vast new seas. Though cast back into the Twisting Nether, the Legion never abandoned Azeroth. Deciding that direct invasion invited failure, Kil'jaeden orchestrated proxy cataclysms from afar. The deceiver bound the orcs of Draenor through demonic blood pacts, sending the Horde charging through the Dark Portal to weaken mortal defenses. When that failed, the Legion forged the Lich King and unleashed the undead Scourge across Lordaeron, paving the way for Archimonde's return in the Third War. Even after Archimonde fell at Mount Hyjal, remnants of the Legion linger across Felwood, the Blasted Lands, and hidden cults throughout the world.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_burning-legion-b265a3dbf6df74a2e704/1-0000.mp3", duration = 129.645714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:cenarion-circle"] = {
  hash = "f49d12",
  fullHash = "f49d12",
  text = "Cenarion Circle. The Cenarion Circle is an ancient, independent order of druids founded by Malfurion Stormrage under the guidance of the demigod Cenarius. Operating from their sacred haven in Moonglade, they strive to maintain the balance of nature, safeguard the Emerald Dream, and mend Azeroth's blighted lands. Though historically composed of night elves, they have recently welcomed the tauren into their ranks, transcending factional divides to defend the living world. The roots of the Cenarion Circle go back thousands of years to the teachings of Cenarius, the Patron of Druids, who mentored the first mortal druid, Malfurion Stormrage. Following the Great Sundering, Malfurion bound together those who wished to study nature's ways. The order made its home in the sacred vale of Moonglade, and ever since, the Circle has held sacred vigil over Azeroth's wild places. While deeply intertwined with night elf history, the Cenarion Circle exists independently of Darnassus and the Sisterhood of Elune. Their heart remains the enchanted valley of Moonglade, centered around the peaceful town of Nighthaven, where weapons are sheathed and the wars of mortals hold no sway. This ancient neutrality was demonstrated anew following the Third War, when Malfurion befriended the tauren elder Hamuul Runetotem. Recognizing the tauren people's profound spiritual reverence for the Earth Mother, the Circle formally inducted Hamuul, reintroducing druidism to his race after thousands of years.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_cenarion-circle-c135f2f5a0918315b769/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.2938776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:cenarius"] = {
  hash = "c1403e",
  fullHash = "c1403e",
  text = "Cenarius. Cenarius, the Lord of the Forest, is the ancient demigod son of Elune and Malorne who taught the night elves the secrets of druidism. For thousands of years he served as the great patron of mortal druids and guardian of the wilderness before falling in battle during the Third War. Cenarius was born to Elune, the goddess of the moon, and the great white stag demigod Malorne. Because Cenarius was bound to the mortal world, Elune placed him in the care of Malorne, while Ysera the Dreamer embraced him as a foster mother and guided him through the infinite paths of the Emerald Dream. Blessed with powers over living wood and earth, Cenarius fathered the Keepers of the Grove and the dryads, forever tying his bloodline to the wild places of Kalimdor. Millennia ago, three youths—Malfurion Stormrage, his twin Illidan, and Tyrande Whisperwind—sought out the demigod of legend. Cenarius recognized Malfurion's exceptional heart and trained him as the first mortal druid. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne unleashed the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients, Cenarius marshaled Kalimdor's wild demigods to fight alongside mortal defenders. For ten millennia following the Great Sundering, Cenarius watched over Kalimdor and nurtured the Cenarion Circle alongside Malfurion. That vigilant watch ended during the Third War when the Warsong clan began harvesting timber in Ashenvale. Outraged by the destruction of sacred woodlands, Cenarius drove the invaders back until Grom Hellscream and his warriors partook of Mannoroth's demonic blood. Infused with demonic fury, the corrupted orcs overwhelmed the demigod and struck him down, casting a dark pall across nature's defenders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_cenarius-9af88fba77428f285721/1-0000.mp3", duration = 96.6791837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:church-of-the-holy-light"] = {
  hash = "c2cddd",
  fullHash = "c2cddd",
  text = "Church of the Holy Light. The Church of the Holy Light is the dominant religious institution of humanity, guiding mortal souls through the virtues of compassion, justice, and devotion. Originally centered in the lost northern kingdom of Lordaeron, its leadership now resides within the grand Cathedral of Light in Stormwind City following the devastation wrought by the Scourge. The Church's origins reach back over a millennium to the faith in the Holy Light that took root among the humans of Arathor, teaching them to channel sacred power to cure injuries and purge affliction. Driven by tenets of charity, righteousness, and the fundamental belief that the Light resides within all thinking beings, these teachings eventually coalesced into an organized faith. The kingdom of Lordaeron became the religious heartland of this doctrine, establishing revered sanctuaries throughout the north, including Stratholme, Andorhal, Tyr's Hand, and Light's Hope Chapel. In the First War, the Brotherhood of Northshire answered the call to battle, healing fallen defenders against the invading Orcish Horde. Because they lacked weapons or heavy armor, these frontline clerics suffered appalling casualties at the hands of the orcs. Recognizing the need for holy warriors who could both channel holy wrath and survive physical combat, Archbishop Alonsus Faol convened with Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar during the Second War to establish the Knights of the Silver Hand. Paladins and priests alike became the moral and martial bulwark of the Alliance of Lordaeron, preserving humanity from total annihilation. The Third War brought immense sorrow upon the faithful. When the Plague of Undeath crept across Lordaeron, the Church worked tirelessly to alleviate the sick and stem the contamination, only to be overwhelmed when the kingdom collapsed from within. With Lordaeron transformed into a Scourge-haunted wilderness, the seat of the Church transferred southward. Under the stewardship of Archbishop Benedictus, the clergy reconstituted its hierarchy within the newly constructed Cathedral of Light in Stormwind, offering solace, spiritual guidance, and military purpose to the battered survivors of the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_church-of-the-holy-light-c462728b0870ee0ee7b0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 123.010612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:cult-of-the-damned"] = {
  hash = "5375e3",
  fullHash = "5375e3",
  text = "Cult of the Damned. The Cult of the Damned is a secretive cabal of mortal zealots, necromancers, and aristocrats devoted to the Lich King. Formed in northern Lordaeron prior to the Third War, its members poisoned the grain supplies that spawned the Scourge and engineered the downfall of humanity's greatest kingdom. Today, the surviving cultists manage the plague cauldrons and necromantic academies across the Plaguelands while actively infiltrating the cities of the living. The Cult of the Damned was forged by the exiled Archmage Kel'Thuzad after he answered the telepathic whispers of the Lich King emanating from the Frozen Throne. Returning to Lordaeron with vast wealth and forbidden dark arts, Kel'Thuzad offered two false gospels: to the desperate, impoverished lower classes, he promised social equality and liberation from misery; to the wealthy landowners, such as the noble House of Barov, he promised eternal life and an unshakeable grip upon their domains. In truth, both the destitute and the affluent were cattle intended for slaughter, bound into a death cult designed to betray their homeland from the inside. Operating in complete secrecy from the dungeons beneath Caer Darrow, the cultists honed their necrotic craft, built hulking abominations, and perfected the Plague of Undeath brought south from Northrend. They struck at Lordaeron's breadbasket, contaminating the grain stores at Andorhal. As tainted flour traveled through regular merchant routes to hearths across the Eastweald, commoners sickened and died within days, only to rise as shambling thralls under the Lich King's telepathic lash. When Prince Arthas slaughtered Kel'Thuzad in Andorhal, the cultist network was already so deeply rooted that the kingdom's doom was sealed. Though Arthas sailed to Northrend and ascended the Frozen Throne, the Cult of the Damned remains fully operational in northern Lordaeron under the command of the resurrected archlich Kel'Thuzad. From the shadows of Stratholme and the halls of Scholomance, living cultists perform dark rites, maintain the toxic plague cauldrons across the ruined farmsteads, and attempt to assassinate Scarlet Crusaders and Forsaken alike. Because they still breathe, these acolytes can walk unnoticed through living settlements, poisoning wells, sabotaging supply lines, and grooming new recruits to feed the Scourge machine.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_cult-of-the-damned-d2261e543137aa9f4e4c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 133.433469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dark-iron-clan"] = {
  hash = "e7c805",
  fullHash = "e7c805",
  text = "Dark Iron clan. The Dark Iron clan is an ancient, ash-skinned dwarven faction ruling from the volcanic depths of Blackrock Mountain. Cast out of Ironforge during the War of the Three Hammers, they accidentally summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, who enslaved their empire. Today, under Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, they wage shadow wars against both the Alliance and the Horde while serving their fiery elemental master. Long ago, three dwarven factions lived together in Ironforge under High King Modimus Anvilmar. When the king died, civil war erupted. The Bronzebeards defeated both rival factions, casting out the Wildhammers and Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan's Dark Irons. Vowing vengeance, Thaurissan established a settlement in the Redridge Mountains while his sorceress wife, Modgud, led an army against Grim Batol. When both assaults failed and the united Bronzebeard and Wildhammer armies marched on his stronghold, Thaurissan attempted to unleash an apocalyptic subterranean entity to save himself. His ritual breached the core of the world and freed Ragnaros the Firelord. The rebirth of Ragnaros violently shattered the surrounding peaks, carving out the blazing caldera of Blackrock Mountain flanked by the Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes. The sorcerer-thane perished in the cataclysm, and the surviving Dark Irons found themselves bound into perpetual servitude to the Firelord. Over centuries within the lower chambers of the mountain, they constructed the sprawling subterranean capital of Shadowforge City. Their artisans and sorcerers became masters of stone golems, dark sorceries, and steam-driven siege machinery, weaponizing the molten elements around them under the gaze of flamewakers and elemental overlords.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dark-iron-clan-af79905fa6d372f73fb4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.0636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dark-iron-dwarf"] = {
  hash = "1ecf43",
  fullHash = "1ecf43",
  text = "Dark Iron dwarf. Dark Iron dwarves are a distinct dwarven sub-race distinguished by their ashen skin, blazing red eyes, and innate affinity for fire. Banished from Ironforge following the civil War of the Three Hammers, they dwell deep within Blackrock Mountain where their society has fallen under the tyrannical rule of the Firelord, Ragnaros. Unlike their Bronzebeard and Wildhammer cousins, Dark Iron dwarves possess dark gray or soot-black skin and eyes that burn like glowing embers. Their history fractured during the War of the Three Hammers, when their Sorcerer-thane Thaurissan sought to conquer Ironforge. Defeated and driven south into the Redridge Mountains, Thaurissan attempted to summon dark powers to avenge his losses, inadvertently releasing Ragnaros the Firelord from the Elemental Plane. The catastrophic summoning shattered the surrounding peaks into the volcanic waste of Blackrock Mountain and bound the Dark Iron race into fiery servitude. Life in the deep subterranean city of Shadowforge is brutal and unforgiving. While the Dark Irons were once merely ambitious and cunning, generations beneath Blackrock Mountain under the oppressive aura of Ragnaros have warped much of their culture into cruelty, paranoia, and tyranny. Dark Iron law knows only one sentence for infractions: trial by combat in the brutal Ring of the Law. Poisoning and political intrigue are so prevalent that Dark Iron travelers customarily pack their own rations rather than trust food from strangers. Not all Dark Iron dwarves serve Emperor Dagran Thaurissan and the cult of Ragnaros. Dissident factions have broken away to pursue their own ends. The most prominent among them is the Thorium Brotherhood, a guild of master smiths based out of Thorium Point in Searing Gorge who have defected from the main clan to hire outside adventurers against their former masters. Others operate as mercenaries, spies, or trade engineers alongside neutral organizations like the Venture Company.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dark-iron-dwarf-d6f66a6974410acaaa60/1-0000.mp3", duration = 113.737143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dark-riders"] = {
  hash = "5f592c",
  fullHash = "5f592c",
  text = "Dark Riders. The Dark Riders are a band of mysterious, hooded horsemen who terrorize Duskwood. Merciless hunters of magical relics, they strike from hiding and leave only slaughter in their wake. Little is known of the Dark Riders' origins. Witnesses describe hooded horsemen who speak in voices both harsh and shrill, and one was seen using a small gem to take orders from a master it called only its 'Lord'. They hunt objects of magical power across the kingdom, cutting down anyone who stands between them and their prize.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dark-riders-885efa138e9bfa11b95b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 31.2685714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:darkspear-tribe"] = {
  hash = "7bba8f",
  fullHash = "7bba8f",
  text = "Darkspear tribe. The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who joined Thrall's Horde after being driven from their ancestral home in Stranglethorn Vale and rescued from a sea witch in the Great Sea. Led by Shadow Hunter Vol'jin from their temporary refuge at Sen'jin Village, they now serve as core members of the Horde across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms. Despite their alliance, they currently struggle to reclaim their new home, the Echo Isles, from the traitorous witch doctor Zalazane. Originally a smaller, less ruthless tribe within the broken Gurubashi Empire of Stranglethorn Vale, the Darkspear endured centuries of warfare and famine against brutal neighbors like the Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter tribes. Driven out to sea, they settled on remote islands, only to face aquatic murlocs, Kul Tiran sailors, and a naga sea witch. Their elder chieftain, Sen'jin, was captured and slain in dark sacrificial rites beneath the sea, but before dying he foretold that the orc Warchief Thrall would lead the Darkspear to a new destiny. Rescued by Thrall's passing fleet, the survivors pledged their undying loyalty to the new Horde and sailed westward to Kalimdor. Unlike their cannibalistic jungle kin, the Darkspear have adapted rapidly to the Horde's values of honor, establishing enclaves like Malaka'jin in the Stonetalon Mountains, Shadowprey Village in Desolace, and Zoram'gar Outpost in Ashenvale. In Orgrimmar, they make their spiritual home within the Valley of Spirits, where shadow hunters and witch doctors commune with the loa. Though respected for their scouts, bat riders, and powerful hexes, old traditions run deep; some ancient rituals frowned upon by their allies are still practiced quietly in the dark.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_darkspear-tribe-0b8b899f76d28292b658/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.8473469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dragonmaw-clan"] = {
  hash = "c857ef",
  fullHash = "c857ef",
  text = "Dragonmaw clan. The Dragonmaw clan is an infamous orcish faction notorious for subduing and riding red dragons during the Second War. Guided by dark artifacts and foul magic, they imprisoned the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza beneath the cursed halls of Grim Batol until her liberation shattered their dominion. Today, their scattered remnants lurk in the wetlands and mountains of Khaz Modan, remaining hostile to both the Alliance and Thrall's New Horde. The Dragonmaw clan hailed from Draenor and crossed the Dark Portal with the Old Horde. On Azeroth, they turned their talents to subjugating its winged terrors. Guided by whispers from the black dragon Deathwing, the clan's chieftain, Zuluhed the Whacked, recovered an ancient golden disk: the Dragon Soul. Zuluhed entrusted this relic to his greatest warlock, Nekros Skullcrusher, who tapped into its fel taint and christened it the Demon Soul. Using the artifact's agonizing power, Nekros ensnared Alexstrasza the Life-Binder herself. The Dragonmaw fortified the abandoned dwarven bastion of Grim Batol, keeping the Dragonqueen chained in darkness and forcing her red dragon brood to serve the Old Horde as aerial dreadnoughts during the Second War. The Dragonmaw reign ended when the Demon Soul was finally destroyed, freeing Alexstrasza and her flight from their psychic torment. Alexstrasza devoured Nekros Skullcrusher, and her vengeful flight laid waste to Grim Batol, breaking the clan's back. Routed and terrified, the surviving Dragonmaw scattered into the marshy bogs of the Wetlands and the jagged peaks around Grim Batol. The remnants on Azeroth live as desperate, feral renegades, fiercely guarding their dwindling encampments against dwarven patrols and vengeful dragons alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dragonmaw-clan-4260f9f5ec46e4558260/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.929796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:elune"] = {
  hash = "7d356d",
  fullHash = "7d356d",
  text = "Elune. Elune is the revered moon goddess of Azeroth, worshipped primarily by the night elves as their benevolent protector and mother of the forest demigod Cenarius. Associated with the larger moon, the White Lady, she governs peace, healing, and lunar fury across the world. In tauren myth, she is honored as Mu'sha, the gentle left eye of the Earth Mother. Elune is one of the few true deities acknowledged across Azeroth, bound inseparably to the night sky and the larger of the world's two moons, the White Lady. While never appearing in a physical form on the world, night elven artisans portray her as a kaldorei of transcendent grace, bathed in luminescence with alabaster skin, silver hair, and eyes of pure moonlight. Ancient legends among the kaldorei claim she once rested in the depths of the Well of Eternity during the daylight hours, whispering wisdom to the early night elves. Night elven spiritual and civil life is anchored in devotion to Elune through the Sisterhood of Elune, an ancient matriarchal priesthood led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind. The Sisters invoke Elune's grace to mend lethal wounds, but they can also channel her radiant wrath to call down searing starlight upon their enemies. Her divine union with the Great White Stag, Malorne, produced Cenarius, binding Elune's heavenly mysteries directly to the druidic traditions that tend the wildlands of Kalimdor. Though renowned for peace, harmony, and tranquil restoration, Elune is not a gentle pacifist. As the Night Warrior, she acts as a fierce patron of battle who gathers the spirits of fallen heroes from the field and places them in the heavens to ride across the night as stars. Beyond the kaldorei, the nomadic tauren know her as Mu'sha, the radiant left eye of the Earth Mother, who illuminates the night and guides wandering hunters alongside her fiery twin brother, An'she the sun.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_elune-0be5c7f9e3b025337dfd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 108.564898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:emerald-dream"] = {
  hash = "8988fb",
  fullHash = "8988fb",
  text = "Emerald Dream. The Emerald Dream is a vast, ever-shifting spirit realm representing Azeroth in its primordial state, completely untouched by civilization. Watched over by the Dragon Aspect Ysera and her green flight, it serves as the spiritual blueprint of nature and the sacred domain where druids commune with the cycle of life. Existing outside the physical plane yet tethered to its heartbeat, the Emerald Dream reflects what Azeroth would look like had intelligent beings never cleared its forests or built cities upon its stone. Sculpted during the ancient ordering of the world, it is an ever-shifting wilderness of pristine forests, boundless plains, and primordial rivers. Time and distance hold little meaning within the Dream; days in the physical world can feel like decades in the spirit, and landmarks change shape like mist before mortal eyes. Following the ordering of the world, the titans entrusted this verdant plane to Ysera the Dreamer, the great Aspect of the green dragonflight. Charged with guiding the evolution and natural rhythm of Azeroth, Ysera dwells in an unending trance, gazing through enchanted dreamsight that perceives both the waking world and its mirrored spirit plane. The green dragons guard the rare, natural portals linking the physical plane to the Dream, maintaining eternal vigilance over the boundary between sleep and waking. To mortal druids, the Emerald Dream is the wellspring of their craft. By channeling nature magic, druids can project their spirits into the Dream, leaving their corporeal bodies resting safely in barrow dens for years or even centuries. In this untamed realm, they commune with ancient nature spirits and the Wild Gods, gaining profound insight into the Great Cycle of life and death. Skilled walkers of the Dream learn to transcend physical laws while in their dreamforms, traversing vast distances or slipping through solid barriers at will.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_emerald-dream-70b1a1e776e4917f0147/1-0000.mp3", duration = 109.949388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:explorers-league"] = {
  hash = "ca3a2a",
  fullHash = "ca3a2a",
  text = "Explorers' League. The Explorers' League is a renowned dwarven archaeological guild headquartered in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. Founded under King Magni Bronzebeard and led by his wandering brother Brann, the League seeks to uncover the forgotten origins of the dwarven race. Their expansive digs span across Azeroth, uncovering ancient titan ruins while frequently igniting regional conflicts. For centuries, the dwarves of Khaz Modan focused their formidable energies on mining, stonework, and engineering. That focus changed drastically when ancient ruins were unearthed, hinting at a forgotten legacy connecting dwarves to mythical titan creators. Eager to solve the mystery of his people's beginnings, King Magni Bronzebeard decreed that dwarven industry pivot heavily toward archaeology. Magni, alongside his brother Brann, established the Explorers' Guild—now widely known as the Explorers' League—to scour the world for relics, forgotten cities, and traces of their ancient ancestry. The League's operations are directed from the cavernous Hall of Explorers in Ironforge, oversaw on a day-to-day administrative level by High Explorer Muninn Magellas while Brann Bronzebeard explores distant reaches. Across Khaz Modan and beyond, prospectors manage extensive digsites. Sites like Ironband's Excavation in Loch Modan and Whelgar's Excavation in the Wetlands uncover immense fossil records and ancient architecture. However, prospectors often find themselves under siege by displaced wildlife, troggs, and rival factions interested in subterranean relics. The League's aggressive expeditions have sparked fierce resistance outside dwarven homelands. In Kalimdor, League operations such as the Bael Modan dig in the southern Barrens drove out the native Stonespire tauren through heavy industrial blasting, stirring vengeance and angering local earth spirits. In the arid Badlands, League prospectors clash openly with the Dark Iron clan over the vast titan vaults of Uldaman.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_explorers-league-db3a51daf92894acc726/1-0000.mp3", duration = 113.057959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:first-war"] = {
  hash = "052f06",
  fullHash = "052f06",
  text = "First War. The First War was the devastating five-year conflict in which the Orcish Horde emerged through the Dark Portal and completely destroyed the human Kingdom of Stormwind. Orchestrated behind the scenes by the demon-possessed Guardian Medivh and the warlock Gul'dan, it concluded with the assassination of King Llane Wrynn I and the flight of human survivors north to Lordaeron. Before the war, the Kingdom of Stormwind enjoyed long years of peace following the Gnoll War, guarded by its Knights and the Clerics of Northshire Abbey. That stability shattered when the Guardian Medivh, secretly possessed by the demon lord Sargeras, contacted the orc warlock Gul'dan on Draenor. Together, they forged the Dark Portal in the swampy Black Morass. Driven by demon blood and the slow death of their homeworld, the Orcish Horde crossed into Azeroth in force. Early rumors dismissed the invaders as vengeful spirits or strange offshoots of trolls, but Sir Anduin Lothar's scouting patrols quickly confirmed the arrival of a disciplined, bloodthirsty army. Led openly by Warchief Blackhand and manipulated from the shadows by Gul'dan's Shadow Council, the Horde overran the southern reaches of the kingdom. While an attempt by the orcs to push into Stranglethorn Vale against the Gurubashi trolls failed, their campaign across human territory proved catastrophic. The Horde conquered outward until only Elwynn Forest remained under human control. Blackhand launched a first direct assault on Stormwind City, but Sir Anduin Lothar repelled the invasion with a brilliant, surprise flanking counterattack by sea, temporarily saving the capital from annihilation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_first-war-a4f5a91bb58974bbd064/1-0000.mp3", duration = 92.6040816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:forsaken"] = {
  hash = "0d519e",
  fullHash = "0d519e",
  text = "Forsaken. The Forsaken are a free-willed faction of intelligent undead who broke away from the Scourge following the Third War. Led by the Banshee Queen Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, they dwell within the subterranean Undercity carved beneath the ruins of Lordaeron's capital. Though deeply distrusted across Azeroth, they maintain a tenuous alliance of convenience with the Horde. When the Lich King's grip over his northern armies slipped after the Third War, thousands of undead in the blighted ruins of Lordaeron suddenly regained their sapience and memories. Horrified by the butchery they had been forced to carry out while enslaved to his will, these former citizens found themselves reviled and hunted by the living. Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the fallen ranger-general of Quel'Thalas who had been transformed into an unholy banshee, rallied these adrift spirits, claimed the catacombs beneath Capital City, and dubbed her new nation the Forsaken. Hemmed in by relentless enemies—chief among them the fanatic Scarlet Crusade and the Scourge—Sylvanas dispatched emissaries seeking allies. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, tauren elders counseled compassion, believing the cursed people might yet find redemption or a cure through the earth. Despite Warchief Thrall's misgivings, the Horde admitted the Forsaken, gaining a vital northern foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_forsaken-bd0b68e4bb4f1964de73/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.0016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:frostmane-tribe"] = {
  hash = "7455fb",
  fullHash = "7455fb",
  text = "Frostmane tribe. Across Dun Morogh’s frigid peaks, the Frostmane tribe fights to reclaim its home. These ice trolls are native to the mountains, but dwarves drove them into caves and remote ridges centuries ago. From there, the Frostmane wage a guerrilla war against the dwarves. Long before the Bronzebeard clan united under Ironforge, the Frostmane trolls ruled across Dun Morogh’s snowbound reaches. The rise and spread of the dwarven kingdom crushed their realm, forcing survivors to retreat into caves and along frozen ridges and crags. That defeat bred a bitter hatred passed down through generations. To the Frostmane, every mountaineer, traveler, and homestead is an invader to drive out or slaughter. For decades, the Ironforge Senate saw the Frostmane as little more than a nuisance in the hills. Then the trogg menace emerged deep beneath Dun Morogh, throwing dwarven defenses into disarray. With the mountaineers overwhelmed and dwarven forces fighting Alliance wars abroad, the Frostmane seized their chance. From strongholds such as Frostmane Hold and Shimmer Ridge, they launched renewed, violent raids to reclaim their ancient lands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_frostmane-tribe-99288f512d986b693865/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.8359184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:gnoll"] = {
  hash = "76806d",
  fullHash = "76806d",
  text = "Gnoll. Gnolls are a savage, hyena-like race of humanoid scavengers and raiders found scattered across both the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor. Renowned for their vicious infighting, brutal strength, and animal cunning, they survive by pillaging settlements, butchering wildlife, and ambushing unwary travelers. Left unchecked, their sprawling packs and warlords pose a constant threat to frontier hamlets and trade roads alike. Standing slightly shorter than the average human but possessing explosive physical power, gnolls are bipedal hyena-kin known for their spiky teeth, thick pelts, and distinct barking laugh. While fully capable of walking upright, they often drop to all fours to lope swiftly through brush or track prey by scent. Gnoll culture values raw physical dominance above all else. They organize into fractious packs led by the strongest, cruelest alpha, only yielding when battered into submission by a superior force. Though usually fractured by petty squabbles, united gnolls once waged open war on the kingdom of Stormwind. Stormwind's armies prevailed in that Gnoll War, and the kingdom enjoyed a lasting peace afterward until the orcs came through the Dark Portal. Gnoll menace is not limited to the human south. In northern Lordaeron, the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw raises undead Rot Hide gnolls to dig up corpses across Tirisfal and Silverpine. Near Hillsbrad, the Mudsnout harvest foul, toxic blossoms among ruined farmsteads. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, the Palemane pack butchers game across Mulgore despite tauren efforts to make peace, while the Woodpaw of Feralas wage a desperate, frenzied war against encroaching silithid swarms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_gnoll-973417c3cab2d6215384/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.2653061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:gnome"] = {
  hash = "6f99ae",
  fullHash = "6f99ae",
  text = "Gnome. Gnomes are a diminutive, eccentric, and astonishingly intelligent race renowned across Azeroth for their inventive engineering and mastery of the arcane. Despite their cheerful demeanor, they recently suffered a catastrophic tragedy when a subterranean trogg invasion and a disastrous defense plan irradiated their capital of Gnomeregan. Now living as refugees in Ironforge's Tinker Town, the gnomes remain staunch Alliance allies as they fight to reclaim their lost home. Small in stature but towering in intellect, gnomes approach the world with boundless curiosity and scientific ambition. While other races focus on recording histories and preserving traditions, gnomes look relentlessly forward, dedicating their lives to mechanical engineering, arcane studies, and technological innovation. Their inventions—ranging from clockwork devices and flying machines to complex subterranean trains—have shaped the modern world. Even after devastating hardships, gnomefolk maintain an irrepressible cheerfulness and an unshakable drive to rebuild and solve the impossible. The gnomes made their home in the frozen crags of Dun Morogh, and when they met the dwarves of Ironforge, the two peoples discovered an immediate kinship. Dwarven brawn and gnomish ingenuity united to carve out the marvelous subterranean techno-city of Gnomeregan in western Dun Morogh, where the gnomes are led by visionary leaders known as High Tinkers. The surviving gnomes fled their poisoned home, finding sanctuary among their dwarven cousins in Ironforge. High Tinker Mekkatorque and his people established a bustling enclave in Ironforge's Tinker Town, continuing to support the Alliance and oversee the Deeprun Tram linking Ironforge to Stormwind. From this adopted quarter, gnomish commandos and brave volunteers constantly mount expeditions into Gnomeregan's irradiated depths, striving to clear out the troggs, deal with mechanical abominations, and someday reclaim their grand city.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_gnome-2c7d49465c76d2991777/1-0000.mp3", duration = 113.214694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:goblin"] = {
  hash = "cac159",
  fullHash = "cac159",
  text = "Goblin. Goblins are diminutive, cunning humanoids originating from the Isle of Kezan, renowned across Azeroth for their relentless pursuit of profit, explosive engineering, and aggressive trade cartels. While most maintain a stance of strict neutrality to maximize commerce between the Alliance and Horde, mercenary and cutthroat outfits like the Venture Company and Steamwheedle Cartel leave an indelible mark on every corner of the world. Goblins hail from the Isle of Kezan in the South Seas, where their society long ago divided into competing economic syndicates known as cartels. Rather than building kingdoms or armies of their own, goblin leaders channeled their ambitions into cutthroat mercantilism, volatile engineering, and industrial ventures that now reach every corner of the world. During the Second War, Trade Prince Steamwheedle allied his cartel with Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, supplying maps, siege craft, and fleet-building expertise in exchange for Stormwind's captured plunder. Following the Horde's eventual defeat, the cartels learned that open allegiance cost them lucrative markets. In the modern era, the major cartels claim official neutrality, maintaining bustling trade havens like Booty Bay in Stranglethorn Vale and Gadgetzan in the Tanaris desert. Here, Alliance and Horde travelers cross paths without blades drawn—so long as port bruisers keep the peace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_goblin-9956d20d0e19c744a8b7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.0653061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:grimtotem-tribe"] = {
  hash = "7b8e92",
  fullHash = "7b8e92",
  text = "Grimtotem tribe. The Grimtotem tribe is an aggressive, fiercely traditionalist tauren clan that refuses to formally swear allegiance to Thrall's Horde. Distinguishable by their dark hides and fierce warpaint, they seek to purge foreign races from Kalimdor and retake ancestral lands by any means necessary. Even as their matriarch, the Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem, resides atop Thunder Bluff playing regional politics, her kin actively raid settlements across Kalimdor. Unlike their peaceful cousins who honor the Earth Mother through reverence, the Grimtotem believe Kalimdor belongs exclusively to the tauren. From youth, they are trained not in the patient harmony of the Great Hunt, but in ruthlessness, stealth, and lethal combat. They harbor immense disdain for the other races that have settled upon their ancient soils. Though Cairne Bloodhoof has welcomed them into Thunder Bluff in an effort to unite all tauren people, the Grimtotem clan stands alone as the only major tribe that has never formally joined Thrall's Horde, maintaining their own insular and violent agenda. In the wilderness far from Thunder Bluff's watchful eyes, Grimtotem hostility flares openly into violence. In Stonetalon Mountains, they butcher local settlements and threaten Horde supply outposts. Across the towering mesas of Thousand Needles, centered around Darkcloud Pinnacle, they constantly menace Freewind Post and plot to seize the canyon. Encounters in Feralas prove equally perilous: the Grimtotem attack Horde travelers, Alliance caravans, and fellow tauren without hesitation. Horde garrisons at Sun Rock Retreat and Camp Mojache regularly post bounties and dispatch warriors to cut down these violent outcasts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_grimtotem-tribe-085f4e7c74bb20462ba5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 96.6269388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:grommash-hellscream"] = {
  hash = "11683e",
  fullHash = "11683e",
  text = "Grommash Hellscream. After the Third War, Grommash Hellscream's people are free of the Blood Curse. Once bound to the Burning Legion by his thirst for battle, the Warsong chieftain gave his life to slay Mannoroth in Ashenvale and free the orcs. Before the Dark Portal opened, Grom Hellscream led the Warsong clan with fierce resolve. His pitch-black tattooed jaw and the deadly sweep of his two-handed axe, Gorehowl, set him apart. On Draenor, Gul'dan and the Shadow Council offered the orcs demonic strength. Grom was the first chieftain to drink the blood of the pit lord Mannoroth. The draught bound the orcs to demonic service and filled them with burning bloodlust. Many chieftains perished or lost themselves to it, but Grom kept his fierce spirit through the old Horde's horrors. He carried a quiet ache for what his people had surrendered. Left behind on Draenor during the Second War, Grom later crossed the Dark Portal and was stranded when the rift was shattered. For nearly fifteen years, he and the surviving Warsong warriors evaded the Alliance of Lordaeron. They endured the lethargy and despair that followed the fading of the demon blood. Then a young fugitive named Thrall found Grom in the wilderness, and hope returned. Grom saw Thrall's strength and noble heart, and taught him the proud heritage of their people. Together with Orgrim Doomhammer, they rallied the scattered clans, broke open the human internment camps, and began the New Horde. During the Third War, Grom's aggression rose again in Ashenvale's ancient forests. The night elves and the demigod Cenarius pressed his clan hard, and Grom drank from a fountain corrupted with Mannoroth's blood to save them. Bound again to the demon lord, he turned against his own people. Thrall and Jaina Proudmoore captured him and cleansed his spirit with ancient shamanic and arcane rites. Restored to his senses and burdened with guilt, Grom pursued Mannoroth into Demon Fall Canyon. He drove Gorehowl into the demon lord's chest, ending the demonic curse forever. Hellfire took Grom's life.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_grommash-hellscream-17eab57c831f43cccd5f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 119.196735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:harpy"] = {
  hash = "8d3ebf",
  fullHash = "8d3ebf",
  text = "Harpy. Harpies are predatory, avian humanoids resembling winged night elves who dwell in foul-smelling roosts across Kalimdor and the high mountain passes of Alterac. Infamous for their shrill shrieks and tempestuous elemental magic, they raid unwary travelers and blight the environments they inhabit. Night elf folklore links them to cursed betrayers of Queen Azshara, though their true origins remain uncertain. Harpies bear the upper torsos and heads of night elf women, joined to broad feathered wings and wicked talons. Despite their delicate, elven visage—which ironically grows more striking as they age—their nature is thoroughly savage. Originally roosting among the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains, they have aggressively migrated across Kalimdor, terrorizing the arid canyons of Durotar, the sacred boughs of Teldrassil, and the heights of Mulgore. Unlike the wild creatures they hunt, harpies do not live in harmony with nature; they despoil surrounding flora and fauna, turning their nesting grounds into foul, fetid middens littered with the picked-clean bones of their prey. Night elf myth often claims harpies originated as treacherous handmaidens cursed by Queen Azshara before the Great Sundering, though no one can say for certain where they truly came from. Whatever their origins, harpies today operate as ruthless matriarchies, where aspiring queens must betray, slaughter, and torment rivals to maintain command. Beyond rending flesh with razor-sharp claws, harpies command dangerous wind and lightning magic, channeling shrieking gales to batter foes from above. They are deeply cunning hunters capable of spoken language, occasionally trading scavenged oddities with subterranean creatures like kobolds in exchange for bright gems and shiny trinkets. Unwary explorers are sometimes beguiled by their elven forms, but physical contact carries grave danger: a harpy's kiss is venomous, rapidly inflicting a violent, wasting fever upon those foolish enough to let their guard down.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_harpy-32183701cfd4018238c6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 115.617959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:highborne"] = {
  hash = "a8d499",
  fullHash = "a8d499",
  text = "Highborne. The Highborne, or quel'dorei, were the aristocratic sorcerer caste of the ancient Kaldorei Empire who served Queen Azshara. Their reckless manipulation of the Well of Eternity brought the Burning Legion to Azeroth, leading to the devastating War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering. Before the Sundering, the Highborne arose as the favored upper class surrounding Queen Azshara. Claiming superiority over common kaldorei through selective bloodlines, intelligence, and opulent privilege, they strictly controlled access to the Well of Eternity. They discovered how to tap and shape its primal arcane energies, erecting magnificent cities across ancient Kalimdor. Their endless thirst for greater sorcery led them to draw deeper from the Well, turning its calm waters turbulent and dark. The ripples of arcane power created by Highborne spellcraft caught the attention of the dark titan Sargeras and the Burning Legion. Convinced by Azshara and her counselor Xavius that the demons would remake the world into an untainted paradise, the Highborne opened a massive gateway within the Well itself. This unleashed the catastrophic War of the Ancients. As demonic hordes slaughtered the populace, rebel forces rallied by Malfurion Stormrage fought to close the portal, causing the Well of Eternity to implode and tear the supercontinent apart. The Highborne fate splintered after the Sundering. Those dragged beneath the waves alongside Azshara were twisted into serpentine naga, while others fell to demonic curses and became satyrs. A faction under Dath'Remar Sunstrider aided the resistance but refused to abandon arcane magic, ultimately suffering exile across the sea to found the high elven kingdom of Quel'Thalas. Today, isolated enclaves like the Shen'dralar remain cloistered in Eldre'Thalas, shunned relics of an empire that nearly destroyed Azeroth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_highborne-ef16cbae77a8f849a7c6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 109.270204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:horde"] = {
  hash = "c15a6a",
  fullHash = "c15a6a",
  text = "Horde. The Horde is a coalition of outcast nations united under Warchief Thrall to secure their mutual survival and freedom. Anchored by the shamanic bond of the orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren across Kalimdor, the faction also maintains an uneasy alliance of convenience with the Forsaken in Lordaeron. Beset by historical grievances and hostile neighbors, the Horde values strength, action, and honor over political maneuverings. The modern Horde bears little resemblance to the demonic army that marched through the Dark Portal in the First and Second Wars. After shattering the Burning Legion's grasp upon his people, Warchief Thrall led the orcs across the Great Sea to Kalimdor to build a spiritual homeland founded on ancestral traditions and honor. Along the way, Thrall forged lasting ties with Vol'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls and Cairne Bloodhoof's tauren tribes, fighting side by side to drive off centaur and demonic incursions during the Third War. In Kalimdor, the orcs carved out the nation of Durotar and built the fortress capital of Orgrimmar, offering the Darkspear refuge upon the nearby Echo Isles. Just south, in the lush plains of Mulgore, the nomadic tauren unified around Thunder Bluff under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Sharing shamanic reverence, a deep bond with nature, and mutual survival needs, these three races view each other not as tactical assets, but as an enduring family bound by spilled blood and spiritual respect. Across the sea in the ruined lands of Lordaeron, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner led the free-willed Forsaken out of the Scourge's grasp. Desperate for allies against surviving human factions and remnants of the Scourge, Sylvanas sought a place within the Horde. Tauren elders argued the undead might yet be redeemed, and Thrall admitted the Forsaken into the Horde, establishing a vital military outpost in the Eastern Kingdoms despite persistent misgivings among Kalimdor's ranks. Despite Thrall's hopes to build an enduring peace with the Alliance, the legacy of the Old Horde casts an inescapable shadow. To human and dwarven eyes, orcs remain bloodthirsty invaders, and undead can never be trusted. Clashes over vital lumber in Ashenvale and skirmishes along territorial borders have plunged both factions into a tense, fragile cold war. Thrall actively directs campaigns against demon cults like the Burning Blade to prove the Horde has severed ties with the Legion, but deep-seated hatred threatens to reignite all-out war at any spark.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_horde-9038bf590ec47abcce90/1-0000.mp3", duration = 143.281633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:juggernaught"] = {
  hash = "c58e70",
  fullHash = "c58e70",
  text = "Juggernaught. A Juggernaught is an enormous, heavily armored battleship bristling with heavy naval cannons. Built with ogre help to serve as the iron core of the Old Horde's fleet during the Second War, these floating fortresses were built to pulverize coastal strongholds and trade routes. Reports of a great vessel beneath Westfall draw attention to the hidden workings of the Defias Brotherhood. Orcs were not traditionally a seafaring culture, so when the Horde required a fleet capable of crossing the Great Sea and contesting the Alliance's naval superiority during the Second War, they relied heavily on ogre ingenuity. The resulting craft—the ogre juggernaught—became the backbone of the Horde armada. Though vastly slower and less nimble than swift troll destroyers, these behemoths carried immense plating and devastating broadside batteries that could shatter Alliance hulls and smash shore fortifications to rubble.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_juggernaught-a6783064bb5efaba6c91/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.0432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand"] = {
  hash = "c38ae1",
  fullHash = "c38ae1",
  text = "Knights of the Silver Hand. The Knights of the Silver Hand are the legendary order of holy paladins founded after the First War to combine martial combat with the healing and smiting power of the Holy Light. Though their original northern chapter was broken during the fall of Lordaeron, surviving knights carry on their sacred vows in Stormwind and Ironforge, while scattered remnants battle the undead in the northern Plaguelands. Following the destruction of Stormwind during the First War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol recognized a dire flaw in the Alliance's defenses: the clerics of Northshire were devoted and skilled healers, but they lacked martial training and fell easily on the front lines. To ensure holy warriors could endure the brutal combat of the Second War, Faol and his premier student, Uther the Lightbringer, founded the Order of the Knights of the Silver Hand. Faol recruited both battle-hardened knights willing to learn devotion to the Light and devout clerics willing to bear plate armor and warhammers. Led by Uther the Lightbringer, the first paladins proved instrumental to the Alliance of Lordaeron. Riding into combat, the paladins lifted the morale of weary soldiers and countered the sinister necromancy of the Horde's death knights. Throughout the war, the Silver Hand broke peasant rebellions, reclaimed Caer Darrow, and helped defend Capital City against the siege of the orcs. In the years of peace that followed, the paladins guarded Lordaeron's borders, settled civil disputes, and served as shining exemplars of honor, humility, and justice. Tragedy struck the order from within during the Third War. Prince Arthas Menethil, a decorated knight of the Silver Hand, clashed bitterly with his mentor Uther outside Stratholme and unlawfully suspended the paladins when Uther refused to butcher the infected citizenry. When Arthas later succumbed to corruption and led the Scourge against Lordaeron, Uther was slain, shattering the order's central leadership. The surviving knights in the north fractured under differing doctrines, eventually birthing the zealous Scarlet Crusade and the vigilant Argent Dawn, while the surviving chapters in Stormwind and Ironforge continue to train new paladins under the classic banner of the Silver Hand.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_knights-of-the-silver-hand-d372d70182eb8a411be5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 123.350204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:kobold"] = {
  hash = "050fa9",
  fullHash = "050fa9",
  text = "Kobold. Kobolds are diminutive, rat-like subterranean humanoids found scuttling through mines and caverns across Azeroth. Notorious for their obsessive devotion to the tallow candles strapped to their heads, they are individually cowardly but dangerous in swarms, fiercely defending their dig sites against any who threaten their light. Standing barely four feet tall, kobolds are filthy, subterranean scavengers who spend their lives delving into the earth. Though they possess low-light vision, the deepest shafts are pitch-black, compelling every kobold to affix a crude candle to their head. While cowardly when cornered alone, kobolds breed in massive numbers and work with relentless energy. When agitated, their rat-like tails whip violently, and they overwhelm intruders in frantic mobs, beating foes to death with heavy iron mining picks. To a kobold, a candle is far more than a light source; it is an existential shield. They harbor a deep, primal terror of the pitch-black tunnels, believing that the unlit darkness will actively consume them. Across the Eastern Kingdoms, kobolds frequently overrun commercial mines. In Elwynn Forest, a clan displaced from the Redridge Mountains seized the Echo Ridge, Fargodeep, and Jasperlode mines, driven in part by Riverpaw gnolls and the Defias Brotherhood. In Loch Modan, the Tunnel Rat clan seized the Silver Stream Mine from the dwarven Miners' League. Even on Kalimdor, kobolds plague industrial digs, laboring for the Venture Company in Stonetalon or uncovering strange minerals in Thousand Needles.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_kobold-c268cb594a5a79f4760c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.4546939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:kolkar-clan"] = {
  hash = "21a162",
  fullHash = "21a162",
  text = "Kolkar clan. The Kolkar are an aggressive clan of centaur native to central Kalimdor, claiming descent from the legendary first Khan, Kolk. They maintain strongholds in Desolace and across the Barrens, frequently raiding Horde settlements and fighting anyone who crosses their path. The Kolkar take their name from Khan Kolk, the legendary first centaur khan born from the union of Zaetar and Theradras. Driven by a deep hatred for other races and their fellow centaur clans alike, the Kolkar distinguish themselves by their red markings and an absolute refusal to form alliances. Historically based out of their ancient village in eastern Desolace, the restless spirit of Khan Kolk still wanders the subterranean depths of their ancestral lands. For generations, the nomadic tauren endured brutal raids from the Kolkar as they traversed the arid Barrens. When the orcs arrived on Kalimdor during the Third War, Thrall allied with Cairne Bloodhoof to push the marauding centaur back and escort the tauren to safety in Mulgore. Defeat did not break the clan; instead, it fueled a lingering blood feud. The Kolkar entrenched themselves around all three vital water sources in the Barrens: the Forgotten Pools, the Lushwater Oasis, and the Stagnant Oasis. Emboldened by their fortified positions across the savanna, the Kolkar have pushed eastward past the Southfury River into Durotar itself. From Kolkar Crag in the south, raiding parties plot assaults against Sen'jin Village, the Valley of Trials, and the paths approaching Orgrimmar. While senior Horde leadership focuses on larger regional threats, young recruits are dispatched to cull the centaur advance before their raiding parties can strike the nascent orc capital.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_kolkar-clan-eb66f1f6918a5432b5b4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.5526531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:kul-tiras"] = {
  hash = "64b74d",
  fullHash = "64b74d",
  text = "Kul Tiras. Kul Tiras is an insular maritime human kingdom situated on an island archipelago in the Great Sea, west of Dun Morogh and Baradin Bay. Renowned for possessing the mightiest navy in Azeroth, it served as a cornerstone of the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War under Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore. Following the fracturing of the old Alliance and tragic losses in Kalimdor, the island realm has largely receded into isolated waters. Kul Tiras blossomed into an unmatched seafaring power, sustained by an industrious merchant marine, rich fishing grounds, and a naval armada without peer in human history. When the Orcish Horde swept through the Eastern Kingdoms, Kul Tiras answered the call of King Terenas Menethil II and committed its armada to the Alliance of Lordaeron. Lord Admiral Daelin Proudmoore commanded the naval forces of humanity, waging bloody warfare across the open ocean to sever the Horde's sea lanes and protect coastal shores. However, following the war's conclusion and the eventual splintering of the northern kingdoms, Kul Tiras pulled back its fleets, withdrawing into heavy coastal defense and quiet insularity.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_kul-tiras-cd10708a30be9f8f2831/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.5004082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:lordaeron"] = {
  hash = "d6a327",
  fullHash = "d6a327",
  text = "Lordaeron. Lordaeron is the great northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms, once the glorious heart of human civilization and the Alliance. Following the devastation of the Third War, its former kingdoms lie in ruins, contested by the undead Scourge, Sylvanas Windrunner's Forsaken, and desperate remnants of the living. Lordaeron shares its name with the human city-state of Lordaeron, which rose in the north after the ancient Empire of Arathor and grew into the most powerful of the human kingdoms. Stretching from the rugged Arathi Highlands in the south across scenic forests and waterways up toward Quel'Thalas, the region nurtured thriving kingdoms like Lordaeron, Dalaran, Gilneas, and Alterac. It was historically celebrated for fertile farmlands in Tirisfal and Hillsbrad, mountain strongholds, and the sacred cradle of the Holy Light. During the Third War, Lordaeron became ground zero for the Lich King's invasion. Through tainted grain, the Cult of the Damned transformed countless northern citizens into mindless undead monstrosities. Prince Arthas Menethil fell to darkness, returning from Northrend to slay his father, King Terenas Menethil II, and orchestrate the total slaughter of the Capital City. The thriving heartland quickly rotted into the blighted nightmare known as the Plaguelands. Today, Lordaeron is a brutal battleground defined by fractured loyalties. Beneath the ruins of the Capital City, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken have carved out the Undercity, establishing a sovereign realm of free-willed undead aligned with the Horde. To the east, the Lich King's mindless Scourge clings to corrupted keeps and strongholds. Meanwhile, human zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, surviving Alliance settlements in Hillsbrad, and wild forest trolls clash relentlessly over what remains.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_lordaeron-c8b2f5a56517e11a1c34/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.698776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:medivh"] = {
  hash = "d1374e",
  fullHash = "d1374e",
  text = "Medivh. Medivh was the last Guardian of Tirisfal, an ancient order tasked with defending Azeroth from demonic corruption. Bound from before his birth by the spirit of the Dark Titan Sargeras, he opened the Dark Portal and brought the orcish Horde into the world, setting off decades of catastrophic warfare. Medivh was born to the powerful Guardian Aegwynn and the court conjurer Nielas Aran. Centuries earlier, Aegwynn had fought the fallen Titan Sargeras in Northrend, unaware that his dark essence slipped into her body to await an heir. When Medivh reached his fourteenth birthday, the vast magical inheritance bestowed by his mother manifested in a terrifying explosion of power, plunging the boy into a decade-long coma under the watch of the clerics at Northshire Abbey. Awakening from his slumber, Medivh took residence in the isolated tower of Karazhan, serving as court magus to his childhood friend King Llane Wrynn of Stormwind. Yet the dark spirit within him slowly eroded his sanity. Seeking to unleash devastation, Medivh made contact across the stars with the orc warlock Gul'dan and the Shadow Council. Together, they channeled tremendous magic to tear open the Dark Portal, delivering the Horde upon the Eastern Kingdoms and sparking the First War. Medivh was ultimately slain in his tower by his closest friend, Anduin Lothar, and his apprentice, Khadgar.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_medivh-69d031fa7a1a89bf8fca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.9159184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:mor-ladim"] = {
  hash = "48b4da",
  fullHash = "48b4da",
  text = "Mor'Ladim. Mor'Ladim stalks the grounds of Raven Hill Cemetery in Duskwood, a vengeful skeletal warrior. He was once Morgan Ladimore, a noble knight who lost his family and was overcome by grief and madness, rising in undeath to kill those who cross his path. Before Raven Hill feared his name, Morgan Ladimore defended the helpless from Grand Hamlet, now called Darkshire. He married Lys at eighteen, and they raised three children in peace until war called him north. During the Third War, Morgan marched with Uther the Lightbringer to Lordaeron. There he witnessed the fall of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the spread of Kel'Thuzad's plague, and Uther's death. Morgan found no peace in the grave. Guilt over the innocent blood he had shed and suffocating grief stirred his corrupted remains from the earth as a hulking skeletal revenant. Travelers and the Night Watch soon learned to fear the name Mor'Ladim. With his ancestral blade Archeus, he patrols the misty graves, cutting down beasts and unwary travelers who stray near his resting place.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_mor-ladim-a1304af35d608aae4fca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.1118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:murloc"] = {
  hash = "4bf757",
  fullHash = "4bf757",
  text = "Murloc. Murlocs are amphibious, bipedal humanoids famous for their gurgling battle cries, territorial coastal villages, and sudden swarming tactics. Though historically dwelling in the deep ocean, they have steadily migrated inland along the shores, lakes, and rivers of Azeroth, acting with far greater coordination and cunning than their primitive appearance suggests. Murlocs dwell in densely populated settlements along coastlines, riverbanks, and freshwater lakes. Averaging roughly four feet in height, they possess bulbous, slime-coated bodies, wide mouths filled with sharp fangs, and keen vision capable of piercing pitch-black water. While individuals are rarely seen wandering far from their kin, encountering one murloc invariably means a dozen more lurk just beneath the surface. They rely on overwhelming numbers and sharp crude weaponry. Though ancient lineages suggest murlocs have existed beneath the waves since before recorded history, their widespread encroachment onto the landmasses of Lordaeron and Kalimdor is relatively recent. Historians note that this inland migration coincided suspiciously with the reemergence of the serpentine naga from the ocean depths. Some tribes appear to worship deep-sea monstrosities waiting in the oceanic fathoms. Whether fleeing darker terrors or preparing the world for their masters' awakening, their coordinated movement into freshwaters poses a rising threat. Because their garbled language sounds like nonsensical bubbling to outsiders, many dismiss murlocs as mere feral pests. However, their coordinated ambushes, mastery of earth and water magic, and communal structures hint at a sharper intellect. Renowned explorer Brann Bronzebeard has suggested that murlocs are far smarter than civilized races realize, intentionally concealing their cunning. Their impact on global history is undeniable: it was a murloc raid upon the Darkspear tribe that spurred Thrall to intervene, forging the alliance between the orcs and trolls that built the modern Horde.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_murloc-844b89f0f317b3b09d28/1-0000.mp3", duration = 120.346122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:naga"] = {
  hash = "5a88d5",
  fullHash = "5a88d5",
  text = "Naga. The naga are a monstrous, serpentine race dwelling beneath the ocean's depths, ruled by their ancient empress, Queen Azshara. Once the Highborne elite of the Kaldorei Empire, they were warped into amphibious horrors during the Great Sundering ten thousand years ago. Recently emerging from the dark waters, they have seized shorelines across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms to reclaim their forgotten surface empire. Ten millennia ago, the Highborne nobility of the Kaldorei Empire ruled supreme from their grand capital of Zin-Azshari. Consumed by an obsession with arcane power, Queen Azshara and her inner court tapped the Well of Eternity to draw the gaze of the Dark Titan Sargeras, inviting the catastrophic invasion known as the War of the Ancients. When the Well of Eternity collapsed into ruin, triggering the Great Sundering, the palace slipped into the crushing abyss. Facing utter annihilation, Azshara bargained with the malevolent Old Gods deep below. In exchange for survival, she and her followers were transformed into cold, scaly, serpentine beings bound to the deeps. Submerged at the seafloor beneath the swirling fury of the Maelstrom, the survivors erected the aquatic empire of Nazjatar. Nursing a ten-thousand-year grudge against the surface world, the naga preserved their Highborne arrogance while mutating into distinct casts: hulking, brute-like warriors and multi-armed, spell-weaving sea witches. Under Azshara's unyielding gaze, they perfected their mastery over sea creatures, storm magic, and frost sorcery, slowly rebuilding their strength in wait for an opportunity to strike back against land-dwellers. The naga broke their ten-thousand-year silence during the Third War. Thrall's Horde first encountered the sea witch Zar'jira in the Great Sea, while Lady Vashj answered the summons of Illidan Stormrage, marching naga forces across the ruins of Dalaran. Across the world today, naga encampments encroach aggressively upon coastlines in Azshara, Darkshore, Ashenvale, Desolace, Feralas, Dustwallow Marsh, Hillsbrad, Arathi, and Stranglethorn. Driven by ancient contempt and whispers of the Old Gods, they forge quiet alliances with the Twilight's Hammer and savage satyrs to claim what was once theirs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_naga-a1226c1859314ff891f1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 132.022857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:new-horde"] = {
  hash = "9b22d8",
  fullHash = "9b22d8",
  text = "New Horde. The New Horde is the modern coalition forged under Warchief Thrall, departing from the bloodthirsty, demon-corrupted Old Horde of the First and Second Wars. Founded upon shamanic tradition, honor, and mutual survival, it unites the liberated orcs, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren tribes of Kalimdor, and a tenuous pact with the Forsaken in Lordaeron. The original Horde was a weapon crafted by the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, fueling the orcs with demonic bloodlust and driving them across the Dark Portal. After the Second War ended in devastation, surviving orcs were rounded up into Alliance internment camps, sinking into a soul-draining lethargy. Through the efforts of Orgrim Doomhammer, Grommash Hellscream, and the young shaman Thrall, the orcs cast off their demonic shackles, rediscovered their ancestral shamanism, and fought their way to freedom. Following prophetic visions from the mysterious Prophet, Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to the untamed continent of Kalimdor. Along the way, the Horde welcomed Sen'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls. Upon arriving on Kalimdor, Thrall allied with Cairne Bloodhoof and the nomadic tauren, helping drive back hostile centaur tribes. Together, these disparate peoples fought alongside humans and night elves at Mount Hyjal, breaking the Burning Legion's invasion and carving out their new home of Durotar. Today, the New Horde strives to build a durable society from their fortified capital of Orgrimmar. While anchored by the strong bond between orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren, survival has forced Thrall to accept uncomfortable alliances—chief among them Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken in the ruins of Lordaeron. Thrall pursues an uneasy peace with the Alliance, but old enmities, resource shortages, and hawkish elements among both factions keep borders simmering on the brink of war.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_new-horde-798b11d92344b8b4e2a5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 112.248163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:night-elf"] = {
  hash = "38fa5a",
  fullHash = "38fa5a",
  text = "Night elf. The night elves, or kaldorei, are an ancient and reclusive race native to the forests of northern Kalimdor. Once the masters of a sprawling magical empire, they abandoned arcane magic following the catastrophic War of the Ancients and turned to the worship of Elune and the path of druidism. In the wake of the Third War, having sacrificed their immortality to stop the Burning Legion, they joined the Alliance to defend their ancestral lands from Horde encroachment. Millennia ago, an inquisitive nocturnal tribe gathered around the shores of the Well of Eternity, a vast font of raw arcane energy. Over generations, the Well's cosmic magic transformed them into tall, violet-skinned, long-lived beings who called themselves the kaldorei—'children of the stars.' Guiding their early society were the moon goddess Elune and the forest demigod Cenarius, who nurtured their deep connection to the living wilds of Kalimdor. As centuries passed, the ruling caste—the Highborne, led by Queen Azshara—became obsessed with unlocking the Well's infinite arcane secrets. Their reckless use of sorcery built breathtaking wonders, but it ultimately caught the attention of the Burning Legion. The ensuing demonic invasion ended in the Great Sundering: the Well imploded, shattering ancient Kalimdor into fragmented continents and destroying Azshara's realm. Horrified by the calamity, the surviving kaldorei strictly outlawed arcane magic under penalty of death or exile. For ten thousand years, the night elves retreated into seclusion. Druids slumbered in the Emerald Dream to heal the world while the Priestesses of Elune and Sentinels stood guard over Kalimdor's woodlands beneath the World Tree Nordrassil. This isolation ended during the Third War when the Legion returned to attack Mount Hyjal. The kaldorei sacrificed their immortality to defeat Archimonde. Now, led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and Archdruid Fandral Staghelm, the night elves dwell in their young World Tree, Teldrassil, and fight alongside the Alliance to safeguard Ashenvale.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_night-elf-48f73738893b48813aa9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 121.965714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:night-watch"] = {
  hash = "76a363",
  fullHash = "76a363",
  text = "Night Watch. In Duskwood, the Night Watch holds Darkshire and the forest roads against rising threats. Abandoned by Stormwind's House of Nobles, the local volunteers face undead, feral worgen, and necromancy with Commander Althea Ebonlocke at their head. They depend heavily on travelers to defend their home. Stormwind withdrew its royal garrison after foul magic consumed the Grand Hamlet and changed the forest into Duskwood. King Varian Wrynn had disappeared, and the troops were recalled to the capital. Left without royal soldiers or supplies, Darkshire's townsfolk formed the Night Watch. They swore to defend Darkshire's borders against the horrors creeping out of the woods. From its headquarters in Darkshire, the Night Watch patrols roads buried in unnatural mist. Skeletal fiends and ghouls emerge from Raven Hill and Tranquil Gardens cemeteries, while feral worgen prowl Roland's Doom and Brightwood Grove. The militia is badly outnumbered and short of resources, and many of its finest defenders have fallen to threats such as Mor'Ladim, the wandering skeletal warrior. Commander Althea Ebonlocke, daughter of Darkshire's mayor, directs the defense with grim pragmatism. With no veteran reinforcements, she enlists capable travelers to thin skeletal legions, cull starving wolf packs, and investigate mysterious local murders. The Night Watch is the last barrier protecting human life in the region. If its lines break, Darkshire will fall into shadow.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_night-watch-a9c275e50e6cd5ea40c9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 87.0138776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:nordrassil"] = {
  hash = "ba7450",
  fullHash = "ba7450",
  text = "Nordrassil. Nordrassil, the 'Crown of the Heavens,' is the original World Tree planted atop Mount Hyjal to shield the second Well of Eternity. Blessed by the Great Dragon Aspects, it granted the night elves ageless immortality and a deep connection to the Emerald Dream for ten thousand years. Its sacrifice during the climax of the Third War broke Archimonde's assault, leaving the tree wounded and the night elves mortal. Ten thousand years ago, in the ashes of the Great Sundering, Illidan Stormrage cast vials of the original Well of Eternity into a mountaintop lake atop Mount Hyjal, birthing a new font of arcane power. To prevent this raw magic from corrupting the world or drawing the Burning Legion anew, the Dragon Aspect Alexstrasza planted an acorn from the Mother Tree, G'Hanir, directly over the waters. From it grew Nordrassil. The roots spread deep beneath Azeroth, sealing the Well's volatile energies and distributing gentle, life-giving vigor across the continents. Nordrassil became the spiritual and cultural heart of night elf civilization through the blessings of three Great Dragon Aspects. Alexstrasza imbued the tree with peerless vigor, extending vitality to the kaldorei. Ysera bound the tree to the Emerald Dream, allowing night elven druids to enter her slumbering realm with effortless clarity. Crucially, Nozdormu the Timeless One cast his blessing across its boughs: as long as the colossal tree stood, the night elves would never age or fall prey to natural disease, gifting an entire race with enduring immortality. Nordrassil's tranquility ended with the Third War, when the demon lord Archimonde marched upon Mount Hyjal to tear the tree apart and claim the Well's infinite power. With mortal defenders pushed to the brink, Malfurion Stormrage made the ultimate choice. Sounding the Horn of Cenarius, he called upon thousands of nature spirits and unleashed the stored enchantments within Nordrassil itself. The ensuing cataclysmic blast obliterated Archimonde and decimated the Legion. Though Nordrassil survived to heal slowly, its enchantments shattered—stripping the night elves of their beloved immortality.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_nordrassil-f49fb17e45aed6a75741/1-0000.mp3", duration = 124.839184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:old-horde"] = {
  hash = "799acd",
  fullHash = "799acd",
  text = "Old Horde. The Old Horde was the blood-crazed coalition of orcish clans manipulated by demonic power into conquering Draenor and invading Azeroth. Led first by Blackhand and later by Orgrim Doomhammer, its path of destruction shaped the First and Second Wars until its decisive defeat by the Alliance of Lordaeron. Today, Thrall's New Horde seeks to overcome that dark legacy, while isolated remnants like the Dark Horde still cling to its bloody past. Before it crossed into Azeroth, the Horde was forged on Draenor through demonic manipulation. The demon lord Kil'jaeden used the warlock Gul'dan and the elder shaman Ner'zhul to corrupt the proud, spiritual orc clans. As elemental spirits abandoned the warring orcs, shamanistic traditions were cast aside for warlock magic. Sealed by the consumption of the blood of Mannoroth, the clans surrendered to bloodlust, slaughtering the draenei and draining their homeland of life. With Draenor's lands withering away and resources exhausted, the warlock Gul'dan and the possessed human guardian Medivh opened the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The Horde poured through, igniting the First War against the Kingdom of Stormwind. Under Warchief Blackhand—and the covert strings of Gul'dan's Shadow Council—the Horde battered the southern realm. Toward the war's conclusion, Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand and purged the Shadow Council, taking command of the Horde and razing Stormwind to the ground. Orgrim Doomhammer drove the Horde north across the seas into the Second War, forging alliances with forest trolls and goblin mercenaries. However, at the height of the siege of Lordaeron, Gul'dan deserted the war effort with his loyalists to seek the Tomb of Sargeras. Fractured and reeling from the betrayal, the Horde was pushed back to Blackrock Mountain and crushed by the Alliance of Lordaeron. Defeated, their portal destroyed, surviving orcs were herded into internment camps, afflicted with lethargy as the demonic blood haze faded.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_old-horde-6cc75b8c92c7f153159c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 114.416327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:orc"] = {
  hash = "58a901",
  fullHash = "58a901",
  text = "Orc. Originally a shamanistic clan people hailing from the alien world of Draenor, the orcs were deceived and corrupted into a bloodthirsty conquering force by the demonic Burning Legion. After invading Azeroth and suffering eventual defeat and internment, they were rallied by Warchief Thrall to reclaim their spiritual heritage. In this new era, the orcs lead the Horde from their newly carved nation of Durotar, striving for honor while grappling with the lingering shadow of their demonic past. Before their fall, orcs lived as nomadic hunter-gatherers and spiritual clans across the rugged landscapes of Draenor. Their culture was grounded in ancestral reverence and deep communion with elemental spirits, guided primarily by revered shamans. Among the disparate clans were the Frostwolves, Warsongs, and Shadowmoon. Though physically formidable and deeply proud, traditional orcish society was defined by survival, honor, and harmony with the natural elements rather than mindless conquest. The fate of the orcs shattered when Kil'jaeden and the warlock Gul'dan orchestrated their corruption. Tricked into drinking the blood of the demon Mannoroth, the orcs were seized by a supernatural bloodlust. Forged into the original Horde, they nearly eradicated Draenor's draenei before pouring through the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The First and Second Wars saw human kingdoms fall and orcish armies reach Lordaeron's gates, but internal treachery and the unified Alliance of Lordaeron eventually crushed the invaders, herding survivors into miserable internment camps. Imprisonment stripped the orcs of their demonic frenzy, leaving them lethargic and broken until Thrall, an orc raised in human captivity, escaped. Reconnecting with shamanism, Thrall rallied the Frostwolf clan, freed his kin from the camps, and established a renewed Horde. Heeding prophetic warnings, he led his people across the Great Sea to Kalimdor. There, the sacrifice of Grom Hellscream finally broke Mannoroth's blood curse forever. Today, the orcs have carved out the arid realm of Durotar and built the fortress-city of Orgrimmar, striving to maintain their hard-won honor amidst renewed tensions with the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_orc-2195ab10a7208fa12344/1-0000.mp3", duration = 126.354286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:pirate"] = {
  hash = "96fd15",
  fullHash = "96fd15",
  text = "Pirate. Pirates and buccaneers are lawless seafaring plunderers operating across the Great Sea and coastal waters of Azeroth. Preying on Alliance, Horde, and neutral merchant vessels alike, they band together under loose flags to seize wealth, capture cargo, and control coastal coves. Their violent presence drives constant naval skirmishes from the waters of Kalimdor to the shores of the Eastern Kingdoms. Pirates—often called buccaneers, corsairs, or freebooters—are maritime raiders of nearly every race who make their living seizing ships and raiding coastal settlements. Their operations stretch far beyond simple robbery; crews regularly engage in smuggling, kidnappings, and mercenary contracts for whoever has coin. With the chaos left behind by the Third War and the collapse of northern kingdoms, naval enforcement has fractured, giving pirate factions the freedom to carve out fortified footholds on neglected islands and coastal coves. Despite common tales painting pirate life as one of romantic adventure, life aboard a buccaneer vessel is brutal and short. Crews endure squalid, rat-infested holds, harsh discipline, and the ever-present threat of scurvy, fought off only with supplies of limes. Betrayal is an everyday hazard, and captains maintain their command solely through strength and fear of punishment, which ranges from immediate execution to being marooned or fed to sharks. When battles end, buccaneers celebrate with roaring bouts of dice, song, and heavy rum. While independent crews roam every sea lane, major organizations wield enough ships to contest sovereign navies. The Southsea Freebooters harass shipping throughout the waters surrounding Kalimdor, while the notorious Bloodsail Buccaneers menace the southern seas and wage open war against the Steamwheedle Cartel in Booty Bay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_pirate-76409ae6f31b77a8b31c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 106.736327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:plague-of-undeath"] = {
  hash = "41b387",
  fullHash = "41b387",
  text = "Plague of Undeath. The Plague of Undeath is a supernatural contagion concocted by the Lich King under the direction of the Burning Legion to scourge northern Eastern Kingdoms. Transmitted through infected grain, airborne cauldrons, and tainted waterways, the sickness rapidly kills living mortals and raises them as mind-bound thralls of the Scourge. The plague was devised by the fallen shaman Ner'zhul after he was transformed into the Lich King by Kil'jaeden. From his icy seat atop the Frozen Throne in Northrend, Ner'zhul perfected the sickness as an unholy vanguard for the Burning Legion's invasion. Testing his power on isolated human villages across Northrend, he discovered that he could snuff out life and psychically command the corpses that arose shortly after. Ner'zhul's telepathic summon reached across the Great Sea to the Kirin Tor mage Kel'Thuzad, who abandoned Dalaran to serve the entity. Kel'Thuzad organized the secretive Cult of the Damned, whose mortal infiltrators laid the groundwork to poison Lordaeron from within. The blight extends far beyond humanity. While non-human fauna generally suffer sickening fevers or rot rather than rising as undead thralls, the land itself bears the brunt of demonic and necromantic decay. Across the Plaguelands, massive plague cauldrons pump alchemical and magical vapors directly into the atmosphere, corrupting the surrounding land, where carrion grubs and colossal spore-choked mushrooms thrive. The Argent Dawn actively battles this airborne corruption by sabotaging these cauldrons, reversing their catalytic mixtures to burn the Scourge rather than feed it. Meanwhile, in the Undercity, the Royal Apothecary Society experiments tirelessly to refine a New Plague designed to slay both living foes and the undead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_plague-of-undeath-9d7d0446e8a3f3c3ee94/1-0000.mp3", duration = 103.78449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:quilboar"] = {
  hash = "c54785",
  fullHash = "c54785",
  text = "Quilboar. Quilboar are aggressive, boar-like humanoids native to central Kalimdor, renowned for their sharp spinal quills and relentless territorial savagery. Claiming descent from the fallen demigod Agamaggan, they dwell within dense mazes of razor-sharp brambles across Durotar, Mulgore, and the Barrens. Their fierce struggle over water, soil, and sacred ground makes them a constant threat to travelers and Horde settlements. Quilboar revere Agamaggan, the colossal boar Ancient who perished battling the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. According to their sacred traditions, the quilboar are his mortal offspring. Where Agamaggan bled and died across central Kalimdor, enormous thorny vines erupted from the scorched earth, forming sprawling root-mazes that the quilboar treat as holy ground. His crystallized blood endures across the Barrens as crimson Blood Shards, which their geomancers dig up and revere as powerful talismans. Rather than building traditional towns of stone or timber, quilboar cultivate colossal briars that choke surrounding vegetation and render nearby ground barren. Tribes are typically guided by powerful geomancers—practitioners of an earthbound, crude shamanism who consult stones and crystals to direct the clan. These mystics call upon Agamaggan's spirit to summon spectral boars into combat. Because the arid central plains offer few oases, quilboar view water as the ultimate treasure; they stage lavish feasts when rare rains fall and defend water holes with vicious tenacity. For generations, quilboar clashed bitterly with the nomadic tauren and roaming centaur packs. When Thrall led the Orcish Horde into Kalimdor, orcs rapidly colonized Durotar and established trade routes through the Barrens, driving displaced tribes into open revolt. Quilboar war parties ambush supply wagons, are suspected of frightening tallstriders into wagon trains, and stage raids on outposts like Crossroads and Camp Taurajo. Two principal tribes dominate the open plains: the Razormane and the Bristleback, both engaged in simultaneous feuds against the Horde and each other.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_quilboar-6071f7e8d02e1d0cf470/1-0000.mp3", duration = 124.16, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:ravenholdt"] = {
  hash = "0c8208",
  fullHash = "0c8208",
  text = "Ravenholdt. Ravenholdt, also known as the Assassin's League, is an elite guild of rogues, thieves, and killers based out of a secluded mountain manor above the Hillsbrad Foothills. Led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the league maintains strict neutrality from faction politics while waging an unending shadow war against their bitter rivals, the Syndicate. They test and recruit only the most cunning operatives across Azeroth, valuing peerless discipline, stealth, and lethal efficiency above all else. Tucked away in the craggy mountains connecting Hillsbrad to Alterac, Ravenholdt Manor serves as the nerve center for Azeroth's premier brotherhood of assassins. Led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the organization operates far beyond the jurisdiction of kings and warchiefs. Within its walls, master specialists hone every lethal art: Master Kang instructs disciples in close combat and assassination, the Thunderwood brothers brew rare and deadly poisons, and gnome craftsmen engineer intricate locks to test fledglings. To non-rogues, entering the estate is an overt flirtation with death, where an unwary step easily ends with a poisoned dagger in the ribs. For years, Ravenholdt has been locked in an escalating feud with the Syndicate, an aristocratic criminal cartel infesting Alterac and Arathi. While the League regards the Syndicate as clumsy, arrogant amateurs, their sheer numbers make them impossible to eradicate through simple slaughter. Instead, Ravenholdt employs psychological and structural disruption: by tasking contracted rogues with pickpocketing the signet emblems worn by Syndicate ringleaders, the League relies on the Syndicate's own paranoia and cutthroat hierarchy to execute disgraced members internally.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_ravenholdt-4becbf445f1c65a9fc9e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.0302041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:razorfen"] = {
  hash = "fe4ab7",
  fullHash = "fe4ab7",
  text = "Razorfen. Razorfen is the collective name associated with the predominant quilboar tribe of southern Kalimdor and their sprawling thorn-choked settlements. Carved into massive briars along the southern border of the Barrens and the edge of Thousand Needles, Razorfen encompasses the strongholds of Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs. It serves as both the spiritual heart and the central bastion of quilboar power. According to legend, the colossal thorned brambles that make up the Razorfen sprouted ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients. When the giant boar Ancient, Agamaggan, fell in battle against the Burning Legion, his blood seeped into the earth wherever he bled and died. From that divine sacrifice grew massive vines of petrified bramble and iron-hard thorns, creating a natural fortress where his descendants, the quilboar, have dwelled ever since. The Razorfen tribe is among the most aggressive and entrenched of all quilboar populations. Rather than scattering into open warrens, they built complex labyrinthine communities directly within the colossal briars. From these fortresses, the tribe fiercely defends their ancestral territory against neighboring tauren tribes, Horde settlements in the Barrens, and any outsiders daring to trespass into their holy grounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_razorfen-ea42dabae8b7494992d0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.284898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:refugee"] = {
  hash = "04912e",
  fullHash = "04912e",
  text = "Refugee. Throughout the violent history of Azeroth, constant warfare has repeatedly driven civilian populations from their homes. From the exodus of Stormwind during the First War to the tragic flight from Lordaeron in the Third War, refugees remain living testaments to the devastating costs of invasion, plague, and betrayal. When the Orcish Horde swept through the kingdom of Stormwind during the First War, settlements like Goldshire, Moonbrook, and Grand Hamlet burned. Following the assassination of King Llane Wrynn, Sir Anduin Lothar took command of the kingdom's remnants. He gathered surviving soldiers and desperate civilians, escorting them to the harbor as the city burned. Lothar led a massive fleet across the Great Sea to the northern shores of Lordaeron. Arriving near Southshore, the exiled survivors—including the young Prince Varian Wrynn—were granted sanctuary by King Terenas Menethil II. A generation after welcoming Stormwind's exiles, the people of Lordaeron faced ruin of their own. During the Third War, the Scourge plague and the legions of the undead systematically dismantled the kingdom. Prince Arthas Menethil slaughtered his own subjects as he swept across the realm, leaving survivors scattered and terrified. Lady Jaina Proudmoore managed to lead an expedition of refugees across the sea to Kalimdor, eventually establishing a settlement at Theramore. However, countless others remained behind in the Eastern Kingdoms, hunted relentlessly by the Scourge, the Forsaken, and monstrous horrors that arose in the ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_refugee-1fe7eefbdf068e9c426c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 87.4579592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:riverpaw-pack"] = {
  hash = "3e6b61",
  fullHash = "3e6b61",
  text = "Riverpaw pack. The Riverpaw pack is a savage clan of gnolls inhabiting the borderlands of Elwynn Forest and Westfall. Known for their brutal raids and ruthless dominance over local kobolds, they have grown increasingly organized and brazen following the Third War. With Stormwind's military stretched thin, their presence threatens vital roads and settlements throughout the heartland. For decades, the Riverpaw clan prowled the fringes of human civilization, but their boldness has surged in recent years. In Elwynn Forest, packs led by the infamous brute Hogger prowl the southern woods around Forest's Edge and roam the shores of Stone Cairn Lake. In Westfall, they roam across the Dust Plains and along the coasts, ambushing solitary travelers and raiding homesteads. Deputy Rainer at Westbrook Garrison and Captain Danuvin at Sentinel Hill rely heavily on mercenary blades to cull their numbers, issuing standing bounties for painted armbands and severed paws. Beyond petty ambushes, the Riverpaws exhibit an unusual drive for conquest and labor. They drove a clan of kobolds out of the Redridge Mountains and forced them into the gold mines of Elwynn Forest and Westfall, working the small creatures into relentless exhaustion to extract ore. This cruel tyranny over the mines suggests the gnolls are no longer simple scavengers living hand-to-mouth, but a faction amassing wealth and consolidating strategic ground right under the crown's nose.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_riverpaw-pack-ad345f99e718f8d5e611/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.9608163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:royal-apothecary-society"] = {
  hash = "f64484",
  fullHash = "f64484",
  text = "Royal Apothecary Society. The Royal Apothecary Society is an alchemical guild established under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and based in the Apothecarium beneath the Undercity. Composed of Forsaken researchers, its primary mission is compounding a lethal New Plague capable of eradicating the Scourge while claiming vengeance against the living. While Horde allies believe the society seeks a cure for undeath, its researchers actively harvest venoms, slimes, and toxic reagents across Azeroth to perfect weapons of mass destruction. Founded in the wake of the Forsaken's liberation, the Royal Apothecary Society serves as the scientific and alchemical arm of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom. From their vats and subterranean laboratories in the Apothecarium, researchers seek to weaponize the very force that damned them. Their preeminent endeavor is the creation of a 'New Plague'—a concoction intended to eradicate the Scourge and destroy the Lich King. Unlike traditional human alchemy, which prioritizes elixirs of healing or transmutation, the Society experiments ruthlessly with foul pathogens, caustic acids, and necromantic blends, raising fallen warhorses as skeletal steeds and brewing deadlier strains of toxic blight. The reach of the Apothecary Society extends far beyond the canals of the Undercity. Field apothecaries are dispatched to remote outposts across Lordaeron and Kalimdor. In Silverpine and Hillsbrad, agents like Apothecaries Renferrel and Lydon gather local floras and distill venoms. In the arid Barrens, Apothecary Helbrim catalogs strange desert flora and creatures, while Apothecary Zamah operates beneath Thunder Bluff in the Pools of Vision. Wherever natural toxins, shifting oozes, or virulent organisms exist, the Society sends acolytes to collect samples for the Apothecarium's cauldrons.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_royal-apothecary-society-556eb24c2064d730cc03/1-0000.mp3", duration = 106.866939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:ruins-of-gilneas"] = {
  hash = "83d396",
  fullHash = "83d396",
  text = "Gilneas. Gilneas is a secluded human kingdom occupying a rugged, storm-lashed peninsula south of Silverpine Forest. Following the Second War, King Genn Greymane severed ties with the outside world and locked his nation behind the colossal Greymane Wall, leaving neighboring territories abandoned and the kingdom shrouded in total isolation. Gilneas was once an assertive maritime powerhouse among the human realms, claiming lineage from the ancient empire of Arathor. King Genn Greymane participated reluctantly in the Second War, believing his kingdom could defend itself without foreign entanglements. Bitter over the loss of Gilnean lives and unwilling to help finance the internment camps for captured orcs, Greymane severed relations with the Alliance of Lordaeron. To permanently shield his realm from outside calamity, he ordered the construction of a massive stone barricade along the kingdom's northern frontier. Erecting the Greymane Wall demanded painful geopolitical compromises. The natural mountain passes used to anchor the ramparts left territories such as Ambermill and Pyrewood Village cut off on the northern side, stranded outside the gate. When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron during the Third War, King Greymane refused entreaties for aid and barred the doors to human refugees, leaving the surrounding villages to face the plague and the undead alone.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_ruins-of-gilneas-7639dc8fa8b47eab964a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.2179592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:sargeras"] = {
  hash = "ca94fb",
  fullHash = "ca94fb",
  text = "Sargeras. Sargeras is the fallen titan and supreme creator of the Burning Legion, bent on scouring all life from the cosmos. Once the noble champion and defender of the Pantheon, his descent into madness led to the Sundering of ancient Azeroth and nearly every demonic catastrophe since. Before his fall, Sargeras was the greatest and noblest warrior among the titans. Entrusted by the Pantheon of Order to safeguard their grand creations across the Great Dark Beyond, he waged war against the chaotic demons spawned from the Twisting Nether. Incapable of conceiving pure evil, Sargeras trapped countless fiends away within a great prison. Yet eons of relentless warfare against cosmic horrors gradually wore down his altruistic spirit. Over eons of battle, Sargeras's faith in the titans' work eroded. Despairing at the evil he faced, he concluded that creation was fundamentally flawed and that order could never truly prevail. Turning his back on the Pantheon, he shattered the demons' prison, embraced the chaotic fel fire he had once fought, and marshaled the liberated demons into the unstoppable Burning Legion. Ten thousand years ago, the reckless magic of Queen Azshara and her Highborne reached across the stars, alerting Sargeras to Azeroth's existence. Whispering through the depths of the Well of Eternity, the Dark Titan seduced Azshara into opening a portal for his vanguard. Though that ancient invasion culminated in the catastrophic Sundering, Sargeras never ceased scheming to consume Azeroth—orchestrating the opening of the Dark Portal and guiding the demonic corruption of the orcish Horde.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_sargeras-2cfd6228d901505d4e2a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.3469388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:satyr"] = {
  hash = "b238d0",
  fullHash = "b238d0",
  text = "Satyr. Satyrs are twisted, caprine demons born from the curse of the Highborne sorcerer Xavius during the ancient War of the Ancients. Once night elves, they were warped by Sargeras into horned, hooved beasts dedicated to spreading fel corruption, tormenting their former kin, and furthering the Burning Legion's will. Across the wilds of Kalimdor, scattered satyr sects corrupt pristine woodlands, desecrate moonwells, and subvert vulnerable mortal cults. Ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients, the Highborne councilor Xavius failed to keep the Burning Legion's initial gateway stable against Malfurion Stormrage. Enraged by this blunder, Sargeras dismantled Xavius's spirit and rebuilt him as a punishment and tool: his torso retained elven features, but he gained wicked horns, taloned claws, a leonine tail, and the cloven lower body of a goat. Sent back into Zin-Azshari, Xavius gleefully passed his fel affliction to other power-hungry Highborne. Though Xavius was struck down, his demonic progeny survived, continually seeking to reproduce their kind by corrupting others. Following the Great Sundering, the surviving satyrs continued to menace the shattered night elf civilization. Over the ages they were broken and driven off, their remnants fracturing into scattered sects that linger in dark forests and ancient ruins while harboring deep hatred for their kaldorei cousins. Today, satyr clans inhabit deep woodland enclaves across Kalimdor, from Darkshore and Ashenvale to Felwood, Azshara, and Feralas. They feed directly on the land's pure magic, leeching and spoiling nature until their hooves swell with drained arcane energy. Satyrs frequently turn peaceful furbolg tribes violent, taint ancient moonwells, and serve as covert liaisons for the Shadow Council and the Burning Blade. While some work alongside Twilight's Hammer cultists or hide within ancient ruins like Dire Maul, all remain bound to the shadow of the Burning Legion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_satyr-4fee9cb0d6a0a8e5eefb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 117.733878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:scourge"] = {
  hash = "c03797",
  fullHash = "c03797",
  text = "Scourge. The Scourge is an apocalyptic army of undead monsters, necromancers, and enslaved spirits bound to the will of the Lich King. Originally created by the Burning Legion to destabilize Azeroth ahead of the Third War, the legion of walking corpses broke free from demonic control and shattered the kingdoms of Lordaeron and Quel'Thalas. Today, they wage unending war from their arctic stronghold in Northrend and their corrupt dominion over the Plaguelands. The Scourge was born as an instrument of destruction forged by the Burning Legion. The demon lords bound the spirit of the fallen orc shaman Ner'zhul to the Frozen Throne in distant Northrend, fashioning him into the Lich King. With terrifying telepathic power, he was commanded to prepare Azeroth for conquest. The Lich King unleashed the Plague of Undeath southward into the unsuspecting human realm of Lordaeron, relying on the mortal traitors of the Cult of the Damned to poison grain supplies. Those who perished arose not as restful dead, but as twisted thralls entirely subservient to the Lich King's iron mind. The Scourge achieved total devastation when Prince Arthas Menethil fell to the Lich King's whispers. Seeking vengeance in Northrend, Arthas claimed the cursed blade Frostmourne and forfeited his soul, becoming the Scourge's paramount death knight. Returning home, Arthas slew his father King Terenas, butchered Capital City, and unleashed the undead horde across Lordaeron. The Scourge swept through towns like Darrowshire and Andorhal, butchered the Silver Hand paladins, destroyed Dalaran to summon Archimonde, and carved a dead scar through Quel'Thalas to resurrect Kel'Thuzad as a lich. The Lich King never intended to serve the Burning Legion permanently. Through Arthas, Ner'zhul secretly engineered the Legion's defeat by goading Illidan Stormrage into slaying the dreadlord Tichondrius in Felwood. Enraged, the demon lord Kil'jaeden later ordered Illidan to obliterate the Frozen Throne. Illidan's assault fractured the icy prison, causing the Lich King's mental grip over his minions to waver. Seizing on this weakened control, a faction of free-willed undead led by the resurrected banshee Sylvanas Windrunner broke away, christened themselves the Forsaken, and seized the ruins of Lordaeron. Though weakened by the fracturing of the Frozen Throne and the rebellion of the Forsaken, the Scourge remains the deadliest force in the northern world. Led in the field by the lich Kel'Thuzad, its abominations, crypt fiends, and necromancers dominate the corrupted Eastweald and Westweald, now known as the Plaguelands. From grim bastions and blighted fields, they continuously wage war against the Forsaken of the Undercity, the fanatic zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, and the remaining enclaves of the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_scourge-fa6539b25ae05412628f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 160.10449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:second-war"] = {
  hash = "e9dc82",
  fullHash = "e9dc82",
  text = "Second War. The Second War was the continent-spanning clash across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the newly forged Alliance of Lordaeron. Sparked after the sack of Stormwind, the war devastated Khaz Modan and northern human realms before turning at the gates of Capital City. It ended in the destruction of the Dark Portal and the dispersal of the old Horde. Following the fall of Stormwind during the First War, Regent Anduin Lothar led human refugees north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. King Terenas Menethil II convened the Council of Seven Nations, forging the Alliance of Lordaeron alongside the high elves of Quel'Thalas, the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, and the gnomes of Gnomeregan. Meanwhile, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer consolidated the Horde, forging alliances with forest troll tribes and the Steamwheedle Cartel while subjugating red dragons through the Dragonmaw clan to invade the northern continents. The Horde launched devastating land and naval offensives, quickly overrunning Khaz Modan and driving the dwarves and gnomes behind the sealed gates of Ironforge and Gnomeregan. Advancing north across the Thandol Span and through the seas, orcish forces burned their way through Hillsbrad and the Hinterlands before laying siege to Capital City itself. The kingdom of Alterac betrayed the Alliance, guiding orc divisions through mountain passes. Despite devastating dragon attacks, the Alliance rallied around the newly created Knights of the Silver Hand and paladins who wielded the Holy Light against orcish warlocks and death knights. The war turned when the warlock Gul'dan deserted the Horde with his followers to pursue personal power, forcing Doomhammer to divert critical forces from the front lines. The Alliance counterattacked, breaking the siege of Lordaeron and systematically driving the Horde back south across the Thandol Span, liberating Khaz Modan along the way. In the final mountain passes of the Burning Steppes, Anduin Lothar fell in single combat against Doomhammer, but his lieutenant Turalyon rallied the troops and routed the orcs. The Alliance pursued the survivors all the way to the Black Morass, shattering the remnants and demolishing the Dark Portal.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_second-war-448822d9ff174bbe32d0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 123.08898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:sentinels"] = {
  hash = "65e5e1",
  fullHash = "65e5e1",
  text = "Sentinels. The Sentinels are the elite, predominantly female military force of the night elves, formed ten thousand years ago after the War of the Ancients. Guided by the Sisterhood of Elune and commanded by General Shandris Feathermoon, they guard kaldorei settlements and defend sacred lands from Ashenvale to Feralas. Today, they wage a bitter war against Horde deforestation while watching over their new home on Teldrassil. Before the Great Sundering, the military of the ancient Kaldorei Empire had devolved into a decadent hierarchy where rank was decided by noble lineage rather than martial skill. The devastation of the War of the Ancients proved that this aristocratic structure was utterly unfit to defend Azeroth. In the aftermath, High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind spearheaded radical reforms across night elf society. She turned to the Sisterhood of Elune, an order that had survived intact and possessed extensive experience in tactics, combat, and healing. From its devout ranks, Tyrande forged the Sentinels: a standing martial army dedicated to selflessly protecting their emerging society without regard to noble heritage. While Tyrande Whisperwind remains the spiritual supreme commander, active military leadership belongs to her lieutenant, General Shandris Feathermoon, operating out of Feathermoon Stronghold in Feralas. The Sentinels are renowned for disciplined marksmanship, deadly moonglaives, and specialized divisions. Their forces consist of elite archers, nightsaber-riding huntresses, and scouting hippogryph riders who dominate the skies. Centuries before the Third War, they fought under Archdruid Fandral Staghelm during the brutal War of the Shifting Sands in Silithus, halting the qiraji swarm. Every night elf settlement—from Astranaar to the sprawling branches of Darnassus—relies upon Sentinel garrisons for security. During the Third War, the Sentinels mounted desperate defenses against both demonic invaders and early incursions by the Horde, suffering immense losses when Cenarius fell in Ashenvale. After surviving the Battle of Mount Hyjal and pursuing Illidan across Lordaeron, they returned to find Kalimdor irrevocably changed. Today, the Sentinels face peril on multiple fronts. On Teldrassil, garrisons contend with corrupted wildlife and enraged furbolgs. Farther south, the Silverwing Sentinels clash relentlessly with the Warsong Outriders in Warsong Gulch, resolved to drive the Horde out of Ashenvale and defend the ancient forests from further exploitation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_sentinels-757c1760c1903f88d959/1-0000.mp3", duration = 149.315918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:skyborne"] = {
  hash = "49d276",
  fullHash = "3a4922",
  text = "Skyborne. The Skyborne, or shen'dorei, are an offshoot of high elves descended from rebels who fled Eldre'Thalas after opposing Prince Tortheldrin's demonic pacts. Striking a desperate bargain with wind spirits, their ancestors escaped Azeroth to dwell atop the floating Zephras Isle within Skywall. Today, with their elemental benefactors abruptly gone, their floating sanctuary faces internal fractures and physical collapse. Following the Sundering, the Shen'dralar of Eldre'Thalas languished without the Well of Eternity. Prince Tortheldrin devised a dark salvation: summon the demon Immol'thar and siphon its power. Horrified nobles and citizens resisted, fearing another Legion invasion. In response, Tortheldrin executed a bloody purge of dozens of houses. From the surviving shadows rose a resistance spearheaded by a masked figure known only as the Shal'nan ('the Veiled One'). When a desperate rebel raid on Tortheldrin's ritual sanctum descended into catastrophic chaotic magic, the Shal'nan rallied the survivors and fled west to the port of Eldre'narr, taking to the seas pursued by the Prince's fleet. Cornered on the open ocean, the exiles forged a pact with primordial wind spirits. These beings lifted Zephras Isle straight into the skies, anchoring it safely within Skywall—the Elemental Plane of Air. Relieved of absolute rule, the Shal'nan stepped down during the consecration of the Shrine of the Four Winds, placing governance in the hands of an elected council. Over millennia in this alien realm, the night elf exiles evolved into fair, slender high elves. In recent generations, an elemental influence known as the Sky-Touched Blessing has emerged, causing a growing number of children to be born with pale blue skin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_skyborne-78972f90c065735f115b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:skywall"] = {
  hash = "bf4c98",
  fullHash = "bf4c98",
  text = "Skywall. Skywall is the vast, storm-swept domain of the air elementals within the Elemental Plane. Banished here by the titans in ancient days, the creatures of wind, cloud, and lightning dwell under the reign of Al'Akir the Windlord. When the titans overthrew the Old Gods eons ago, they crafted the Elemental Plane as a secure dimensional prison to contain Azeroth's unruly elemental spirits. The lords of the elements fought fiercely amongst themselves, ultimately carving out distinct dominions. The realm of wind and storm became known as Skywall, ruled by Al'Akir the Windlord. While sealed away from the surface world, its tempests continue to swirl in eternal isolation. Banishment did not end the ancient animosity between the elemental rulers. Skywall has long waged border disputes against neighboring realms like the Firelands. One of the most infamous clashes was the Elemental Sundering, wherein Prince Thunderaan the Windseeker was ambushed and betrayed by Garr and Baron Geddon, the chief lieutenants of Ragnaros the Firelord. Ragnaros devoured the prince's essence and bound the fragments into a shattered talisman. Skywall is not composed solely of raging gales and djinn citadels. Among the cloudbanks drifts Zephras Isle, a sanctuary inhabited by the Shen'dorei. Mortals on Azeroth rarely pierce the veil into the domain of air, though reckless spellcasters and weapon-crafters across history have courted disaster attempting to siphon the living storms of the Windlord.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_skywall-d038783591351050b3ed/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.1257143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:steamwheedle-cartel"] = {
  hash = "75fdc9",
  fullHash = "75fdc9",
  text = "Steamwheedle Cartel. The Steamwheedle Cartel is the largest and most influential goblin enterprise operating across modern Azeroth. Fiercely neutral, the cartel governs bustling commercial hubs like Booty Bay, Ratchet, and Gadgetzan, serving Alliance and Horde patrons alike so long as their coin is genuine. Their sprawling operations encompass cross-continental shipping, alchemy, salvaging, and cutthroat mercenary contracting. Originating from the subterranean goblin capital of Undermine, the Steamwheedle Cartel initially allied with the Old Horde during the Second War, supplying war machines, sappers, and naval engineering. After the dust settled, the cartel's leaders realized that picking sides cost more than it earned. Embracing absolute neutrality, they expanded across both continents, opening trade networks that welcome Alliance and Horde alike. Today, as open tensions simmer, Steamwheedle ports provide vital common ground where anyone with gold can purchase passage, bank resources, and hire local specialists. The cartel's influence rests on strategic ports and frontier towns. In Stranglethorn Vale, Baron Revilgaz presides over Booty Bay, a fortified harbor town carved into the cliffs alongside the Blackwater Raiders. Across the Great Sea, Chief Engineer Gazlowe directs Ratchet on the coast of the Barrens, running a deep-water ferry line directly back to Booty Bay. Deep within the deserts of Tanaris lies the boomtown of Gadgetzan, while smaller operations like Steamwheedle Port and remote outposts in Desolace, Azshara, and Stonetalon Mountains secure regional resources. Steamwheedle trade is constantly imperiled by opportunistic cutthroats. In the Southern Seas, the Bloodsail Buccaneers continually menace Booty Bay's naval lanes, while Southsea Freebooters raid the coastlines near Ratchet. On land, the cartel frequently clashes with their corporate arch-rivals, the Venture Company, competing violently for timber rights, oil fields, and shredder technology across Stonetalon and the Barrens. To maintain order and enforce trade contracts, cartel barons routinely hire passing mercenaries to settle grievances with extreme prejudice.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_steamwheedle-cartel-f579d24b90ca933657fe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 130.507755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:stonefield-family"] = {
  hash = "a17be1",
  fullHash = "a17be1",
  text = "Stonefield family. The Stonefield family is a lineage of human farmers residing at the Stonefield Farm in western Elwynn Forest. Known for their long-standing rural roots, they tend their homestead while caught up in an ongoing rivalry with their neighboring peers, the Maclures. Despite the hostility between the two households, younger generations find themselves entangled across the family divide. The Stonefields are quintessential Elwynn agrarian stock, working the soil south of the road leading to Westfall. The homestead revolves around Gramma Mildred Stonefield, Ma Stonefield, and young Tommy Joe Stonefield. Daily life on the farm involves tending crops and managing livestock while fending off local pests and dealing with nearby kobold incursions that plague the region's mines and borders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_stonefield-family-f7124bd2323275a7a8ae/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.8595918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:stormwind-army"] = {
  hash = "494a3c",
  fullHash = "494a3c",
  text = "Stormwind Army. The Stormwind Army is the standing military force charged with defending the Kingdom of Stormwind and preserving the security of the Grand Alliance in the south. Though legendary for its resilient knights and marshals, the army is currently crippled by royal absence, political infighting, and crippling troop shortages across the provinces. The army traces its martial lineage through centuries of brutal conflict, from early frontier clashes against gnolls to the desperate defense against the Gurubashi trolls. In the First War, under King Llane Wrynn I and Sir Anduin Lothar, the Knights of Stormwind mounted legendary ambushes against the Orcish Horde, yet fell when the capital burned. Surviving remnants evacuated to Lordaeron, spearheading the formation of the Alliance of Lordaeron and fighting through the Second War. After the Dark Portal's fall, Stormwind's military was rebuilt alongside the restored kingdom. Headquartered within Stormwind Keep, the military relies on dedicated regional garrisons overseen by the Stormwind Marshals. Within the stone walls of the capital, the Stormwind City Guard maintains civil order, while the elite Royal Guard attends the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. Beyond the gates, the army deploys outposts to patrol Elwynn Forest, keep order along trade roads, and man defensive ramparts as far as the Redridge Mountains, assisted on the water by the Stormwind Fleet.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_stormwind-army-5c288e61007ece31e4ea/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.6473469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:stormwind-guard"] = {
  hash = "812171",
  fullHash = "812171",
  text = "Stormwind Guard. The Stormwind Guard comprises the regional garrisons and sentries tasked with enforcing the kingdom's laws and defending its surrounding territories. Stretched perilously thin by political gridlock and distant military campaigns, its scattered marshals struggle to hold back gnolls, orcs, and bandits across Elwynn and Redridge. Operating as a vital branch of the Stormwind Army, the Stormwind Guard patrols the highways, crossroads, and stone watchtowers that bind the kingdom together. In outlying settlements, governance and defense fall to appointed marshals—such as Marshal McBride in Northshire Valley, Marshal Dughan in Goldshire, and Marshal Marris in Lakeshire. These officers muster recruits and maintain order, relying on trusty brown horses known for stamina to cover ground between fortified outposts like Westbrook Garrison. Following the Third War, the Guard successfully kept Elwynn Forest safe and quiet, but recent years have brought crisis. With King Varian Wrynn vanished and the kingdom's standing army tied down abroad, regional garrisons have received little aid. At Westbrook Garrison, Deputy Rainer can barely hold the western border against Riverpaw gnolls and Defias thieves, forcing local commanders to outsource crucial security tasks and bounties to visiting adventurers. While Duskwood and Westfall were abandoned, the crown kept a tenuous military presence in the Redridge Mountains. Even so, the guard's position is desperate. Overwhelmed by Shadowhide gnolls and Blackrock orcs descending from the Burning Steppes, soldiers were forced to cede Stonewatch Keep. In Lakeshire, blacksmith Verner Osgood burns through iron merely keeping guards outfitted, while exhausted sentries scan the road waiting for a 2nd Battalion that never arrives.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_stormwind-guard-f7b9ac77b48fa1cb91c2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.724898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:tauren"] = {
  hash = "0703f2",
  fullHash = "0703f2",
  text = "Tauren. The tauren, or shu'halo in their native tongue, are a noble race of nomadic bovine humanoids native to central Kalimdor. Deeply devoted to the Earth Mother, shamanism, and the natural world, they were driven near extinction by ruthless centaur clans before forging a bond with Thrall's New Horde. Today, united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, they have established a permanent home across the verdant plains of Mulgore and the high rises of Thunder Bluff. For countless generations, the tauren lived as seasonal nomads wandering the arid plains of the Barrens, hunting the great kodo and listening for the voice of the Earth Mother. However, their peaceful wanderings were plagued by centuries of brutal bloodshed. The ferocious centaur tribes, born from the tragic union of Zaetar and Princess Theradras, waged an unrelenting war of extermination against them. Lacking a fortified homeland and reluctant to wage endless war, the tauren tribes were pushed to the brink of annihilation, their very survival hanging by a thread. Survival came from an unexpected alliance across the sea. During the Third War, Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof encountered Warchief Thrall and his newly arrived orcs. Recognizing a shared spirit of honor and a longing for shamanic heritage, the two leaders joined forces. With the Horde's assistance, the tauren crushed the marauding centaur and claimed the lush, sheltered grasslands of Mulgore. Upon its windswept mesas, Cairne raised the city of Thunder Bluff. Indebted by this salvation, the tauren marched alongside the Horde to Mount Hyjal to turn back the Burning Legion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_tauren-14a63f8de967f0a90706/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.242449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:terenas-menethil-ii"] = {
  hash = "b1e474",
  fullHash = "b1e474",
  text = "Terenas Menethil II. Across Lordaeron, Terenas Menethil II carries the weight of a kingdom through war and its aftermath. For more than five decades, he ruled as a wise and diplomatic king, forged the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War, and gave shelter to the young Prince Varian Wrynn. His murder by his corrupted son, Prince Arthas, began Lordaeron’s collapse to the Scourge. When refugees from Stormwind reached the north with Sir Anduin Lothar, King Terenas Menethil II answered their call. He gathered the monarchs of the divided human kingdoms, along with envoys from Ironforge and Quel'Thalas, and brought them together in the Alliance of Lordaeron. Terenas did not claim command of its armies. He chose Lothar as supreme commander, uniting humanity’s kingdoms for the first time in millennia. Together, they defeated the Old Horde. After the Second War, Terenas insisted that surviving orcs be held in internment camps rather than executed. He believed peace might hold once their demonic fury faded. The camps placed a heavy tax burden on Lordaeron’s allies. Gilneas shut itself behind the Greymane Wall, Stromgarde left the Alliance over political disputes, and the high elves withdrew entirely. Terenas was left to bear the cost as the coalition fractured.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_terenas-menethil-ii-6903e39434ad358971e6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.1804082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:third-war"] = {
  hash = "ed367f",
  fullHash = "ed367f",
  text = "Third War. The Third War was the apocalyptic conflict that shattered the old world order just a few years ago. Orchestrated by the Burning Legion and unleashed through the Lich King's Plague of Undeath, the war saw the fall of the Kingdom of Lordaeron and the near-total destruction of Quel'Thalas before culminating in a desperate, unified stand at Mount Hyjal. The war began subtly in northern Lordaeron, disguised as a virulent sickness carried in infected grain distributed from Andorhal. The tainted harvest did not merely slaughter its victims; it raised them as ravenous ghouls bound to the Lich King's will. Prince Arthas Menethil raced to stem the contagion alongside the Kirin Tor sorceress Jaina Proudmoore. Blinded by grief, fury, and dreadlord manipulations, Arthas purged the infected population of Stratholme before pursuing his foe to the frozen waste of Northrend. There, he took up the cursed runeblade Frostmourne, casting aside his soul. He returned south only to murder his father, King Terenas, delivering Lordaeron into the hands of the Scourge. Empowered by death, Arthas crushed the high elven realm of Quel'Thalas to resurrect the necromancer Kel'Thuzad at the Sunwell. The undead army then laid siege to Dalaran, seizing the spellbooks necessary to summon the demon lord Archimonde into Azeroth. With the commander of the Burning Legion walking mortal soil, Dalaran was leveled in an arcane display of devastation. The Scourge had served its purpose as an advance guard, and the Burning Legion turned its sights toward the eternal power resting across the Great Sea. Guided by cryptic warnings from the mysterious Prophet—later revealed as the redeemed Guardian Medivh—Warchief Thrall led the New Horde across the Great Sea, while Lady Jaina Proudmoore evacuated surviving Alliance refugees. In ancient Kalimdor, the newcomers clashed with the reclusive night elves before all three factions recognized the supreme threat looming over them. Beneath the World Tree Nordrassil atop Mount Hyjal, mortals stood together against impossible odds. Archimonde was destroyed by ancestral wisps when he reached the tree, shattering the Legion's vanguard and ending the war. Although Archimonde fell, the peace of 25 ADP is fragile and stained with loss. The Alliance of Lordaeron is gone, leaving the kingdom of Stormwind and Ironforge to anchor the southern Alliance while Lordaeron sits rotting as the Plaguelands. In Kalimdor, Thrall's Horde carved out Durotar, yet tensions with the newly allied night elves remain volatile. The Scourge endures in the northern ruins, and the freed undead—the Forsaken—now hold the catacombs of Capital City, waging a war of survival against both former masters and vengeful crusaders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_third-war-1eecca13e927f35b623a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 163.448163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:titan"] = {
  hash = "06a5fd",
  fullHash = "06a5fd",
  text = "Titan. The titans are a primordial race of colossal, godlike beings who wander the cosmos bringing order and shaping worlds. In ancient antiquity, they came to Azeroth to vanquish the chaotic Old Gods, establish massive terrestrial facilities, and foster life before departing into the Great Dark Beyond. The titans are cosmic giants forged from primordial matter and arcane magic, embodying the forces of order and creation. In their journeys across the Great Dark Beyond, they sought out slumbering worlds to cultivate, raising up continents, calming chaotic elemental forces, and designing life. To enforce balance, they established powerful constructs made of stone, iron, and sea to watch over their works. Long ago, the Pantheon of titans discovered Azeroth in the grip of the chaotic elemental lords and the horrific Old Gods. The titans waged war against these parasitic horrors, banishing the elementals to pocket planes and sealing the monstrous Old Gods deep beneath the crust of the earth. Upon securing the world, they carved great biomes, established vault complexes like Uldaman, and entrusted five great dragons—the Dragon Aspects—with stewardship of the land, magic, time, and life itself before journeying back among the stars. Not all titans remained aligned with the Pantheon's vision of perfect order. Sargeras, once the greatest warrior and defender of the titans, grew despondent and terrified by the deep cosmic darkness and demonic malevolence he was charged with combating. Rejecting the work of his kin, Sargeras turned to nihilism, founding the demonic Burning Legion to extinguish all creation rather than allow it to be corrupted.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_titan-b3106b3e085301e754ad/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.851429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:trogg"] = {
  hash = "4eee46",
  fullHash = "4eee46",
  text = "Trogg. Troggs are brutish, subterranean humanoids descended from the earliest, failed titan-forged experiments sealed deep within the earth. Having recently clawed their way to the surface after dwarven excavations disturbed their ancient vaults, these relentless diggers have overrun dwarven quarries and driven the gnomes out of their beloved capital of Gnomeregan. Long before mortal races recorded history, the titan-forged keepers sought to fashion servants from stone. Their initial design went awry, yielding stone-skinned brutes of low intelligence and unbridled savagery. Distressed by their botched creations, the keepers refined the matrix into the earthen race, who would eventually become the ancestors of the dwarves. Unwilling to annihilate the imperfect troggs, the giantess Ironaya sealed them deep underground within the vault of Uldaman, intending for them to remain trapped in eternal stasis. For countless millennia, the troggs lay dormant until the Explorers' League of Ironforge breached the ancient subterranean complexes of Khaz Modan. Waking in an unfamiliar era, swarms of furious troggs poured from the depths, pursuing their distant dwarven relatives with ancestral fury. Finding the surface bright and unwelcoming, they carved sprawling tunnel networks beneath Dun Morogh, Loch Modan, and the Badlands, quickly ambushing quarries, archaeological dig sites, and trade outposts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_trogg-1c9467472911f0ff6762/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.2783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:troll"] = {
  hash = "794291",
  fullHash = "794291",
  text = "Troll. Trolls are among the oldest sentient races native to Azeroth, having arisen around the Well of Eternity long before the Sundering. Renowned for their incredible physical regeneration, worship of primal spirits known as loa, and mastery of voodoo, their fractured empires once ruled vast swaths of the ancient world. Today, while ancient kingdoms like the Gurubashi and Amani remain deeply hostile to outsiders, the Darkspear tribe has found a lasting haven within Thrall's Horde. Long before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, the primal energies of the Well of Eternity nurtured the rise of trollkind. Emerging as savage, highly agile hunter-gatherers, the early trolls possessed a unique physiology that granted them astonishing regenerative powers, allowing them to recover rapidly from grievous wounds and even regrow severed limbs over time. Settling around the sacred mountains of Zandalar, they directed their reverence toward primal spirits they called loa. Under the spiritual guidance of shadow hunters and witch doctors, these rituals coalesced into the practice of voodoo. In ancient times, the trolls waged a long war against the insectoid aqir, whose swarms threatened to overrun the continent. The Amani and Gurubashi empires led the fight and eventually drove the aqir to the far corners of the land: north into what became Azjol-Nerub and south into the deserts where Ahn'Qiraj would rise. With the insect swarms broken and contained, the trolls' common cause dissolved. Each great faction withdrew into its own sovereign empire, developing distinct cultures, physical traits, and tribal hierarchies adapted to forests, jungles, and frozen wastes. Over millennia of separation, rivalry, and territorial wars against younger races, troll dominion slowly decayed into ruin and insularity. Most remaining troll kingdoms view all other races with fierce, unyielding hostility, clinging bitterly to the ruins of their former greatness. Only the exile jungle trolls of the Darkspear tribe have chosen a new path, joining the Horde alongside orcs and tauren.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_troll-804bf264e84cd74ac40d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 122.958367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:twilight-s-hammer"] = {
  hash = "6026ad",
  fullHash = "6026ad",
  text = "Twilight's Hammer. The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to awakening the Old Gods and bringing about the end of Azeroth. Originally an orcish clan led by the ogre-mage Cho'gall, it has evolved into a secretive, multiracial order actively conducting dark rituals across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms. The Twilight's Hammer began on Draenor as an orc clan of the Old Horde, led by the two-headed ogre-mage Cho'gall, one of Gul'dan's chief lieutenants in the Shadow Council. Under Cho'gall the clan embraced a grim, apocalyptic creed, coming to see the Horde as merely an engine of destruction meant to bring about the end of all things. When the Twilight's Hammer crossed the Dark Portal during the First War, their apocalyptic fanaticism only deepened. In the soil of Azeroth, the cultists sensed the genuine whispers of the Old Gods—ancient horrors chained beneath the earth. Blackhand tolerated their erratic, ritualistic behavior solely because Cho'gall managed their bloodlust, deploying them alongside Kilrogg Deadeye's Bleeding Hollow clan in early assaults on Stormwind Keep. The clan later operated deep within Grim Batol during the Second War, overseeing critical refineries until Gul'dan's betrayal fractured the Horde. Though Orgrim Doomhammer ordered the traitorous clan butchered near the Tomb of Sargeras, a core cadre of zealots survived. Upon reaching the forgotten shores of Kalimdor, the cult transformed from a shattered orc clan into a clandestine, multiracial conspiracy. Seducing disillusioned members of both the Alliance and the Horde, the Twilight's Hammer recruits any soul broken enough to welcome oblivion. In northern Kalimdor, led by Twilight Lord Kelris, cultists have entrenched themselves within the submerged halls of Blackfathom Deeps, feeding sacrifices to Aku'mai, a monstrous servant of an Old God. Further north in Darkshore, their excavators disturb the colossal fossilized remains at the Master's Glaive. Across the southern deserts of Silithus, camps of robed fanatics tend abyssal windstones, listening for the stirring of ancient horrors entombed beneath the sands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_twilight-s-hammer-31f4771c19a49cccf67b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 123.95102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:venture-company"] = {
  hash = "015725",
  fullHash = "015725",
  text = "Venture Company. The Venture Company is a notoriously ruthless goblin cartel focused on aggressive resource extraction, heavy industrial machinery, and logging. Unaligned with the major goblin ports like Booty Bay or Ratchet, the cartel strip-mines and clear-cuts territories across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms without regard for local populations or the natural world. Led overall by Mogul Razdunk from afar, the Venture Company operates by a simple corporate philosophy: if it can be chopped down, drilled into, or blasted out of a mountain, they will exploit it. The cartel deploys some of the most advanced heavy machinery on Azeroth, including massive lumber-harvesting shredders, drilling rigs, and industrial refineries. Unlike traditional merchant houses that focus on shipping or retail, the Venture Company specializes in seizing raw resources by force, ignoring treaties, borders, and local sovereign rights.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_venture-company-ed312f48a61456aa60eb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.1665306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:westfall-brigade"] = {
  hash = "ecd942",
  fullHash = "ecd942",
  text = "People's Militia. In Westfall, the People's Militia holds its ground against the Defias Brotherhood. Gryan Stoutmantle founded the volunteer force at Sentinel Hill after Stormwind withdrew its troops. With local farmers and visiting adventurers, they defend the region against banditry. Westfall once fed the Kingdom of Stormwind, but the kingdom withdrew its troops and left the people defenseless. The Defias Brotherhood spread across the plains, seizing family homesteads, taking over iron mines, and extorting local farmers. With no royal reinforcements in sight, veteran warrior Gryan Stoutmantle rallied citizens and retired soldiers to form the People's Militia. Sentinel Tower, atop Sentinel Hill, is the Militia's base. From there, its volunteers patrol the region's main roads with little military gear or steady supplies. Gryan Stoutmantle directs them against the growing ranks of Defias highwaymen, thieves, and pillagers. When Redridge asks for mutual aid, the Militia must refuse. Sending even a handful of men could leave Westfall lost.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_westfall-brigade-fab38a1a5de8366a74e3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.3469388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:worgen"] = {
  hash = "43d6a3",
  fullHash = "43d6a3",
  text = "Worgen. Worgen are fierce, lupine humanoids cursed with uncontrollable primal rage, moving upright yet hunting with bestial swiftness. Once thought to be mere nightmares conjured from other planes, these savage pack-hunters now terrorize Silverpine Forest, Ashenvale, and Duskwood. Long before their recent emergence in Lordaeron and Kalimdor, wolf-men were whispered of in folktales and cautionary fables. Their true origins remain a mystery: scholars who have studied the beasts believe they were drawn into Azeroth from another realm, and both Archmage Arugal and the night elves' Scythe of Elune have proven able to call them forth. During the Third War, as the Scourge hordes overran Lordaeron and slain Kirin Tor wizards rose again as undead, Archmage Arugal sought a weapon to bolster Dalaran's dwindling ranks. Drawing from the forbidden research of the Dalaran wizard Ur, Arugal pierced the veil and summoned the beasts into Silverpine Forest. The summoned worgen devastated the undead invaders with savage abandon, but the archmage could not leash their fury. The beasts turned on the wizards themselves, spreading their curse through fang and claw. Driven mad by guilt and fascination, Arugal adopted the monsters as his children, seizing Baron Silverlaine's ancestral seat—now rechristened Shadowfang Keep—and cursing nearby villagers into monstrous nocturnal servitude.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_worgen-8b8fa124bdb168b20d8f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.3640816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:alterac"] = {
  hash = "b79aea",
  fullHash = "c060a2",
  text = "Alterac Mountains. The Alterac Mountains are a cold, ruined highland in central Lordaeron, once home to the treacherous human kingdom of Alterac and the scholarly magocracy of Dalaran. Today, the snow-dusted crags are overrun by the Crushridge ogres, the aristocratic bandits of the Syndicate, and remnant arcane forces. Travelers venture from nearby foothill settlements and the rebuilding city of Dalaran. During the Second War, Alterac was one of the Seven Kingdoms of humanity, but King Aiden Perenolde betrayed the Alliance of Lordaeron to side with the Old Horde, hoping to preserve his realm. His treachery was discovered and stamped out, fracturing the nation into disputed borderlands. Following the kingdom's dissolution and the horrors of the Third War, Alterac's grand stone capital was reduced to rubble. Today, the Ruins of Alterac are occupied by the brutal Crushridge ogres, led by Mug'thol, while the surviving human nobility organized into the Syndicate, a vicious criminal syndicate fighting to reclaim their lost estates by force. On the western edge of the mountains, along the shores of Lordamere Lake, sits what remains of the Kirin Tor's great city-state of Dalaran. Devastated by the demon lord Archimonde during the Third War, Dalaran's surviving magi threw up a massive, opaque violet dome of protective magic over the ruins, but it has since been removed, and they rebuild the city in the open. The Kirin Tor continue rebuilding their sanctum, and Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver can be found within the Violet Citadel. Adventurers often venture into the peaks using Southshore or Tarren Mill as staging grounds. Beyond the wandering yetis, mountain lions, and Syndicate footpads, the region's greatest conflict churns beneath the snow line. Deep within the mountain passes lies Alterac Valley, where the dwarven Stormpike Guard have clashed with the reclusive Frostwolf clan of orcs over ancient territorial and mining rights.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_alterac-242bd368f72b76eb6411/1-0000.mp3", duration = 112.352653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:arathi"] = {
  hash = "064ca3",
  fullHash = "631e20",
  text = "Arathi Highlands. The Arathi Highlands are a rugged plateau in southeastern Lordaeron, renowned as the ancient birthplace of the Empire of Arathor. Today, the kingdom of Stromgarde lies in ruins, leaving the craggy realm engulfed in multi-front warfare between the remnants of Stromgarde, the Horde, criminal Syndicate nobles, and invading ogres. Millennia ago, King Thoradin united the fractured human tribes under the banner of the Arathi, constructing the majestic capital of Strom across these dry, defensible plains. It was here that humanity forged its legendary compact with the high elves during the Troll Wars, mastering the arcane arts to push back the Amani. Over time, noble bloodlines migrated north into Lordaeron and south toward Stormwind, but the imperial guard stayed behind to form the martial realm of Stromgarde. For ages, Stromgarde stood as a bastion of stalwart warriors dedicated to keeping the forest trolls at bay. Stromgarde proved vital in defending the Alliance during the Second War under King Thoras Trollbane, but the Third War and its aftermath shattered the nation. Although the kingdom spared itself the worst ravages of the Scourge plague, the mysterious death of King Thoras threw the realm into turmoil. Boulderfist ogres swept in from the hills, while the traitorous Alteraci nobles of the Syndicate seized sprawling districts of Stromgarde Keep. Today, Prince Galen Trollbane and his loyalist survivors cling to a tiny sliver of the city and their redoubt at Refuge Pointe, waging a bitter guerrilla war against hopeless odds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_arathi-5cdccb5ae6d8021093ab/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.4171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ashenvale"] = {
  hash = "30d864",
  fullHash = "6d87ac",
  text = "Ashenvale. Ashenvale's sacred woods are now a battleground as the Horde cuts timber for Orgrimmar. The ancient forest lies between the Barrens to the south and Darkshore to the north, under the care of the Sentinels and Cenarius. Its canopy shelters moonwells and old ruins, alongside demonic blights, hostile tribes, and a contested frontier. Beneath Mount Hyjal, Ashenvale has belonged to the kaldorei and the children of Cenarius for millennia. Immense trees form a close canopy, turning the daily rain to mist and leaving the forest floor in twilight. Moss covers the ruins of elven settlements, where walking ancients and dryads keep watch. For thousands of years, the night elves built no true cities here. They hunted lightly and kept the wild domain in balance. The Third War broke Ashenvale's peace when Grommash Hellscream and the Warsong clan entered for lumber. Their demonic bloodlust led them to slay Cenarius. The struggle is now a full territorial war. From Splintertree Post and the southern Mor'shan Rampart, the Warsong Outriders send axes and shredders into the eastern woods to supply Orgrimmar. The Silverwing Sentinels hold Astranaar, a sanctuary town, and have sworn to drive every orcish logger from the sacred grove. The lumber war is only one danger in Ashenvale. The Burning Legion left blighted scars at Felfire Hill and Demon Fall Canyon, where Mannoroth died. Satyrs use ruins such as Satyrnaar and Xavian as bases, corrupting waterways and wildlife. Furbolg tribes, once friends of the elves, have largely fallen to dark madness. Mysterious worgen stalk the northern shrine near Felwood, while cursed dragonkin prowl Bough Shadow and guard a portal into the troubled Emerald Dream.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ashenvale-def297b6fe7e6083037e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 102.530612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:azshara"] = {
  hash = "d0a0f1",
  fullHash = "083fb6",
  text = "Azshara. Azshara is a rugged, windswept coastline in northeastern Kalimdor cloaked in vibrant, perpetual autumn foliage. Once part of the glorious night elven capital before the Great Sundering shattered it into the sea, it remains a dangerous wilderness littered with ancient ruins, brooding naga, and isolated practitioners of magic. Both the Horde and the Alliance maintain precarious outposts along its borders, wary of the restless ghosts and ocean horrors lurking within. Ten thousand years ago, this coast was the beating heart of the kaldorei civilization, surrounding the royal capital of Zin-Azshari. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne court drew the Burning Legion into Azeroth, the resulting War of the Ancients culminated in the catastrophic collapse of the Well of Eternity. The sea thundered inward, swallowing most of the magnificent city and leaving behind fractured cliffs, treacherous coral reefs, and waterlogged spires. The survivors renamed the ruined landscape after their fallen monarch, branding it as a cursed monument to pride and catastrophic betrayal. Azshara is famous for its stunning crimson, orange, and gold canopy, giving the impression of an eternal autumn unbroken by changing seasons. Beneath this tranquil canopy, however, lies an inhospitable expanse. Corrupted satyrs practice dark rituals in forested hollows, lingering Highborne apparitions haunt drowned foundations, and Spitelash naga prowl the shallow reefs of the Coral Sea. Ferocious beasts, giant hydras, and mountain giants roam unchecked across the highlands and ivory beaches. Because of its isolation and harsh terrain, Azshara draws recluse figures pursuing forgotten crafts or watching over dangerous powers. The enigmatic Archmage Xylem inhabits a secluded tower perched high above the cliffs, testing promising spellcasters and cunning rogues alike. Farther along the coast, the demon hunter Loramus Thalipedes watches the waves from a barren isle, while the immense sea giant Lord Arkkoroc demands blood sacrifices from his temple overlooking the churning waters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_azshara-840d61ed3268abdcc7d6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 125.33551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:badlands"] = {
  hash = "9c579d",
  fullHash = "cb90ef",
  text = "Badlands. The Badlands is an arid, sun-baked expanse of cracked earth, dust storms, and rocky canyons nestled between Loch Modan and Searing Gorge. Despite being deep within dwarven Khaz Modan, it serves as a volatile frontier dominated by Dark Iron dwarves, troggs, Dustbelcher ogres, and nesting black dragonkin. It also houses the titan ruins of Uldaman and the Horde stronghold of Kargath, making it a critical strategic waypoint. Wedged south of Loch Modan's verdant hills, the Badlands quickly turns into a sweltering basin of barren flats and rugged ravines. Surviving here requires contending with merciless coyotes, cougars, vultures, and fierce sandstorms that obscure the horizon. Scattered across the plateaus are the crude encampments of the Dustbelcher ogres, while the brooding Dark Iron clan fortifies Angor Fortress and clashes with anyone trespassing on their territory. In the northern bluffs lies Uldaman, an ancient subterranean complex carved out by the titan-forged earthen. Recently uncovered by Ironforge prospectors, the excavation unearthed monumental secrets regarding the ancient ancestry of the dwarven race. However, digging into the subterranean vaults awoke hordes of vicious Stonevault troggs and animated earthen guardians, slaughtering archaeological teams and turning the digsites outside into bloody skirmish grounds. The Badlands is one of the Horde's most precarious footholds in the southern Eastern Kingdoms. Established in the northwest, the fortress of Kargath gives Warchief Thrall's forces a base of operations to keep watch on the rogue Dark Horde in nearby Blackrock Mountain. Meanwhile, to the far east lies Lethlor Ravine, a treacherous chasm where black whelps and dangerous black dragonkin guard their brooding grounds under the shadow of the mountains.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_badlands-3c51ce0f2da798ade43c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 108.930612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:barrens"] = {
  hash = "883834",
  fullHash = "f8b903",
  text = "The Barrens. Across The Barrens, Horde outposts hold the roads while rival forces press in from every side. Once a forest before the Great Sundering, the central Kalimdor plain is now dry country of red dust, scrub, and wandering wildlife. The Crossroads anchors Horde traffic, but quilboar, centaur, and creeping corruption threaten its hold. For generations, tauren nomads hunted kodo and zhevra across the plains and fought a repeating defensive war against Kolkar centaur. During the Third War, Thrall led the orcs to Kalimdor and helped Cairne Bloodhoof drive back the centaur and cross the savanna into Mulgore. The Barrens now links Durotar, Mulgore, and Ashenvale. The Crossroads and Camp Taurajo guard trade along the Gold Road, while orcish homesteads endure the harsh climate as they build a lasting home. Horde control of the savanna remains contested. Razormane quilboar, with their porcupine-like bristles, raid farms and supply lines from thorn-choked strongholds. Razorfen Kraul's dens loom to the south. On the eastern coast, Theramore forces hold Northwatch Hold, while dwarven prospectors dig into the titan complex of Bael Modan. Ratchet's goblins keep an uneasy neutrality in trade, welcoming travelers who bring coin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_barrens-666e7805d5002f1435f9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.2734694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:blackfathom"] = {
  hash = "241f6a",
  fullHash = "049d6d",
  text = "Blackfathom Deeps. Twilight's Hammer cultists now gather in the flooded halls of Blackfathom Deeps, along the Zoram Strand in northwestern Ashenvale. The temple once honored Elune, the night elven moon goddess. Naga and satyrs have joined the cult in veneration of Aku'mai, a primordial hydra and servant of an Old God. Blackfathom Deeps once honored Elune, the night elven moon goddess. Long before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, Highborne masons raised gem-encrusted bridges through its splendid halls. Queen Azshara commanded the waters around the sacred moonwell, keeping salt seas and mountain streams apart. When the Sundering broke the world ten thousand years ago, the temple sank beneath the coastal waters of the Veiled Sea. It lay undisturbed for millennia, until foul whispers began to rise from the darkness below. Twilight's Hammer cultists came to the sunken ruins, drawn by mad dreams and dark portents. Inside, they forged an unholy truce with coastal naga led by Lady Sarevess and prowling satyrs. Together, they desecrated the moonwell and enslaved the creatures nearby. Years of torment drove the giant sea turtle Ghamoo-ra mad. The brutal murloc Gelihast raised a blood shrine, and the caverns filled with fresh sacrifices. Under Twilight Lord Kelris, the cultists made the ruins a staging ground for the Old Gods' impending return.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_blackfathom-1d6adbcf654adbb875db/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.6138776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:blastedlands"] = {
  hash = "7fb091",
  fullHash = "977846",
  text = "Blasted Lands. The Blasted Lands are a desolate expanse of red clay and barren rock at the southeastern tip of the Eastern Kingdoms, ruined by the chaotic energies of the Dark Portal. Once part of the verdant Black Morass, the shattered earth now harbors demonic remnants of the Burning Legion, brutal Dreadmaul ogres, and hardy desert beasts. Nethergarde Keep stands vigil over the desolate wasteland, guarding Azeroth from whatever horrors may spill out of the dormant gateway. Before the First War, this region was a continuation of the dense fenlands known as the Black Morass. That lush swamp was utterly eradicated when the warlock Gul'dan and the possessed Medivh tore open the Dark Portal to unleash the orcish Horde. Chaotic fel energies bled through the dimensional breach, stripping all moisture and baking the earth into cracked red clay. When the Dark Portal was later destroyed at the end of the Second War, catastrophic shockwaves scorched the terrain further, transmuting the native stone into its unearthly crimson hue. Perched upon the northern bluffs, Nethergarde Keep watches over the only road entering the valley. Commissioned by Archmage Khadgar and the Kirin Tor following the Second War, this stone bastion is manned by grim mages and stalwart soldiers sworn to prevent any renewed demonic or orcish incursions. It is an uninviting and strictly disciplined posting; infractions among the garrison are punished with banishment to the Stockade in Stormwind. The defenders face relentless pressure from the Dreadmaul ogres who stayed behind after Draenor's fall, raiding the roads and attacking the fortress gates under fell guidance. In the southwestern reaches lies the Tainted Scar, a cratered canyon teeming with the deadliest demonic force on the continent. Stranded after the defeat at Mount Hyjal, the doomguard commander Lord Kazzak gathers wandering demons, doomguard, and felhounds to his banner. High above the red barrens, his dreadlord lieutenant, Razelikh the Defiler, orchestrates mortal servitude among the Shadowsworn cultists at the Altar of Storms and commands the local Dreadmaul ogres. Despite the land's desolation, scholars and wanderers brave its horrors for unique discoveries. Just inside the northern pass, Bloodmage Drazial and Bloodmage Lynnore conduct eccentric alchemical experiments, extracting organs from local scorpids, basilisks, and boars to brew extraordinarily potent physical enhancers. Farther into the wastes, a lone draenei hermit named Kum'isha seeks scattered draenethyst fragments, rewarding travelers who recover these alien remnants left behind by Draenor's shattered past.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_blastedlands-2be885f3ff541153e6eb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 152.52898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:burningsteppes"] = {
  hash = "a115b0",
  fullHash = "f70df7",
  text = "Burning Steppes. The Burning Steppes are a scorched, volcanic wasteland flanking the southern base of Blackrock Mountain. Formed by the catastrophic summoning of Ragnaros centuries ago, this treacherous territory holds the primary overland passage between Stormwind and Khaz Modan. Today, it crawls with rogue Blackrock orcs, Firegut ogres, Dark Iron dwarves, and broodlings of the black dragonflight. Long ago, these foothills were simply the northern reaches of the Redridge Mountains. During the War of the Three Hammers, the exiled Dark Iron clan established their kingdom here, raising their capital city of Thaurissan. Facing total defeat at the hands of the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans, the desperate Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan attempted to conjure elemental forces. Instead, he shattered the earth and summoned Ragnaros the Firelord into Azeroth. The violent explosion pulverized the surrounding mountains, tore open rivers of lava, and birthed the colossal volcano known as Blackrock Mountain, leaving the steppes forever charred and barren. During the Second War, the Old Horde claimed Blackrock Spire as its foremost seat of power. At the climax of the conflict, the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron pushed deep into the Burning Steppes to lay siege to the mountain. It was at the foot of Blackrock Mountain that Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer clashed with Lord Anduin Lothar, striking down the human champion. Rather than breaking the Alliance's resolve, Lothar's death galvanized the Allied armies to rout the orcs, sealing the victory of the Second War and leaving behind an enduring valley of ash and rusted steel. Though Thrall established a New Horde in distant Kalimdor, the Blackrock clan remaining in the steppes maintains the bloodthirsty cruelty of the Old Horde. Operating from Blackrock Stronghold, these orcs openly ally with the black dragonflight and local Firegut ogres rather than honor any truce. Meanwhile, Dark Iron dwarves and elemental servitors lurk among the fiery ruins, all while black drakes and wyrmkin prey upon unwary travelers attempting to use the thoroughfare to cross between Stormwind and the northern kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_burningsteppes-30125d11008e0c3ac0bc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 124.003265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:darkshore"] = {
  hash = "3b8af3",
  fullHash = "47e9e4",
  text = "Darkshore. In Darkshore, Auberdine links night elf lands to the wider Alliance. This cold, misty coast in northwestern Kalimdor is the main gateway for travelers from Teldrassil and the Eastern Kingdoms. Ancient ruins and troubled wildlife mark the land, while cultists, naga, and a creeping rot threaten it. Darkshore stretches beneath the western shadows of Mount Hyjal and Felwood, where pine groves meet fog and cold, rocky shores. Millennia ago, the Kaldorei cities of Ameth'Aran and Bashal'Aran flourished along this coast. The Great Sundering shattered them, leaving pillars and arches half-drowned or tangled in roots. Highborne spirits haunt the ruins, while scavenging murlocs and invading naga range among them. After the Third War and the planting of Teldrassil, the night elves made Auberdine a busy maritime haven in the wilderness. Ships arrive from Rut'theran Village, Stormwind Harbor, and Menethil Harbor in the distant Wetlands by way of Southshore. Auberdine is the lifeline between night elf lands and the rest of the Alliance. Sentinels patrol the trade road south to Ashenvale, but their small garrison cannot keep watch over the deep wilds beyond it. Darkshore's shelter beneath the eastern mountains has not spared it a slow, malignant rot. Corrupted runoff from Felwood reaches sources such as Cliffspring Falls, poisoning the water and driving bears, stags, and the furbolgs of Blackwood Den into violent madness. The Dark Strand cultists perform foul rites at the Tower of Althalaxx. Twilight's Hammer gathers around the ancient, terrifying monument called the Master's Glaive.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_darkshore-564a731aee37a8e69ddb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 93.8057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:darnassus"] = {
  hash = "64ec56",
  fullHash = "5b6936",
  text = "Darnassus. Darnassus shelters the night elves high among Teldrassil’s branches. Tyrande Whisperwind and the druids guide the city, where an ancient people face life without immortality and seek ties with the Alliance. Darnassus spreads across Teldrassil’s upper canopy, off northern Kalimdor’s coast. Wooden buildings and winding paths circle a quiet central lake. Bridges join terraces for tradesmen, craftsmen, Sentinels, and druids, leaving the wilderness close to the city’s daily life. Darnassus is young beside the civilization of the kaldorei. After the Third War and the sacrifice of Nordrassil at Mount Hyjal, the night elves faced aging and sickness. With Archdruid Malfurion Stormrage lost in the Emerald Dream, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm and his followers planted Teldrassil in hope of renewing their lost vitality. The city grew among its boughs as refuge and monument to their survival, though malaise already stirs among the tree’s roots. Darnassus holds two centers of authority. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind guides the spiritual life and defense of her people from the Temple of the Moon, with the Sentinels at her command. Across the waterways, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm leads the druids in Malfurion’s absence. Both leaders present a united face to their new Alliance partners, yet friction between the priestesses and the druidic circle lingers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_darnassus-9f92f9fae349e6f8f58b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.875102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:deadmines"] = {
  hash = "9ef30a",
  fullHash = "d40ea7",
  text = "The Deadmines. Beneath Moonbrook, the Deadmines hide the Defias Brotherhood's staging ground. Once a source of Stormwind's gold, the tunnels were abandoned in the First War and long feared as haunted. Edwin VanCleef and goblin engineers work there beyond the realm's eyes, building an engine of vengeance against the kingdom. Before the First War, the Deadmines were called the greatest gold-producing center in human lands. Their gold supplied a third of Stormwind's reserves. When the Orcish Horde swept through the kingdom and razed the capital, the mines were hastily abandoned to darkness. Sir Anduin Lothar spent twenty months imprisoned by subterranean ogres while recovering the sacred Tome of Divinity. Warchief Blackhand's rebellious daughter Griselda fled into the lower passages with the ogre Turok, and both met their ends there. Believed cursed and home only to shambling undead and lurking subterranean vermin, the depths lay untouched for decades. After the Stonemasons' Guild was cast out of Stormwind without payment for rebuilding the city, Edwin VanCleef reorganized the disenfranchised artisans into the Defias Brotherhood. Seeking a secure headquarters beyond the reach of Gryan Stoutmantle's People's Militia, the Brotherhood chose its central redoubt in the abandoned tunnels. They drove out the legitimate Miners' League, leaving Foreman Thistlenettle's doomed workers to perish and rise as undead ghouls. The shafts became an industrial war camp, guarded by lumbering ogres, seasoned brigands, and mechanical constructs. Picks, fires, and illicit industry echo through the lower mines once more.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_deadmines-50bf3021ef2a7ce2e321/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.4253061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:deadwind"] = {
  hash = "7990db",
  fullHash = "ebbb90",
  text = "Deadwind Pass. Deadwind Pass is a blighted, jagged canyon in central Azeroth connecting Duskwood to the Swamp of Sorrows. At its center looms the Ivory Spire of Karazhan, former seat of the Guardian Medivh, whose death unleashed corrupting magical runoff across the land. It is a lifeless corridor stalked by ogres, ghosts, and demonic winds, offering neither refuge nor civilized shelter. Once considered an extension of the Redridge Mountains, Deadwind Pass has devolved into a hollowed-out scar of bleached stone and petrified wood. A foul, relentless wind cuts through the ravines, giving the gorge its name. The sky above is locked in a perpetual sepulchral gray, threatening erratic lightning storms that make traveling with metal arms hazardous. Totemic effigies hang from lifeless branches alongside the main road, warning wayfarers to keep moving. There are no fertile soils here, no mineable mineral veins, and no towns or flight masters to offer aid. The history of the pass revolves around the towering citadel of Karazhan. Built atop a potent convergence of subterranean ley lines, the tower served as the redoubt of the Guardian Medivh. It was within this spire that Medivh plotted the opening of the Dark Portal and the ruin of Stormwind. When his madness and possession came to light during the First War, an expedition led by Lord Anduin Lothar and young Khadgar breached the tower and executed him. Medivh's violent demise released a shockwave of fel taint that blighted both the pass and the neighboring forest of Duskwood, leaving Karazhan an ominous remnant leaking unstable magical energy. South of the main thoroughfare, the pass branches into the territory of the Deadwind ogres around the Vice. Dressed in foul rags and wielding jagged iron, these brutes relentlessly ambuscade anyone venturing near the southern road, reportedly serving a hidden master within the vicinity. Further south lies Morgan's Plot and the skeletal remnants of a hamlet that once catered to the tower. Today, the village's above-ground ruins and labyrinthine cellars crawl with wailing phantoms and restless shades, remnants of those whose lives were extinguished by Karazhan's unbridled sorcery.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_deadwind-e6a9179984d37f462fcc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 132.362449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:desolace"] = {
  hash = "0855ef",
  fullHash = "524830",
  text = "Desolace. Desolace is a bleak, windswept desert on Kalimdor's western coast, defined by cracked gray earth, constant stormy skies, and the bones of fallen beasts. It is the ancestral cradle of the vicious centaur race and a contested wasteland scarred by ancient Highborne ruins, encroaching demonic cults, and bitter tribal warfare. Both the Horde and Alliance maintain precarious footholds amidst the dust, each seeking to carve out influence while warding off spreading corruption. Desolace was not always a barren expanse of ash and yellowed pools. Legend holds that the land withered when the earth elemental Princess Theradras drained the surrounding life force to regenerate her form. Zaetar, son of Cenarius, came to her, but rather than subduing her, he took the princess as his mate. From their unnatural union sprang the first centaur. In shame and bloodthirsty wrath, the newborn race murdered their father and overran the plains, leaving behind the scarred wasteland known today. The interior of Desolace echoes with the thunder of thousands of hooves as four splintered centaur tribes wage endless war against all interlopers and each other. The Kolkar roam the eastern cliffs, the Magram hold crude yurts around brackish sinkholes, the Gelkis dwell behind rope bridges in deep southern ravines, and the fanatical Maraudine defend the approach to Maraudon itself. Travelers venturing across the wastes frequently find themselves drawn into this ancient blood feud, forced to slaughter one tribe to gain safe passage and tenuous parley with another. Beyond the warring centaur, darker perils fester along the borders. The Burning Blade cult operates freely out of Thunder Axe Fortress and Shadowbreak Ravine, summoning demons and satyrs that threaten to drown the region in fel fire. Along the stormy shoreline, naga creep onto the beaches of Ethel Rethor, while Darkspear trolls spear fish from the stilted platforms of Shadowprey Village. Tucked high in the northern crags, the Alliance watches from Nijel's Point, suspicious of the Horde's expansion and ever wary of the demonic taint spreading from Mannoroc Coven.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_desolace-5b64bfd7dbe81f20c369/1-0000.mp3", duration = 126.066939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:dunmorogh"] = {
  hash = "ee26fa",
  fullHash = "aa5d06",
  text = "Dun Morogh. In Dun Morogh, dwarves and displaced gnomes face threats from troggs, Frostmane trolls, and leper gnomes. Its guarded peaks hold Ironforge and offer shelter to both peoples. Dun Morogh’s high peaks rise between Searing Gorge to the south and the Wetlands to the north. Snowmelt feeds rivers across Khaz Modan, while copper and iron veins run through the mountains. Dwarven Mountaineers watch the narrow routes through North Gate Pass and South Gate Pass. At the region’s heart, Ironforge lies beneath the mountain as the capital of King Magni Bronzebeard’s realm. The Deeprun Tram links the city directly to Stormwind. Centuries ago, dwarves drove the native Frostmane ice trolls into the crags and made room for themselves beside their gnome allies. The two peoples thrived in neighboring mountain halls until Gnomeregan fell during the Third War. Troggs invaded, and radioactive gas ruined the city, turning its defenders into leper gnomes. High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque and the surviving gnomes fled into exile. They now live as guests in Ironforge, while leper gnomes and surviving troggs spill into Chill Breeze Valley. Trouble reaches across the mountain drifts. The Frostmane tribe has grown bolder, raising armed camps from Shimmer Ridge to Coldridge Valley. Troggs have clawed up from ancient underground fissures into Gol'Bolar Quarry, endangering the mines. Wendigos prowl, wild boars roam, Dark Iron dwarven agents infiltrate, and toxic fallout spreads from Gnomeregan’s ruins. Dwarven Mountaineers struggle to keep order from Kharanos to the border gates.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_dunmorogh-641771f13272f95ea231/1-0000.mp3", duration = 96.2089796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:durotar"] = {
  hash = "660cb8",
  fullHash = "020a34",
  text = "Durotar. In Durotar's red canyons, young orcs and Darkspear trolls prove themselves to the New Horde. Warchief Thrall founded this arid homeland after the Third War and named it for his father, Durotan. The Southfury River and the Great Sea hem it in. Razor Hill and the warrior city of Orgrimmar rise from its rock, while quilboar tribes and stranded Alliance troops still hold out. When Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to Kalimdor, he chose an untamed frontier on purpose. Here the orcs would earn an honest living through labor and grit, not conquest. The cracked red soil, baked gullies and towering spires remind them of Draenor before its destruction. Water is scarce and little of the land can be farmed, but the Horde embraced its natural defenses and stark grandeur. Across the Southfury to the west lie the open Barrens, and to the north the ruined wilds of Azshara. Durotar is proof that the orcs have forged their own destiny. The Horde holds Durotar, but keeping it is still a war. Razormane quilboar, pushed out when the nation was founded, raid farms and settlements from the western crags. In the south, what is left of Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's Kul Tiras fleet still holds the crumbling garrison of Tiragarde Keep under Lieutenant Benedict, and refuses to surrender. Worse, the demonic Burning Blade coven lurks in hidden ravines and deep sea caves, tempting young orcs back to fel power. On the southern coast, Sen'jin Village is the staging ground of the exiled Darkspear trolls, led by Master Gadrin. They lost their home on the nearby Echo Isles when the witch doctor Zalazane lost his mind and used dark voodoo to enslave his own kin. Unable to take the islands back alone, the Darkspear count on new orc and troll warriors from the Valley of Trials to break Zalazane's hold and secure the coast.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_durotar-456cd42a8eb66f8c9cc9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 110.942041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:duskwood"] = {
  hash = "246803",
  fullHash = "6934cc",
  text = "Duskwood. Duskwood lies beneath a curse, its sunlit past hidden by unending gloom. Stormwind has abandoned its human inhabitants, who cling to survival in Darkshire under the Night Watch's protection. Ghouls, giant venomous spiders, raiding ogres, and savage, mysterious worgen roam its groves and haunted barrows. Duskwood was once a rich agricultural tract in the Kingdom of Stormwind, with prosperous settlements such as Grand Hamlet and Raven Hill. Its ruin began when Guardian Medivh fell at Karazhan during the First War, unleashing waves of corrupted fel magic that twisted the woodland into a gnarled, shadowy morass. Spiders became terrifying predators, and fleeing wolves spilled north into Elwynn Forest. Grand Hamlet became Darkshire, where terrified townsfolk were left largely to fend for themselves after the royal garrison withdrew to defend the capital. Commander Althea Ebonlocke and her volunteer militia, the Night Watch, keep Darkshire alive, while Lord Ello Ebonlocke tries to govern the crumbling settlement. Beyond the town's lantern-lit perimeter, danger fills the land. Restless dead raised by the necromancer Morbent Fel stir in massive Raven Hill Cemetery. The Vul'Gol ogres came from Deadwind Pass to claim the south. Dark Riders stalk the crossroads, relentlessly searching for the lost Scythe. Near Duskwood's center lies a deep, ancient crater untouched by the surrounding corruption: the Twilight Grove. An ancient Great Tree there holds a dormant portal into the Emerald Dream. Soft, ethereal twilight bathes this unearthly sanctuary in the dying land, while sleeping druidic energies watch over it.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_duskwood-d262c1550a3d87562ead/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.4555102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:dustwallow"] = {
  hash = "da49f5",
  fullHash = "e88812",
  text = "Dustwallow Marsh. Dustwallow Marsh is a vast, sweltering swampland in central Kalimdor, trapped between the arid plains of the Barrens and the Great Sea. Fed by perpetual subterranean springs, its treacherous fens harbor dangerous predators, covert faction skirmishes, and the lurking presence of the black dragonflight. It serves as home to the Alliance fortress-island of Theramore in the east and the Horde-allied Stonemaul ogres of Brackenwall Village to the northwest. Before the Great Sundering, Dustwallow was a high, grassy plain teeming with herd animals. The cataclysm shattered the valley, causing the water table to rise and drown the earth in endless stagnant pools, mossy shallows, and choking mud. Underground springs ensure the marsh remains perpetually humid and submerged, blanketed in thick fog and infested with crocolisks, raptors, and spiders. Jagged shallows and wandering bogs make travel perilous for any adventurer stepping off the crumbling causeways. Dustwallow was largely wild until the aftermath of the Third War, when Lady Jaina Proudmoore led human refugees across the sea to establish the citadel of Theramore Isle on its eastern coast. Soon after, Kul Tiran forces under Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore waged war against Thrall's fledgling Horde, culminating in bloody battles across the wetlands and the Admiral's defeat. Today, an official armistice exists between Theramore and Orgrimmar, yet tension smolders beneath the surface. Theramore struggles with desertions and sabotage, while spies wage quiet, deadly conflicts across the fens.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_dustwallow-6eccca9cde58ff8c3681/1-0000.mp3", duration = 93.2832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:easternplaguelands"] = {
  hash = "0cae87",
  fullHash = "0dc9e9",
  text = "Eastern Plaguelands. The Eastern Plaguelands represent the devastated heart of the fallen Kingdom of Lordaeron, suffocated under Scourge blight, toxic mists, and corrupted fungal growths. Stratholme looms in the north as the undead power base in the region, while the zealous Scarlet Crusade entrenches in the east. Amid the nightmare, Light's Hope Chapel stands as a resolute, neutral sanctuary for anyone brave enough to fight back. Once known as the fertile Eastweald, this region was the agricultural spine of northern Lordaeron, anchored by the great metropolis of Stratholme and prosperous towns like Darrowshire and Corin's Crossing. During the Third War, Prince Arthas purged Stratholme after discovering its grain supply had been laced with the Plague of Undeath. Following the kingdom's collapse, the Scourge's dark magic ran unchecked. The soil turned brown and rotten, ordinary flora warped into colossal mushrooms, and an eerie orange mist settled across the valleys. Stratholme serves as the southern seat of Scourge authority in Lordaeron, commanded by Baron Rivendare under the direction of the lich Kel'Thuzad. Defying the undead from the southeast is the fanatical Scarlet Crusade, which has fortified the town of Tyr's Hand and assaults Stratholme's scarlet bastions. Their dogmatic hatred extends beyond the dead to any living traveler who crosses their borders. To the north, the Mossflayer forest trolls of Zul'Mashar wage their own isolated, desperate campaigns, caught in the crossfire of the greater conflict. Survival in the Eastern Plaguelands requires careful footing. The neutral Argent Dawn operates out of Light's Hope Chapel in the far east, offering a safe haven, essential supplies, and flight routes to both Alliance and Horde champions. Beyond the chapel, the ruined watchtowers of Crown Guard, Eastwall, Northpass, and Plaguewood stand as silent reminders of Lordaeron's lost defenses.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_easternplaguelands-60a7b1bcf3b3f6bbad67/1-0000.mp3", duration = 107.467755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:elwynn"] = {
  hash = "9881e6",
  fullHash = "93c321",
  text = "Elwynn Forest. In Elwynn Forest, Stormwind draws timber, gold, fish, and crops from the land. Northshire Abbey sends human recruits into the woods, where vermin, displaced wildlife, and brigands threaten the peace. Elwynn Forest stretches south from Stormwind’s gates to the Nazferiti River. Rain feeds its fertile fields, while dense hardwood forests supply the capital. Goldshire and the Eastvale Logging Camp send grain, livestock, lumber, and raw copper to Stormwind. Elwynn also breeds sturdy Evendale horses, prized by travelers and knights across the kingdom. Trouble presses in from every side. The Stormwind Army is stretched thin by duties far away, leaving local marshals and deputies to depend on untested recruits. Kobolds have overrun the Fargodeep and Jasperlode mines. Riverpaw gnolls prowl the southwestern borders, while murlocs hold the shores of Stone Cairn Lake and Crystal Lake. Wolves fleeing corruption across the river in Duskwood have crossed into Elwynn.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_elwynn-c47f9d98044b8a013cae/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.9918367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:felwood"] = {
  hash = "95909a",
  fullHash = "146059",
  text = "Felwood. Felwood is a blighted forest nestled between Ashenvale and Mount Hyjal, deeply poisoned by demonic magic during the Third War. Once a lush paradise of meadows and groves tended by the night elves, it now crawls with demonic satyrs, slavering beasts, and twisted ancients. Cenarion Circle druids, alongside outposts of the Alliance and Horde, desperately work to cleanse the land and purge the Shadow Council from its depths. Before the Third War, this valley was a peaceful haven of singing birds, clear streams, and tranquil groves guarded by ancient protectors. That ended when the dreadlord Tichondrius and the Burning Legion deployed the Skull of Gul'dan to poison northern Kalimdor ahead of their assault on Mount Hyjal. The skull's vile sorceries twisted the forest floor into a blackened mire. Though Illidan Stormrage eventually slew Tichondrius and consumed the artifact's essence, the land remained steeped in fel corruption. Mighty treants and ancients rotted from within, rivers turned to boiling bile, and the forest's beasts were driven into cannibalistic madness. Healing Felwood has proven agonizingly slow. At the Emerald Sanctuary along the southern border, the Cenarion Circle wages an uphill battle against the blighted corruption. Druids equip wanderers with Cenarion beacons and cleansing salves, laboring to heal poisoned plants like songflowers and night dragon bulbs. Both factions maintain footholds in the tainted wilds—the night elves at Talonbranch Glade in the north and the Horde at Bloodvenom Post. These outposts serve as fragile bulwarks amid a deadly wilderness where nature has turned against all mortal flesh.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_felwood-9195f25d178cdd7921ce/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.9779592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:feralas"] = {
  hash = "f6fa49",
  fullHash = "aca08d",
  text = "Feralas. Feralas is a vast, mist-shrouded rainforest nestled between Desolace, Thousand Needles, and the Veiled Sea. Sheltered by soaring mountain walls, it preserves an ancient ecosystem dotted with overgrown Kaldorei ruins and primordial beasts. Today, the wilderness is contested territory, caught between ancient Highborne remnants, invasive Gordunni ogres, and rival outposts established by the Alliance and the Horde. While neighboring Desolace and Thousand Needles have dried into barren badlands and desiccated canyons, Feralas remains intensely verdant. Mountain ranges seal off the canopy, sustaining a warm microclimate of deep mists, ancient trees, and towering waterfalls. Before the Great Sundering, much of Kalimdor looked like this. Over millennia, the deep jungle swallowed the monuments of the Kaldorei civilization that once ruled here, hiding forgotten temples and pavilions beneath strangling moss and thick fern brush. Dominating central Feralas is the ruined metropolis of Eldre'Thalas, now called Dire Maul. Built at the peak of the Kaldorei empire, its vast stone courtyards have fallen into ruin, desecrated by the savage Gordunni ogre clan. These ogres spread across the valley, pillaging sacred sites, defiling pristine groves, and driving indigenous wildlife into territorial frenzy. Deep within Dire Maul's sealed chambers, whispers persist of Highborne remnants and trapped powers dating back ten thousand years. Both the Alliance and the Horde have mounted major expeditions into the jungle, though traversing it remains dangerous. From Feathermoon Stronghold on Sardor Isle, General Shandris Feathermoon directs Sentinels against both ogre expansion and encroaching naga. To the east lies Camp Mojache, a central Horde garrison struggling against aggressive Woodpaw gnolls, hostile Grimtotem tauren, and the relentless ogre clans. Neither faction commands true control over the untamable interior.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_feralas-1a430a4a24074a10ace1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 119.301224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:gnomeregan"] = {
  hash = "7af386",
  fullHash = "f447e7",
  text = "Gnomeregan. In Dun Morogh, Gnomeregan lies ruined and quarantined after a desperate defense against troggs. Once the greatest technological wonder of the gnomish race, it was their underground capital. Its surviving people live as refugees in nearby Ironforge, while irradiated halls hold mutated troggs, deranged leper gnomes, and rogue mechanical sentries. Gnomeregan once gathered gnomish invention, competitive craft, and politics beneath Dun Morogh. With help from the nearby dwarves of Ironforge, its people built a vast mechanical complex powered by grinding engines and whirring gears. An intricate ventilation network needed fifteen technicians working around the clock to keep fresh air moving. At Tinkers' Court, gnomes debated and competed as their capital grew into a marvel of industry. During the Second War, they sent the Alliance of Lordaeron siege engines, flying machines, and armaments. During the Third War, as the Scourge tore through Lordaeron, dwarven excavations at the ancient titan vault of Uldaman awakened primitive troggs beneath Dun Morogh. The creatures burrowed into Gnomeregan's lower foundations, and High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque led his forces in a desperate defense. When the city's defenses began to fail, Mekkatorque followed the counsel of his chief advisor, Mekgineer Thermaplugg. He ordered the radioactive waste tanks vented into the lower levels to eradicate the invaders. The toxic gas spread beyond control, and nearly eighty percent of the gnomish population died within days.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_gnomeregan-83a3779034ed8572f71b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.2938776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:hallofthanes"] = {
  hash = "d9aa4b",
  fullHash = "8044c9",
  text = "Hall of Thanes. Deep beneath the bustling city of Ironforge lies the Hall of Thanes, an ancient subterranean complex steeped in dwarven history. Intended for early Alliance adventurers, this secluded dungeon holds relics of the mountain kingdom's past that have fallen into peril. Gaining entry requires unearthing forgotten routes through the mountain itself. While Ironforge hums with the ringing of hammers in the Great Forge, forgotten halls delve far deeper beneath the stone. The Hall of Thanes represents one of these venerable subterranean reaches. Long tied to ancestral dwarven bloodlines and relics of stonecraft, it now requires intrepid Alliance champions to venture into its shadowed vaults to secure the depths. Accessing the Hall of Thanes is no casual feat. The corridors leading to these ancient vaults have been sealed away or hidden from everyday travelers in Ironforge. Adventurers must decipher forgotten charting, beginning with recovering an Underground Map, before they can navigate the deeper tunnels and breach the threshold of the dungeon.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_hallofthanes-a29f208418dde63485a8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.3314286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:hillsbrad"] = {
  hash = "49a273",
  fullHash = "511206",
  text = "Hillsbrad Foothills. Hillsbrad Foothills remains green while war tears through the last pastoral reaches of Lordaeron. Southshore's humans and the Forsaken at Tarren Mill clash across the fields, making the region a violent crossroads for Alliance and Horde armies. Hillsbrad remains green while blight consumes much of northern Lordaeron. Rolling pastures, pine groves, and working farms still recall the kingdom before the Third War. Armed patrols, rogue mercenaries, and scavenger beasts cross its fields and roads. Lordaeron's breadbasket has become one of its most contested borderlands. Southshore stands on the southern coast, held by human refugees and soldiers loyal to the Alliance. North of it, Tarren Mill was once a quiet human farming hamlet, until the Scourge destroyed it and the Forsaken fortified it as their southernmost garrison. The open ground between the settlements has become a relentless killing ground, where Alliance and Horde champions skirmish for control of Hillsbrad. Hillsbrad's violence reaches back to the Second War, when its coast and hills saw the first massive clashes between the Grand Alliance and the Old Horde. Durnholde Keep later served as the internment camp where Thrall was raised before leading the orcish rebellion. Now the keep lies in ruins, held by the Syndicate, displaced human nobles turned bandits. Coastal murlocs, hostile gnolls, and the shadowed dead on Purgation Isle leave no corner of the hills secure.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_hillsbrad-930960ce8aa0e2de0b9f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.0767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:hinterlands"] = {
  hash = "ecf2d1",
  fullHash = "a53dc6",
  text = "The Hinterlands. The Hinterlands are a rugged, densely forested wilderness in eastern Lordaeron, tucked between the Plaguelands and the sea. The region is defined by ancient Amani troll temple ruins, the towering aeries of the Wildhammer clan at Aerie Peak, and a colossal Great Tree anchoring a mysterious rift to the Emerald Dream. Long before humans or dwarves walked northern Lordaeron, the forest trolls of the Amani Empire held dominion over the Hinterlands. Following their defeat in the ancient Troll Wars, their empire shattered into disparate tribes. Centuries later, after the tragic War of the Three Hammers drove the Wildhammer dwarves from Grim Batol, the dwarves migrated north and made their home on the surface at Aerie Peak. Taming the native gryphons and embracing shamanic traditions, the Wildhammer constructed a massive skybound stronghold. The trolls viewed these surface-dwelling dwarves as unwelcome squatters upon ancestral lands, sparking a ceaseless feud of skirmishes and raids. Three distinct forest troll factions occupy the dense canopy and crumbling stone temples. The brutal and fanatical Vilebranch tribe is the strongest, maintaining sprawling strongholds at the terraced temple-city of Jintha'Alor and the towering Altar of Zul. To the south, the weaker Witherbark tribe clings to Shadra'Alor and Hiri'Watha. The nomadic Revantusk tribe, long scorned by their cousins and lacking a true home, chose a radical path after the Third War: they struck a treaty with Thrall's New Horde. Bolstered by orcish and Forsaken aid, the Revantusk founded Revantusk Village on the eastern sea cliffs, carving out a defiant coastal bastion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_hinterlands-7b8354ffdd8e1a4ff611/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.2465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ironforge"] = {
  hash = "48033d",
  fullHash = "d8192b",
  text = "Ironforge. Carved into the heart of Ironforge Mountain in snowy Dun Morogh, Ironforge is the ancestral capital of the Bronzebeard dwarves and an unassailable bastion of the Alliance. Centered on the molten heat of the Great Forge, this sprawling underground city serves as a bustling hub of commerce, industry, and ancient dwarven lore. Under the steadfast rule of King Magni Bronzebeard, it now also shelters the displaced gnomes of nearby Gnomeregan. Ironforge was founded twenty-five hundred years ago when the first generation of dwarves awakened in Uldaman and journeyed west into Dun Morogh's jagged peaks. Delving into the tallest mountain, they built a massive shrine to their titan progenitor Khaz'goroth alongside the colossal Great Forge. Ever since, the city has expanded in concentric rings around that fiery hearth. Unlike surface capitals, Ironforge is entirely enclosed beneath stone ceilings, warmed by roaring hearths and subterranean rivers of molten magma. Its fortified gates and cavernous depths have proven near-impregnable throughout centuries of war. For generations, three dwarven clans coexisted within Ironforge: the Bronzebeards, the Wildhammers, and the Dark Irons. When High King Modimus Anvilmar died in 230 BDP, a bitter civil war erupted for dominion over the mountain. The Bronzebeard clan prevailed and expelled their rivals. Later, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan led a vengeful Dark Iron siege that penetrated to the heart of Ironforge before being repulsed by Bronzebeard forces. Thaurissan accidentally summoned Ragnaros the Firelord to the south, shattering the Redridge Mountains and dooming his own host. The Bronzebeards have held undisputed control of Ironforge ever since. During the Second War, the Bleeding Hollow orc clan overran much of Khaz Modan, but Ironforge held firm behind its sealed gates until the Alliance of Lordaeron lifted the siege. Bound by this debt of honor, King Magni Bronzebeard pledged Ironforge's eternal loyalty to the Alliance, standing firm even when kingdoms like Gilneas and Quel'Thalas abandoned it. In the Third War, Magni mourned the loss of his brother Muradin in Northrend. Today, Ironforge remains one of the Alliance's fiercest bulwarks. Following the tragic fall of Gnomeregan to trogg invaders and irradiation, King Magni opened Ironforge's gates to thousands of gnome refugees. Under High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque, the exiles established Tinker Town, carving out a buzzing technological haven within the stone city. Coupled with the subterranean Deeprun Tram connecting Ironforge directly to Stormwind City, the dwarven stronghold has transformed into a lively crossroads of dwarven steelcraft and gnomish ingenuity.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ironforge-8937d4235ba0c7986a01/1-0000.mp3", duration = 158.066939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:lochmodan"] = {
  hash = "338a02",
  fullHash = "fc5017",
  text = "Loch Modan. The Stonewrought Dam holds Loch Modan's great lake above the Wetlands. Around Thelsamar, troggs threaten the southern valleys while kobolds work the mines and ogres hold the northeast highlands. Loch Modan's lake fills a green valley east of Dun Morogh. Pines and birches climb toward the rugged peaks, while the Stonewrought Dam spans the northern rim. The dwarven town of Thelsamar draws mountaineers, hunters, and miners. Copper and tin veins run through the surrounding hills. Broken catapults and ballistae on the lake bed recall the Second War, when the orcish Horde occupied Loch Modan before the dwarves drove them out. More recently, dwarven excavations opened underground chambers and released savage troggs. Trogg tribes, led by chieftains, have overrun the southern canyons of Stonesplinter Valley and the Valley of Kings. The Ironforge Mountaineers fight to defend the trade routes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_lochmodan-9953ec54ecdd519296bc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.2114286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:moonglade"] = {
  hash = "def14d",
  fullHash = "e6bb1c",
  text = "Moonglade. Moonglade is an ancient, secluded forest valley nestled high in northern Kalimdor, revered as the sacred heartland of druidism and the home of the Cenarion Circle. Sheltered beneath perpetual twilight and untouched by mortal corruption, it is a neutral haven where night elf and tauren practitioners study nature side by side. For non-druids, reaching this highland sanctuary requires braving the hazardous mountain tunnel of Timbermaw Hold. Ringed by sheer mountains north of Felwood and Winterspring, Moonglade exists in a state of eternal, serene night under the soft glow of Elune's moon. At its center lies Lake Elune'ara, a deep, tranquil body of water held sacred since the dawn of kaldorei civilization. The glade remained unmarred by the Burning Legion during both ancient invasions and the recent Third War, shielded by the vigilant demigod Cenarius and his offspring. Today, under the stewardship of Keeper Remulos, the valley serves as a peaceful redoubt where nature's balance is preserved free from factional conflict. Moonglade is the headquarters of the Cenarion Circle, founded millennia ago by Malfurion Stormrage. In the lakeside village of Nighthaven, ancient traditions guide druidic instruction. Following the Third War, the Circle took the historic step of welcoming tauren into their ranks, led by the teachings of Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem. In Moonglade, political loyalties to the Alliance or Horde are put aside; armed sentries enforce strict neutrality, ensuring that any violence between members meets swift justice.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_moonglade-f71a2b2c0d3fab52d058/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.7159184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:mulgore"] = {
  hash = "437a28",
  fullHash = "abb244",
  text = "Mulgore. Mulgore shelters the tauren in a valley beneath the Stonetalon Mountains. High natural walls surround its emerald grasslands, plateaus, and plains. After Cairne Bloodhoof joined Thrall's Horde and drove out the centaur, the united tribes made Mulgore their homeland, with Thunder Bluff as their spiritual and territorial heart. The tauren have reclaimed a home after generations on the move. Centaur warbands hunted and displaced them across the central plains until High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof met Thrall and his displaced orcs. The tauren and orcs shared ideals of honor, shamanism, and strength, and the tauren joined the new Horde. With orcish help, Cairne broke the centaur hold on the region and led his people into Mulgore. They built permanent settlements here for the first time in centuries, and raised their capital, Thunder Bluff, on the highest northern mesas. Young tauren face the Great Hunt and honor the Earthmother as they reach maturity. Their training begins at Camp Narache on the southern plateau of Red Cloud Mesa, under Chief Hawkwind. From there, they descend into the Golden Plains and Bloodhoof Village to undertake the Rites of the Earthmother. They learn to commune with ancestral spirits, forage native herbs, and respect the kodos and tallstriders whose herds sustain tauren life. Mulgore's quiet plains face threats from within and beyond its borders. Displaced quilboar have settled in Brambleblade Ravine and defiled the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks. Windfury harpies hunt wild game in the mountains, while Palemane gnolls strike from hidden caves. Goblins of the Venture Company foul sacred water wells and quarry the eastern hills. Dwarven explorers from Bael Modan have also opened the Bael'dun Digsite to unearth ancient titan artifacts, despite tauren protests.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_mulgore-9254b17cf88b973f2841/1-0000.mp3", duration = 107.31102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:orgrimmar"] = {
  hash = "bbb1c4",
  fullHash = "9b8a7d",
  text = "Orgrimmar. Orgrimmar rises from the red canyons of northern Durotar as the orcs’ fortified capital and the New Horde’s heart. Trolls, tauren, and Forsaken allies also find shelter within its walls. Orgrimmar grew from the orcs’ search for a homeland after the Third War. Warchief Thrall led them to Kalimdor’s arid coasts, seeking freedom from demonic corruption and human bondage. He named the harsh land Durotar for his father and the city for his fallen mentor, Orgrim Doomhammer. Building the stronghold cost blood and sweat. With help from the tauren, Darkspear trolls, goblin engineers, and the Champion Rexxar, the Horde drove back centaur, harpies, and ferocious thunder lizards that threatened the canyons. Rugged mountains shield Orgrimmar from foreign invaders and Durotar’s violent razor-winds. The city’s natural hollows give each valley its own place in Horde life. The Valley of Strength holds the civic gatehouse, with a route deeper into the trade bazaar of The Drag. Tauren druids and shaman gather by the pools in the Valley of Wisdom. Darkspear shadow hunters and witch doctors live among the stilted pavilions of the Valley of Spirits.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_orgrimmar-f7475ff9c9e33bdd1ddb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.0416327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ragefire"] = {
  hash = "7abebc",
  fullHash = "72908f",
  text = "Ragefire Chasm. Beneath Orgrimmar, Ragefire Chasm's volcanic tunnels shelter troggs and a demonic cult. The Searing Blade plots against the young Horde from caverns below the Cleft of Shadow, threatening the capital above. Ragefire troggs erupt from deep beneath Orgrimmar into a maze of volcanic corridors and lava pits. As orcish builders raise their new home above, the troggs grow aggressive. Magatha Grimtotem sends Maur Grimtotem to open talks on Thunder Bluff's behalf, but the troggs slaughter the envoy. They yield only to force. A demonic cult called the Searing Blade is more dangerous than the troggs. Loyal to the Shadow Council, the fanatics plot the young Horde's ruin from caverns beside streams of magma. The warlock Jergosh the Invoker, the cunning satyr Bazzalan, and the vicious felguard Taragaman the Hungerer lead them. Taragaman presides over an altar in the lava lake.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ragefire-f17be7db68ea92fe2318/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.2563265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:razorfenkraul"] = {
  hash = "c3b1aa",
  fullHash = "c3b1aa",
  text = "Razorfen Kraul. In the far south of the Barrens, quilboar tribes defend the sacred brambles of Razorfen Kraul. They revere the thickets as the resting place of their ancestor, Agamaggan, while Charlga Razorflank’s rule threatens the region. Ten thousand years ago, during the War of the Ancients, Agamaggan joined the fight against the Burning Legion. The colossal wild god was slain, but his sacrifice preserved life on Azeroth. Legends say massive thorn-covered vines sprang up wherever his blood seeped into the earth, forming thick, defensive labyrinths across the southern plains. The quilboar revere Agamaggan as their divine ancestor and claimed these thickets as holy lands. The greatest became the Razorfen.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_razorfenkraul-747331ef444232a668b0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.7363265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:redridge"] = {
  hash = "1add3c",
  fullHash = "059888",
  text = "Redridge Mountains. Redridge Mountains face attacks from Shadowhide gnolls and Blackrock orcs of the Dark Horde. Rushing rivers cut through wooded slopes around Lake Everstill, but Lakeshire must hold the line with help from wandering mercenaries while Stormwind’s forces are tied up by strife at home. Redridge was once among the kingdom’s most peaceful regions, supplying Stormwind with timber, freshwater fish, and crops. Now Lakeshire stays fortified against aggressive incursions. Stonewatch Keep, once a proud stronghold of the kingdom, has fallen to renegade Blackrock orcs. Their control cuts off roads and threatens to overrun the eastern valleys. These peaks form part of the spine running down the subcontinent of Azeroth. Centuries ago, the range stretched much farther north. Then the Dark Iron clan summoned the Firelord Ragnaros by accident, and the earth tore open to form the fiery crater of Blackrock Mountain. The cataclysm left the northern peaks severed into the ash choked Burning Steppes. Only the southern Redridge valleys remain lush and green, exposed to attacks from the slopes above. Magistrate Solomon and Marshal Marris plead for reinforcements, but Stormwind’s regency council has left Lakeshire to fend for itself. Desperate entreaties to Sentinel Hill and Darkshire bring no help; both settlements are crippled by crises of their own. With no reinforcements in sight, local magistrates hire any capable sellsword willing to brave the Lakeridge Highway, clear the gnoll camps, and push back the danger over Lake Everstill.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_redridge-2feccd01fe56e35107e6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.9730612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:riverglades"] = {
  hash = "202c0d",
  fullHash = "f8c733",
  text = "Riverglades. The Riverglades is a contested river valley east of the Burning Steppes and Badlands, historically claimed by the Kingdom of Stormwind. Overrun by the Old Horde during the First War and largely abandoned by the crown, the region remains a battleground as local human loyalists clash with modern Horde outposts and local wildlife. It borders Redridge Mountains to the southwest, Swamp of Sorrows to the south, and faces Tanaris across the sea. Historically part of the Kingdom of Stormwind, the Riverglades fell into ruin during the First War when the Old Horde swept through the region. Its central settlement, Sunnyglade, was sacked by the orcs, who spared its mage tower solely to study human sorcery before enslaving the surviving townsfolk. Though Stormwind soldiers mounted an effort to liberate the captives and rebuild, the kingdom eventually deemed the entire valley lost and diverted resources toward defending the core homelands. For years, the region remained largely cut off from formal crown support. Despite being left behind by Stormwind's higher leadership, the human defenders never fully surrendered their homeland. Under Knight Commander Demar Brant of the legendary Brotherhood of the Horse, the remaining loyalists hold Farholde Keep as a bastion against the wilderness and hostile invaders. Meanwhile, the modern Horde maintains their own strong presence nearby at outposts like Rog'mar and Bolder'ok, ensuring that the ancient conflict over the glades continues to smolder. Stretching along the coast of the Forbidding Sea, the Riverglades is flanked by mountainous terrain and winding waterways. Travelers on foot can cross west through Blackrock Pass to the Burning Steppes, wind southwest through Redridge Pass toward the Redridge Mountains, navigate south into the Swamp of Sorrows, or trek northwest past Twilight's Shroud into the Badlands. On the coast, Powderfuse Port provides sea passage connecting directly across the ocean to Steamwheedle Port in Tanaris.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_riverglades-dbef125368c24a50c6f2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 109.453061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ruinsoflordaeron"] = {
  hash = "f03631",
  fullHash = "d6fd25",
  text = "Ruins of Lordaeron. Perched directly above the subterranean Undercity, the Ruins of Lordaeron are the hollow remains of humanity's once-mighty northern kingdom. Fallen to the Scourge and Prince Arthas during the Third War, the surface streets and cracked plazas now crawl with wandering monstrosities, vengeful specters, and vile experimenters. Both the Alliance and the Horde dispatch ventures into this blighted citadel to reclaim relics of the fallen past or purge the horrors lurking above the Forsaken capital. Capital City was once the crown jewel of the Eastern Kingdoms, the seat of King Terenas Menethil II and the beating heart of human civilization. When Prince Arthas returned from Northrend, he slew his father in the throne room and unleashed the undead upon the citizenry. Today, while the Forsaken dwell in the catacombs of the Undercity below, the surface ruins remain a forsaken twilight zone. Broken archways and weed-choked cobbles shelter feral beasts like Witherfang and deranged remnants of the past. The dangers within the ruined grounds go beyond restless dead. Apothecaries and sinister figures like Viktor the Vile work on testing new plagues among the shadows, while twisted experiments like The Abandoned roam freely. Wrathful spirits such as Rath'mael and cursed retainers like The Baron haunt the collapsed hallways, locked in unending torment and striking out against any living or undead trespassers who cross their paths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ruinsoflordaeron-d82e6c1af7b3df0b07ec/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.0767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:scarlet"] = {
  hash = "f7370c",
  fullHash = "b49442",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery. Perched in the northeastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Monastery was once a peaceful sanctuary and center of learning for the Church of the Holy Light. Following the fall of Lordaeron, it was seized and heavily fortified by the Scarlet Crusade as their primary forward base in the west. Now, it stands as a sprawling stronghold divided into four wings, where fanatical zealots train, torture captives, and wage an uncompromising war against all outsiders. Before the Third War, the monastery served as a proud seat of Lordaeron's priesthood and a center for learning and enlightenment. Following the Scourge's devastation, surviving crusaders purged the halls of lingering undead and spiders, transforming the tranquil archives and courtyards into a fortified garrison. Driven by paranoia and grief, the Scarlet Crusade came to view all outsiders—living or undead, human or otherwise—as carriers of the plague who must be cleansed with fire. Past the monastery's Grand Vestibule lie four distinct quarters. The Graveyard is an unhallowed open-air terrace where fallen crusaders rise as undead abominations and torturers pry confessions from prisoners. The Library preserves precious theological texts and historical tomes, guarded by the renegade mage Arcanist Doan and his hounds. The Armory serves as a training ground where Scarlet weaponmasters like Herod drill fanatic recruits into ironclad warriors. Deepest within stands the Cathedral, the grand heart of the stronghold where the Crusade's leadership directs their crusade across the blighted north.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_scarlet-107e7e0404d2f3229c1e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.3763265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:searinggorge"] = {
  hash = "0dce1e",
  fullHash = "ee1599",
  text = "Searing Gorge. Searing Gorge is a scorched, desolate canyon in southern Khaz Modan, bordering Blackrock Mountain to the south. Once a verdant expanse of the Redridge Mountains, it was devastated over two centuries ago by the apocalyptic summoning of Ragnaros the Firelord. Today, the gorge is heavily worked by the Dark Iron clan, whose extensive slave mines in the Cauldron are closely monitored by the breakaway Thorium Brotherhood. Before the War of the Three Hammers, the territory that is now Searing Gorge was a green, rolling mountain region connected to the Redridge chain. That lush landscape ceased to exist over two hundred and thirty years ago when Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan, desperate to turn the tide of defeat, summoned Ragnaros from the depths beneath the world. The cataclysmic emergence of the Firelord tore the earth asunder, raised Blackrock Mountain, reduced the grand Dark Iron city of Thaurissan to smoking ruins, and burned the northern valley into a cracked expanse of ash, sulfur, and volcanic glass. Seeking raw materials to supply their war machine after the loss of their old capital, the Dark Iron dwarves seized the desolate gorge. In the center of the valley lies the Cauldron, a colossal open-pit excavation plunging deep into the stone. Within its lower recesses sits the Slag Pit, a brutal subterranean mining complex. Here, Dark Iron overseers and lumbering war golems drive captured enemies into grueling slave labor, pulling iron, mithril, and rare deposits of Dark Iron ore out of the burning rock under the constant threat of lava surges and roaming beasts. Perched on the northern crags overlooking the excavation sits Thorium Point, the fortified outpost of the Thorium Brotherhood. Having split from their kin in Blackrock Mountain, these master craftsmen offer neutral shelter to travelers willing to undermine the Dark Iron operations or supply them with rare smelting components. Access to the gorge itself is tightly restricted: the Alliance keeps the Stonewrought Pass locked down behind heavy iron gates toward Loch Modan, while Horde expeditions from nearby Kargath have cut a narrow trail through the eastern badlands to scout the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_searinggorge-c6e525ab5f1224b7ba00/1-0000.mp3", duration = 126.223673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:shadowfang"] = {
  hash = "f27519",
  fullHash = "a539d2",
  text = "Shadowfang Keep. In southern Silverpine, Shadowfang Keep shelters the rogue Kirin Tor wizard Archmage Arugal and the worgen he summoned. The old Silverlaine estate now holds feral beasts and the phantoms of its slaughtered defenders, while nearby Pyrewood Village remains bound to a horrifying curse. Shadowfang Keep was once Silverlaine Keep, a noble seat defended by the pious Commander Springvale. During the Third War, the Scourge swept through Lordaeron and Dalaran's wizards fell in droves. Desperate to stem the rising dead, Archmage Arugal defied the Kirin Tor leadership and turned to the research of the wizard Ur. He summoned extra-dimensional worgen to turn the tide. The beasts savaged the undead, then slipped beyond mortal control and stormed southern Silverpine. Baron Silverlaine and his entire court were massacred, doomed to haunt the stone halls beside the feral monsters that overran their home. Broken by guilt and madness, Arugal withdrew behind the blood-stained battlements. He called the ravenous wolf-men his beloved children and named the castle Shadowfang Keep. In its stone roosts, lupine packs and shadowy hounds thrive under trainers such as Wolf Master Nandos, watched by Arugal's massive lupine beast, Fenrus the Devourer. The curse did not halt at the walls. Nearby Pyrewood Village remains trapped in a horrifying nocturnal cycle: its people walk as men by day and hunt as ravenous worgen beneath the moon, bound to the mad archmage on the heights above.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_shadowfang-cbe62bd919fde237c1b5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.6857143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:shendralas"] = {
  hash = "9a7f5d",
  fullHash = "13175a",
  text = "Shen'dralas. Shen'dralas is a rugged, contested wilderness situated between Feralas, Mulgore, and south-eastern Desolace. Dominated by ancient night elven remnants and troubled borders, it serves as a volatile crossroads where encroaching centaur, displaced outcasts, and bristling quilboar collide. Tucked away in the heights south-east of Desolace and bordered by Mulgore and Feralas, Shen'dralas is a raw borderland far removed from major faction capitols. Its craggy passes and hidden enclaves harbor desperate groups carving out existence on the edge of wild Kalimdor. From secluded vantage points like Evenshade Overlook to lawless refuges like Outcast Hideaway, the zone is defined by those who wish to watch unseen or stay forgotten. The land bears the distinct mark of the highborne legacy that gave it its name, yet it is far from peaceful. Centaur conflict bleeds across the Magram Front, keeping old blood feuds alive and well along its borders. Meanwhile, encroaching quilboar maintain fortified redoubts such as Bristleback Retreat, turning every shadowed ravine into contested territory for any traveler passing through. Leading out north-west toward Desolace lies the Valley of Bones, a grim corridor that serves as the primary overland access route into Shen'dralas. Bleached by sun and wind, this passage stands as a harsh reminder of the violent struggles that constantly ripple through these crags.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_shendralas-e1dbed34e36911e7bef8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.2971429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:silithus"] = {
  hash = "e23931",
  fullHash = "90083a",
  text = "Silithus. Silithus is a desolate, wind-scoured desert located in the far southwestern corner of Kalimdor. Beneath its arid sands lie vast colonies of insectoid silithid and the ancient ruins of Ahn'Qiraj, sealed a millennium ago behind the formidable Scarab Wall. Today, the druids of the Cenarion Circle maintain an embattled vigil against creeping swarms, doomsday cultists. Reaching Silithus requires navigating through the primeval jungle of Un'Goro Crater and ascending a wide, rocky pass nestled in its northwestern corner. The transition is stark: lush primordial growth vanishes into a vast expanse of shifting dunes, jagged stone, and unnatural towering hives. Water is virtually nonexistent, and the winds carry the hum of insect wings and the whispering sands. The Cenarion Circle maintains Cenarion Hold near the center of the desert, serving as the sole bastion of civilization and shelter for travelers, researchers, and warriors braving the hostile wastes. A thousand years ago, this arid expanse served as the battlefield for the War of the Shifting Sands. An allied army of night elves and great dragonflights fought desperately to drive back the Qiraji, an insidious insectoid empire spawned from the ancient Aqiri civilization. When total victory proved impossible, the defenders sealed the swarms within their stronghold using the Scarab Wall and the enchanted Scarab Dais. While the monumental gates held, subterranean tunnels allowed the beast-like silithids to burrow out, establishing sprawling colonies such as Hive'Ashi, Hive'Zora, and Hive'Regal throughout the sands. The apocalyptic Twilight's Hammer cult has entrenched itself across several fortified outposts, summoning elemental terrors and venerating the dark power slumbering beneath Ahn'Qiraj. Cenarion Circle observers monitor the desert's spreading silithid colonies and the ancient threat behind the Scarab Wall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_silithus-ba7a09f98de098693e8f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 112.53551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:silverpine"] = {
  hash = "5b4e73",
  fullHash = "5b4e73",
  text = "Silverpine Forest. Silverpine Forest lies under Forsaken rule and threat from rival forces along Lordaeron's western coast. The Scourge's plague scars the woods, where worgen, diseased gnolls, and hostile human enclaves endanger travelers. Silverpine Forest lies between Tirisfal Glades and the sealed Greymane Wall. Once a lush wonder of Lordaeron, it has withered since the Third War. Ghostly fog moves among dying, silver-barked pines, and ruined farmsteads lie abandoned to the wilderness. Tirisfal Glades borders the forest to the north, and Lordamere Lake lies along its eastern shore. To the south, the Greymane Wall shuts Gilneas away from the chaos beyond. The Forsaken hold their central outpost at the Sepulcher and struggle to secure the woods from enemies on every side.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_silverpine-7b7aaadd294a0628aa8a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.9044898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stockade"] = {
  hash = "17f666",
  fullHash = "17f666",
  text = "The Stockade. The Stockade holds dangerous prisoners beneath Stormwind's canal district near the Mage Quarter. Built as a high-security prison for cutthroats, political insurgents, and captured enemies of state, it has fallen into violent anarchy. Warden Thelwater is forced back to the entrance, seeking help to crush the riot before it reaches the capital. Beneath Stormwind's canals, the Stockade has become a war zone. When the regular army marched to distant fronts, the understaffed prison relied on inexperienced youths, including fourteen-year-old recruits, to keep order. They failed. The inmates launched a bloody, coordinated rebellion, tore their wool blankets into gang scarves, and cut down the remaining guards. Warden Thelwater barely holds the entrance, while convicts control the cells, hallways, and holding blocks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stockade-31e2946e686c66ab0c0f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.1028571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stonetalon"] = {
  hash = "4a5f9d",
  fullHash = "4fc1f4",
  text = "Stonetalon Mountains. In Stonetalon Mountains, sacred heights now face smoke, deforestation, and elemental ruin. Night elves and tauren revere the range, while border hostilities simmer below. Stonetalon Mountains rise from central Kalimdor beneath the Ill Winds. Moisture from the western seas brings sudden downpours and slick sandstone trails, while fierce gusts scour the lower valleys, blinding travelers with grit and hurling the careless from cliffs. Higher ridges carry evergreen forests around Stonetalon Peak. Night elves tend ancestral barrows and glades there, and watch sacred wyvern and hippogryph roosts along Mirkfallon Lake. The Venture Company has broken the range's quiet with logging camps and strip mines in Windshear Crag. The goblins, aided by the deranged leper gnome Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle, have felled old-growth timber, filled the skies with furnace smoke, and fouled mountain runoff with industrial sludge. Local shamans from Orgrimmar answered the mountain spirits' cries. Small Alliance camps nearby also resist the defilers and try to protect the highlands that remain. South lies the Charred Vale, once a blossoming wilderness and now an ash-covered wasteland filled with rogue fire elementals, black dragonspawn, and harpies. During the Third War, the Horde and Alliance forged an uneasy truce in these peaks under the mysterious Oracle's guidance. Relations have since frayed. Renegade Grimtotem tauren hold seized encampments, while Darkspear trolls in Malaka'jin clash with Alliance forces pressing out from the Barrens and Ashenvale.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stonetalon-57ac760b752d9369c3a3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.2865306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stormwind"] = {
  hash = "2527da",
  fullHash = "e94558",
  text = "Stormwind City. In Stormwind City, the crown rests on a young prince while King Varian is missing. Rebuilt after the First War, the city is now the heart of the Grand Alliance, though debt and political turmoil trouble its white stone districts. Stormwind was founded over a thousand years ago by descendants of the Arathi. For generations, it was a flourishing, self-sufficient realm. The Orcish Horde crossed the Dark Portal and laid waste to the southern continent, razing the capital during the First War. Survivors followed Sir Anduin Lothar north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. After the Horde fell in the Second War, King Terenas Menethil II and the Alliance paid to rebuild the city. The Stonemasons Guild raised New Stormwind from polished white stone, with canals running through its boroughs. Stormwind’s grand appearance hides a kingdom in debt and a court divided. The House of Nobles and the Stonemasons Guild fought over wages, and the dispute left the kingdom paralyzed by debt. The guild’s workers rioted and were driven from the city they had built. King Varian Wrynn has vanished on his way to a diplomatic summit. To keep panic at bay, the crown went to his ten-year-old son, Prince Anduin Wrynn. Highlord Bolvar Fordragon now rules as Regent and military commander, with royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor at his side. With Lordaeron overrun by the Scourge, Stormwind has become humanity’s greatest haven and the Alliance’s center. High Tinker Mekkatorque’s underground Deeprun Tram links the city directly to Ironforge, the dwarven kingdom. Across the Valley of Heroes, towering statues honor the Sons of Lothar, who vanished beyond the Dark Portal. The Cathedral of Light draws the faithful, while the Wizard’s Sanctum houses arcane study. Trade, culture, and defense across the southern seas all meet in Stormwind.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stormwind-23c76af6585d073cf0c2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 108.120816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stranglethorn"] = {
  hash = "c999e7",
  fullHash = "754c04",
  text = "Stranglethorn Vale. Stranglethorn Vale is an untamed, dense jungle dominating the southern tip of the Eastern Kingdoms, south of Duskwood. Once the seat of the great Gurubashi Empire, the rainforest is now strewn with crumbling troll ruins, savage beasts, and competing encampments of goblins, pirates, and soldiers. Control of the region is hotly contested by local troll tribes, the Venture Company, and rival factions carving out footholds in the heat. For millennia, the vale was governed by the mighty Gurubashi Empire from their capital city, Zul'Gurub. That dominion shattered when radical priests summoned Hakkar the Soulflayer, plunging the region into a catastrophic civil war. Though the Zandalari stepped in to crush the blood god's uprising, the empire splintered beyond repair. Today, the jungle is overrun by warring successor factions—most prominently the Bloodscalp in the northwest and the Skullsplitter in the east—both zealously slaughtering outsiders to reclaim their ancestral home. Where ancient ruins fall silent, commercial greed fills the void. At the southern tip lies Booty Bay, a bustling haven carved out by the Steamwheedle Cartel and run by the Blackwater Raiders under Baron Revilgaz. The port serves as the prime hub of southern commerce and seafaring transport, though it is perpetually besieged by the rival Bloodsail Buccaneers anchored along the coast. Further inland, the Venture Company aggressively strips the jungle of timber and mines the hills for rare singing crystals. Stranglethorn Vale serves as one of the most volatile borderlands in the world. The Horde commands Grom'gol Base Camp on the western coast, tethered by goblin zeppelins to Durotar and Tirisfal Glades, while Alliance loyalists operate out of the Rebel Camp to track down the rogue Colonel Kurzen. Amid the dense foliage, the legendary hunter Hemet Nesingwary leads daring big-game expeditions, drawing wanderers into perilous encounters with prowling panthers, enormous apes, and enemy soldiers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stranglethorn-bd85358460dee8b7088e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 115.226122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:swampofsorrows"] = {
  hash = "283d7e",
  fullHash = "89c06e",
  text = "Swamp of Sorrows. The Swamp of Sorrows is a murky, brackish wetland tucked between Deadwind Pass and the arid Blasted Lands. Once the northern expanse of the ancient Black Morass, its salt-crusted bogs are home to dangerous wildlife, green dragonspawn, and the ruined Temple of Atal'Hakkar sunken beneath the Pool of Tears. While the Horde maintains the fortified garrison of Stonard, the Alliance has no safe haven within the mire, leaving the wilderness largely untamed. Long before the coming of the Orcish Horde, these marshes formed the northern half of an enormous wetlands known as the Black Morass. Following the shattering of the Gurubashi Empire, the fanatical Atal'ai trolls fled into the mire to erect the Temple of Atal'Hakkar, intending to summon their bloodthirsty loa, Hakkar the Soulflayer. Sensing the catastrophe, the Aspect Ysera unleashed her powers, drowning the cursed pyramid beneath the waters of the Pool of Tears. Green dragonflight guardians led by Eranikus were stationed to vigilantly watch over the drowned structure. When the Dark Portal's cataclysm later scorched the southern morass into the Blasted Lands, this northern fen earned its current sorrowful name. Stonard serves as the primary civilized foothold in the swamp. Originally established during the First War alongside the now-ruined settlements of Rockard and Kyross, the outpost was rebuilt under Thrall to serve as the Horde's southernmost staging ground in the Eastern Kingdoms. Beyond Stonard's palisades, strange wanderers roam the fen. A group of draenei stragglers stranded on Azeroth succumbed to homesickness and madness, degenerating into feral Lost Ones. However, a small enclave of lucid Broken exiles persists at the Harborage, wary but willing to trade peacefully with travelers. Surviving the swamp requires constant vigilance against its predatory wildlife. Deep bogs hide lunging crocolisks, venomous spiders, prowling swamp jaguars, and wandering bog beasts encrusted with marsh debris. Coastal caverns and the Misty Reed Strand crawl with vicious murlocs, while the salt-laden tide creeps continually inland, preserving rotten logs in thick briny crusts. Farther east lies Sorrowmurk, where aggressive elite dragonspawn fiercely guard the approaches to the Sunken Temple, attacking anyone who strays near their vigil.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_swampofsorrows-3c7732e14c4dc975d9fe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 137.16898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:tanaris"] = {
  hash = "f09fd8",
  fullHash = "3f5846",
  text = "Tanaris. Tanaris is a vast, sweltering desert spanning the southern reaches of Kalimdor, defined by rolling sand dunes and blistering heat. At its heart lies Gadgetzan, a neutral trading outpost operated by the goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel. Beyond the bustling settlements, the dunes conceal hostile sand trolls, dangerous silithid hives, and the hidden domain of the bronze dragonflight. Tanaris is a barren, sun-scorched wilderness. Within the walled city of Zul'Farrak, the hardened Sandfury trolls guard their isolated domain. Today, the desert floor is ruled by hyenas, basilisks, and scorpids, while subterranean tunnels snake beneath the baking earth. Because Tanaris is as close to their island homeland of Kezan as the mainland allows, the Steamwheedle Cartel has established a thriving enterprise here. From the walled oasis of Gadgetzan to the coastal docks of Steamwheedle Port, the goblins maintain strict neutrality, welcoming coin from both the Alliance and the Horde. Their peace is maintained by heavy bruiser muscle, though rogue threats like the Southsea Freebooters constantly harass shipping and dig sites along the coastline. The sands hide powers far older than mortal empires. In the eastern reaches lie the Caverns of Time, the ancestral domain of Nozdormu and his bronze dragonflight, whose drakes can occasionally be seen riding the desert thermals. To the south, massive stone gates mark the titan complex of Uldum. Yet a darker danger stirs from subterranean nests like the Gaping Chasm and the Noxious Lair, where creeping silithid swarms have begun burrowing outward across the sands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_tanaris-3b3370fe0bb79ab1995e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 96.8097959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:teldrassil"] = {
  hash = "9db73b",
  fullHash = "f8ef4d",
  text = "Teldrassil. Teldrassil rises from the sea off Kalimdor's northwest coast, its crown sheltering Darnassus. Druids raised the World Tree after the Third War, but its growth is troubled. You stand beneath Teldrassil's vast limbs above the Veiled Sea. Hills, rivers fed by morning dew, misty lakes, and ancient glades spread across its boughs. Darnassus, the kaldorei capital, lies at the western edge. At the base of the trunk, Rut'theran Village is the island's harbor, linked to the crown by a mystical gateway. After Nordrassil was sacrificed during the Third War, Shan'do Malfurion Stormrage vanished into the Emerald Dream. Archdruid Fandral Staghelm persuaded the Circle of Ancients to grow a replacement, hoping to restore the kaldorei's lost immortality. Nozdormu and the Dragon Aspects judged the effort hubristic and withheld their blessings. Without their warding, Teldrassil has grown with unnatural flaws.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_teldrassil-98e178f2df9f35a56eb2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.524898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:thousandneedles"] = {
  hash = "595df6",
  fullHash = "3fb560",
  text = "Thousand Needles. Thousand Needles is a sprawling canyon of sheer sandstone pillars and sunbaked salt pans in southern Kalimdor. Tauren tribes cling to settlements atop towering mesas to escape aggressive centaur packs, while gnomish and goblin tinkerers run high-speed rocket cars across the dried-up seabed of the Shimmering Flats. Before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, this territory was a lush river canyon fed by inland waterways flowing out toward the sea. The cataclysm tore the continent apart, thrusting the riverbed above the water table and altering the river's path until only dry stone and sandstone spires remained. Today, dozens of slender cylindrical columns rise from the canyon floor like a petrified forest. Tauren settled on the summits, stringing precarious rope bridges between peaks to establish villages out of reach of the hostile centaur marauders patrolling the shadow-draped canyon floor below. Atop the needle-like spires, life is far from peaceful. At Freewind Post, tauren loyal to the Horde contend not only with predatory wind serpents and wyverns from Highperch, but also with their treacherous kin: the militant Grimtotem clan entrenched at Darkcloud Pinnacle. Cliffwatcher Longhorn oversees the defense of the plateau, seeking bold fighters to strike at Grimtotem agitators and secure the fragile bridges linking the peaks. To the north, the enormous mechanical winches of the Great Lift carry travelers down from the southern Barrens, though crossing the canyon floor remains a deadly gambit. To the southeast, the canyon walls peel back into the Shimmering Flats, an enormous, desiccated salt pan sitting below sea level. In this harsh, blinding white expanse, gnomes and goblins maintain an uneasy neutrality around the Mirage Raceway. Rival engineers Pozzik and Fizzle Brassbolts race experimental rocket cars across the hard salt to settle which race possesses technological superiority. The pit stop attracts scoundrels, swindlers like Kravel Koalbeard, and hunters hunting the hardy scorpions, turtles, and basilisks that roam the searing basin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_thousandneedles-d72013dd76b5fe12415c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 121.756735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:thunderbluff"] = {
  hash = "d0a075",
  fullHash = "f64146",
  text = "Thunder Bluff. Across the plains of Mulgore, Thunder Bluff shelters the tauren above the grasslands. High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof founded the city after uniting the nomadic tribes with the New Horde. Rope bridges and wooden lifts link its mesas, while its heights offer safety from centaur marauders and shelter travelers crossing Kalimdor. For generations, the tauren wandered across Kalimdor, struggling to survive centaur attacks. During the Third War, Cairne Bloodhoof joined Warchief Thrall, and together the orcs and tauren drove back the centaur and secured Mulgore’s pastures. Cairne led the allied tribes to these natural stone heights to guard their future. Thunder Bluff became their first unified, permanent home in centuries. Thunder Bluff stretches across high mesas, joined by rope bridges. The central bluffs hold the markets, bank, inn, and Wind Rider roost. Three more mesas rise beyond them. Hunter Rise serves scouts and trackers. Elder Rise is home to lorekeepers and the Cenarion Circle, led by Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem. Spirit Rise is devoted to spiritual communion, with shelter for shamans and priests and the underground Pools of Vision.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_thunderbluff-c58f872cb9a8ef7812a7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.5191837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:tirisfal"] = {
  hash = "2f6067",
  fullHash = "c582bb",
  text = "Tirisfal Glades. In northern Lordaeron, Tirisfal Glades is home to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and her Forsaken. Under blighted skies, fallen manors and dormant crypts surround their vast underground capital, the Undercity. Mindless Scourge and zealous Scarlet crusaders hem them in, and the free-willed dead fight for their survival. Tirisfal was once the fertile cradle of humanity, before the Lich King's plague withered them. The sun rarely breaks through the murky gloom over dying woods, neglected pumpkin patches and cold coastal hills. Old farmsteads like the Agamand Mills and Balnir Farmstead still stand, but most of their masters lie in mass graves or walk as restless dead. Yet a dark resilience holds on in towns like Brill and in the cloistered sanctuary of Deathknell, where freshly awakened Forsaken learn to endure their second life. Lady Sylvanas rules the region from beneath the Ruins of Lordaeron, yet the Forsaken are beset on all sides. Scourge thralls still infest hollows, fields and crumbling crypts, wholly bound to the frozen throne of Northrend. To the east, the fanatical Scarlet Crusade has made northern Tirisfal its main bastion, centered on the Scarlet Monastery. Sworn to destroy every undead creature, its outposts and patrols wage constant guerrilla war on Forsaken scouts and civilian travelers. The story of the Glades is older than any human crown. High elven exiles once tried to settle these woods, until shadowy energies deep beneath the earth began driving them mad and they moved on north. Centuries later, the secret mortal cabal called the Council of Tirisfal chose these shadowed groves to empower its solitary Guardians, who shielded Azeroth from demons.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_tirisfal-e055e698b527e93ac724/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.720816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:undercity"] = {
  hash = "57ce7b",
  fullHash = "869019",
  text = "Undercity. Beneath the shattered ruins of Lordaeron's Capital City, the Undercity is the Forsaken's dark stronghold. Lady Sylvanas Windrunner took it from the Scourge and remade it into an impenetrable sanctuary. Today it is the seat of Forsaken power and the Horde's vital bastion in the northern Eastern Kingdoms. Long before the plague, the catacombs, dungeons and sewers beneath the palace were already called the Undercity. They held the tombs of nobles, the poorest people of the realm and the kingdom's deepest prisons, where the former Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer was held before his escape. In the Third War, Arthas Menethil murdered King Terenas and laid waste to the capital. The prince then set his undead servants to dig out and expand the passages into a fortified seat of Scourge rule. The Scourge never saw their dark capital finished. When the Lich King's power waned after the battle of Mount Hyjal, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner broke free of the hive mind and took thousands of undead with her. She lured Arthas into an ambush and drove him back to Northrend. After a vicious three-way civil war against Scourge remnants, dreadlords and the human resistance, she claimed the half-finished catacombs. She named her people the Forsaken and oversaw years of labor to complete the sprawling city. The city spreads in concentric rings divided into quarters. At its heart is the Trade Quarter, linked by great lifts to the palace ruins above. Around it lie the War Quarter, the Rogues' Quarter, the Magic Quarter and the toxic Apothecarium, where the Royal Apothecary Society brews virulent plagues beside canals of green slime. Deeper still, in the Royal Quarter, the Banshee Queen holds court with her dreadlord commander, Varimathras, guarded by legions of towering abominations.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_undercity-7ff09142090902565606/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.568163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ungoro"] = {
  hash = "aead63",
  fullHash = "10ee3a",
  text = "Un'Goro Crater. Un'Goro Crater is a sunken, primordial jungle tucked between the arid wastes of Tanaris and Silithus. Dominated by roaming dinosaurs, living elementals, and a central volcano, this lush caldera is filled with ancient Titan relics and power crystals whose origins remain a profound mystery. Sandwiched between desert expanses, Un'Goro Crater defies the geography of southern Kalimdor. Ancient night elf records detailing the War of the Shifting Sands note that the insectoid Qiraji were turned back at the crater's edges, unable to penetrate what their tongue named the 'God Lands.' Scholars theorize the caldera served as a testing ground or ancient dwelling for the Titans during the shaping of Kalimdor. Stone pylons rise along the ridges, humming with energy, while crystal formations of blue, red, green, and yellow sprout through the primeval earth. Un'Goro contains no cities, towns, or native humanoid societies. Instead, life thrives in an untamed, primeval state. Apex devilsaurs stalk the flats of Terror Run, predatory bloodpetals lash out in the marshlands, and oozes creep across the tar pits of Lakkari. At the heart of the crater looms Fire Plume Ridge, an active volcanic peak teeming with hostile fire elementals led by the entity Blazerunner. For explorers and skinners, the basin offers unmatched natural wealth—including the coveted hides of devilsaurs—provided one survives the food chain. Civilized presence in the crater is limited almost entirely to Marshal's Refuge, an elevated camp tucked into the northern cliffs. Led by Williden Marshal, an expedition of scientists, naturalists, and fortune seekers attempted to catalogue the basin's treasures only to be battered by the monstrous wildlife and driven to high ground. Today, the refuge serves as an embattled staging ground for intrepid travelers studying the crater's mysterious power crystals, mapping volcanic activity, and keeping a watchful eye on encroaching silithid swarms breaching the southern ridges.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ungoro-ce98eebfd85ef4b8abf2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 118.360816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:wailing"] = {
  hash = "6c8555",
  fullHash = "770c46",
  text = "Wailing Caverns. Beneath the Barrens' Lushwater Oasis, the Wailing Caverns are named for the eerie whistle of trapped steam. Their maze of underground springs and fissures was meant to revive Kalimdor's sun-bleached savanna, a noble druidic effort. Now it is a warped, poisonous underworld, teeming with twisted wildlife and fallen disciples in the grip of a deep, unnatural nightmare. The night elf druid Naralex found this vast water system and devised an ambitious plan. Through a conduit into the Emerald Dream, he would draw pure primal life through the springs and turn the parched Barrens back into a thriving paradise. Deep in the caverns, anchored by his loyal acolytes, Naralex slipped into communion with the Dream. But his sleeping mind touched the Emerald Nightmare instead. The corruption seized him at once, and his conduit became a valve pouring venom and madness into the underground waters. As the Nightmare's dark energy radiated from Naralex's helpless form, his students fell to the madness. They forsook the tranquil wisdom of the Cenarion Circle and became the fanatical Druids of the Fang. Their leaders, Lady Anacondra, Lord Cobrahn, Lord Pythas and Lord Serpentis, take reptilian forms and embrace the venomous mutation of the caverns instead of fighting it. They shield their sleeping master from any outside interference, convinced this twisted growth is nature's true evolution.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_wailing-c5f5121292cc7dc7ed90/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.8269388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:westernplaguelands"] = {
  hash = "4bb9e5",
  fullHash = "dc8171",
  text = "Western Plaguelands. Once the fertile agricultural breadbasket of northern Lordaeron, the Western Plaguelands now choke beneath a toxic mist and legions of undead. While Kel'Thuzad's cauldrons ruined the region's farms and towns, the soil still clings tenaciously to life. The Argent Dawn, the Alliance, the Forsaken, and the Scarlet Crusade all wage desperate campaigns across its blighted fields. Before the Third War, this land was a verdant corridor of farms stretching east from Tirisfal Glades toward Darrowmere Lake. Hearthglen governed the northern administration from Mardenholde Keep, while the sprawling town of Andorhal orchestrated the storage and export of grain across the kingdom. That crucial agricultural role made the region Kel'Thuzad's primary target. By introducing the Plague of Undeath directly into the grain shipments at Andorhal, the Cult of the Damned turned Lordaeron's sustenance into its doom. To permanently poison the soil, the Scourge raised four massive plague cauldrons across the largest agricultural estates: Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt, and Gahrron's Withering. Today, monstrous Cauldron Lords maintain these noxious vats, pumping venomous fog into the atmosphere. The surrounding trees twist with swollen, tumorous growths, and blighted wildlife rampages through the brush. Beneath Caer Darrow on Darrowmere Lake, Darkmaster Gandling trains necromancers within Scholomance, compounding the undead threat. Despite the omnipresence of the Scourge, several factions maintain embattled footholds. The Scarlet Crusade controls the fortress of Hearthglen in the north, purging anything they deem tainted with rabid zeal. To the south, Alliance forces stage from Chillwind Camp, while the Forsaken defend their borders at the Bulwark. Mediating and directing the fight against the undead cauldrons is the neutral Argent Dawn, which aids champions of both factions and guards Uther's Tomb—the last truly sanctified ground in the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_westernplaguelands-2a37b457e192aaf451ff/1-0000.mp3", duration = 119.14449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:westfall"] = {
  hash = "343283",
  fullHash = "8dd71d",
  text = "Westfall. Westfall's farms lie exposed, and the Defias Brotherhood holds much of the countryside. Once Stormwind's breadbasket, the territory now struggles without crown support or military garrisons. Westfall once fed the Kingdom of Stormwind with its pastures and wheat fields. After the Second War, the kingdom's finances ran thin and its leaders neglected the territory. Stormwind sent resources elsewhere and let its garrisons go, leaving the land fallow. Now barren pastures, harsh coastal winds, rogue harvest golems, and Riverpaw gnolls make survival hard for the few homesteaders who remain. The Defias Brotherhood's arrival hastened Westfall's collapse. The bandits wear red masks and follow a leader whose name no one in the countryside knows. They made Westfall their stronghold, burning farmers' fields, salting the soil, and taking abandoned mines. From Moonbrook, a ghost town, the Brotherhood rules most of the countryside in all but name. The House of Nobles has ignored Westfall's plight, so local people have taken up their own defense. At Sentinel Hill, Gryan Stoutmantle formed the People's Militia, a volunteer force holding the line against gnolls and bandits. Stormwind sends no formal soldiers or supplies. The Militia depends on wandering adventurers to push back the Defias and protect the farms that remain.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_westfall-464129db19a80ec35914/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.8302041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:wetlands"] = {
  hash = "5a7384",
  fullHash = "6b127e",
  text = "Wetlands. Fog and fighting shape life in the Wetlands, between Khaz Modan and Baradin Bay. Dwarven wars and orc invasions scarred the marsh, a rugged buffer between Ironforge and Lordaeron. Menethil Harbor keeps the Alliance connected to distant shores, amid Dragonmaw orcs, murlocs, and gnolls. Below the Stonewrought Dam, cold marshland lies under gray skies and heavy fog. Once home to thriving dwarven settlements, the Wetlands now hold sunken stone ruins, sodden bogs, and treacherous mires. You can enter by the narrow pass at Dun Algaz, coming north from Loch Modan, or cross the arches of the Thandol Span from the Arathi Highlands. Grim Batol looms over the eastern swamp, its halls once held by the Wildhammer dwarves. Defiled during the War of the Three Hammers, the stronghold became a redoubt for the Dragonmaw clan during the Second War. When the red dragonflight broke free of orcish captivity, its fiery vengeance shattered the orcish forces and laid waste to the slopes. Red wyrmkin guard the Dragonmaw Gates, while feral Dragonmaw survivors live in scattered hill camps and harass travelers across the swamp. Menethil Harbor occupies the rim of Baradin Bay, carrying Alliance trade and supplies through Khaz Modan. The port honors King Terenas Menethil II and links the Eastern Kingdoms with Kalimdor. Ships sail to Theramore Isle and, by way of Southshore, to Auberdine. Dwarven-built defenses protect the harbor, but coastal murlocs, swamp creatures, and raiders threaten vessels in the rocky channels. War reaches the Wetlands from several directions. In the north, Dark Iron dwarves led by the warlock Balgaras the Foul sabotaged the Thandol Span and seized Dun Modr, cutting off overland supply trains. Mosshide gnolls roam the central fens and threaten even Rethiel the Greenwarden, an ancient protector. Rogue orc warbands, Dark Iron infiltrators, and cursed shipwrecks keep the swamp dangerous, and its defenders must stay alert.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_wetlands-a0363526e51a04b32800/1-0000.mp3", duration = 119.719184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:winterspring"] = {
  hash = "356040",
  fullHash = "c8197b",
  text = "Winterspring. Winterspring is a vast, snow-covered valley nestled high along the northeastern slopes of Mount Hyjal, directly above the enchanted forests of Moonglade and Felwood. Known for its bitter cold, ancient Highborne ruins, and wandering apex predators, the region is anchored by the neutral goblin outpost of Everlook. It remains a land of fierce natural perils, sheltering remnants of the blue dragonflight while confronting demonic corruption along its southern heights. Perched thousands of feet above the surrounding wilderness, Winterspring rests beneath crisp mountain air and relentless snowstorms. Enormous exposed roots of the World Tree, Nordrassil, cut through the snowdrifts like towering wooden arches, warped and twisted during the climactic struggles of the Third War. While much of northern Kalimdor is dominated by dense woodlands and warm climates, Winterspring is a quiet land of medium alpine forests, rocky bluffs, and frozen lakes, home to formidable frostsabers, chimaeras, yetis, and thick-furred winter bears. Reaching this high plateau requires traversing the ancient carved passages of Timbermaw Hold, guarded by uncorrupted furbolgs who closely scrutinize any travelers passing from Felwood or Moonglade. At the valley's center sits Everlook, a thriving trading town operated by the Steamwheedle Cartel. Goblins welcome both Alliance and Horde adventurers with open registers, acting as an indispensable haven of engineering, trade, and warm fires against the biting mountain frost. Centuries before modern settlements arose, ancient elves built the lakeside city of Kel'Theril. After reckless Highborne sorcerers shattered the cursed Crystal of Zin-Malor, madness claimed them, leaving restless phantoms to wander the ruined pillars and frozen waters to this day. Further south rise the cavernous heights of Mazthoril, the traditional Kalimdor stronghold of the blue dragonflight. Here, the brood of the Spell-Weaver Malygos, overseen by the matron protector Haleh, watches over arcane currents and contests local threats. The serenity of Winterspring is sharply broken along its southernmost border at Darkwhisper Gorge. There, elite demonic forces, remnant legions from the Third War, crawl along jagged canyons blocking the high approaches toward the summit of Mount Hyjal. Meanwhile, the native Winterfall furbolgs have succumbed to foul corruptions, prompting independent researchers like Donova Snowden to investigate the encroaching taint before the entire valley's wildlife is consumed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_winterspring-a2b1d4277cc80c64adbf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 146.729796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:zephras"] = {
  hash = "665598",
  fullHash = "72d458",
  text = "Zephras Isle. Zephras Isle is a flying island suspended within Skywall, the Elemental Plane of air. It serves as the ancestral starting home of the Skyborne, whose society now faces an existential crisis as the island's sustaining magic steadily erodes. With contact severed from neighboring sky-realms, its inhabitants struggle against elemental collapse and arriving refugees. Zephras Isle is an isolated landmass adrift in the endless winds of Skywall. For generations, the Skyborne lived here alongside native air elementals who helped preserve the magical currents holding the isle aloft. However, several centuries ago, those elemental stewards vanished entirely. Deprived of their presence and guardianship, the latent enchantments upholding the land have slowly begun to fail, causing Zephras to slowly fracture and destabilize. Zephras was not the only sanctuary inhabited by the Skyborne within the Elemental Plane. Sister islands known as Anvilas and Siroccas also drifted across Skywall, but communications between them ceased after the elementals vanished. In recent times, refugees from these crumbling neighboring landmasses have managed to reach Zephras seeking safety, bearing grim tidings that the other islands are disintegrating outright beneath their feet. With their own landmass in slow decay, the Skyborne of Zephras have begun looking beyond the boundaries of Skywall toward Azeroth. From the skydocks of Valanaar, airships now venture forth to establish rare lifelines to the mortal realm. These voyages connect the airy domain directly to points on Azeroth, including the outskirts of Dalaran in the Alterac Mountains and the Skywatcher Plateau in Mulgore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_zephras-8d091150815fd79ac5ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.317551, start = 0},
  },
}
